window.GAKS_SEARCH = [{"t":"Error, Defect, and Failure Causal Chain","i":"CONCEPT-istqb-v1-001-01-error-defect-failure","h":"doc/catalogs/istqb/concepts/CONCEPT-istqb-v1-001-01-error-defect-failure.html","s":"accepted","x":"Error, Defect, and Failure Causal Chain Definition The causal chain of software flaws distinguishes between human actions, flaws in artifacts, and runtime discrepancies as defined in CTFL v4.0 §1.2.3: 1. Error (Mistake): A human action that produces an incorrect result (e.g., a software developer misinterpreting a requirement or typing a wrong comparison operator). 2. Defect (Fault / Bug): An imperfection or deficiency in a work product where it does not meet its requirements or specifications (e.g., an off-by-one boundary in code or an omitted case in a design document). 3. Failure: An event in which a component or system does not perform a required function within specified limits during runtime execution (e.g., an unhandled exception thrown or an incorrect calculation displayed to a user). A human makes an error, introducing a defect into the codebase. When the affected code path is executed with triggering inputs, that defect causes a runtime failure. Environmental conditions (such as hardware faults or network timeouts) can also cause failures without a code defect. text +----------------+ introduces +-----------------+ triggers +-----------------+ Human Error --------------------- Defect (Fault) ------------------- Runtime Failure (Mental lapse) (Static artifact) (Dynamic event) +----------------+ +-----------------+ +-----------------+ e.g. Logic error e.g. if (x 10) e.g. Crash at x=10 Why it exists Understanding the error-defect-failure chain is foundational for quality engineering: 1. Defects Can Remain Dormant: A defect in static code only produces a runtime failure when execution reaches that path under specific input conditions. Automated testing systematically forces execution through dormant paths to trigger failures before production. 2. Failures Are Symptoms, Not Causes: Automated test suites report failures (assertion errors, crashes). Effective debugging requires tracing backwards from the dynamic failure to the static defect, and root-cause analysis investigates the human or procedural error that introduced it. 3. Guiding Static vs. Dynamic Testing: Static analysis and code reviews detect defects directly in work products without execution, preventing defects from ever manifesting as runtime failures. Dynamic automated tests detect failures during execution, proving whether defect fixes were successful."},{"t":"Verification vs. Validation","i":"CONCEPT-istqb-v1-002-01-verification-vs-validation","h":"doc/catalogs/istqb/concepts/CONCEPT-istqb-v1-002-01-verification-vs-validation.html","s":"accepted","x":"Verification vs. Validation Definition Verification and validation represent two complementary evaluation processes defined in CTFL v4.0 §1.1.1: Verification (\"Building the system right\"): The confirmation through objective evidence that specified requirements have been fulfilled. It evaluates whether a work product conforms to its inputs, design specifications, standards, and regulatory rules. Validation (\"Building the right system\"): The confirmation through objective evidence that the requirements for a specific intended use or application have been fulfilled. It evaluates whether the system satisfies genuine operational needs, business goals, and user expectations in its operational environment. python Verification: Asserting system conforms strictly to technical specification def testverifydiscountcalculation(): Spec: \"When total 100, apply 10% discount\" cart = ShoppingCart(total=150) cart.applyrules() assert cart.discount == 15.0 Validation: Evaluating fitness for actual operational use def testvalidatecheckoutworkflowsatisfiesuserneed(usersession): User need: \"A shopper must be able to complete checkout with one click\" usersession.addsaveditem(\"widget-1\") usersession.oneclickbuy() assert usersession.orderstatus == \"CONFIRMED\" assert usersession.totalsteps == 1 Why it exists Differentiating verification from validation prevents the failure mode of delivering a technically flawless system that solves the wrong problem: 1. Specification Flaws: A system can pass 100% of automated unit and integration verification tests while remaining entirely useless if the underlying specification was flawed or obsolete. 2. Layered Test Strategy: Automated unit and component tests primarily verify structural code correctness against detailed design. Higher-level acceptance tests, user journeys, and exploratory testing validate whether workflows deliver the intended business value. 3. Traceability: Verification traces code upwards to written requirements; validation traces systems outward to user workflows, customer feedback, and business operational realities."},{"t":"The Seven Testing Principles","i":"CONCEPT-istqb-v1-003-01-seven-testing-principles","h":"doc/catalogs/istqb/concepts/CONCEPT-istqb-v1-003-01-seven-testing-principles.html","s":"accepted","x":"The Seven Testing Principles Definition The seven testing principles defined in CTFL v4.0 §1.3 provide the universal rules governing all software testing activities: 1. Testing shows the presence of defects, not their absence: Testing proves that defects exist, but passing all tests can never prove that no defects remain in the software under test. 2. Exhaustive testing is impossible: Testing all possible combinations of inputs, preconditions, and execution paths is mathematically intractable for non-trivial software. Testing must use risk analysis, test techniques, and priorities to focus effort. 3. Early testing saves time and money (Shift-Left): Defects discovered early in the lifecycle (requirements, architecture, code reviews) cost orders of magnitude less to fix than defects discovered in production or late integration phases. 4. Defects cluster together (Pareto Principle): A small proportion of system components, modules, or features typically contains the majority of discovered defects and operational failures. 5. Beware of the pesticide paradox: Running the exact same automated tests repeatedly without modification causes them to lose defect-finding efficacy. Tests must be reviewed and evolved with new test cases and techniques to uncover new defects. 6. Testing is context-dependent: Testing is performed differently across different domains; testing a safety-critical medical device requires different techniques and rigor than testing an e-commerce website. 7. Absence-of-defects fallacy: Fixing all discovered defects and building a 100% bug-free system does not guarantee success if the software fails to meet user expectations or business goals. text The Seven Principles at a Glance: 1. Presence != Absence -- Passing tests != defect-free software 2. No Exhaustive Testing -- Sample domains with EP, BVA, and risk analysis 3. Shift-Left Testing -- Lint, review, and test at the earliest phase 4. Defect Clustering -- Target testing where bugs historically cluster 5. Pesticide Paradox -- Vary test paths, seed mutations, evolve suites 6. Context Dependent -- Fit strategy to safety, scale, and domain risk 7. Absence Fallacy -- Verifying bug fixes != delivering user value Why it exists The seven principles form the philosophical foundation for all automated test design: 1. Disciplined Automation Strategy: Because exhaustive testing is impossible, test automation frameworks rely on rigorous input partitioning rather than brute-force loops. 2. Managing Test Decay: The pesticide paradox warns against static automated regression suites that never exercise novel paths; it justifies model-based testing, fuzzing, and continuous test refactoring. 3. Combating False Confidence: The first principle reminds engineering teams that green CI pipelines indicate the absence of known tested regressions, not absolute defect-free reliability."},{"t":"Test Levels","i":"CONCEPT-istqb-v1-004-01-test-levels","h":"doc/catalogs/istqb/concepts/CONCEPT-istqb-v1-004-01-test-levels.html","s":"accepted","x":"Test Levels Definition Test levels are structured groups of test activities that are organized and managed together, defined in CTFL v4.0 §2.2.1. Each level targets a specific test object, utilizes specific test bases, detects characteristic defect types, and runs in an appropriate test environment: 1. Component Testing (Unit Testing): Focuses on components (functions, classes, modules) in isolation. Test doubles (mocks, stubs, fakes) eliminate external dependencies. Goal: verify internal algorithmic logic, boundary conditions, and state transitions. 2. Component Integration Testing: Focuses on interactions and communication interfaces between internal components, packages, or services. Goal: identify protocol mismatches, serialization errors, and parameter ordering defects. 3. System Testing: Focuses on the overall behavior and capabilities of the complete integrated product or application. Goal: verify end-to-end business functional workflows and non-functional requirements (performance, security). 4. System Integration Testing: Focuses on interactions between the system under test and external third-party systems, external APIs, microservice boundaries, hardware platforms, or database tiers. 5. Acceptance Testing: Focuses on determining whether the system satisfies acceptance criteria and is ready for operational deployment or user sign-off (e.g., User Acceptance Testing / UAT, Operational Acceptance Testing / OAT, regulatory compliance). text +-----------------------------------------------------------------+ Acceptance Testing User readiness, business fit +-----------------------------------------------------------------+ System Integration Testing External APIs, payment gateways +-----------------------------------------------------------------+ System Testing End-to-end product workflows +-----------------------------------------------------------------+ Component Integration Testing Internal package/service boundaries +-----------------------------------------------------------------+ Component (Unit) Testing Isolated classes, functions +-----------------------------------------------------------------+ Why it exists Organizing automated test suites into distinct levels prevents architectural antipatterns such as the \"ice-cream cone\" (where all tests run end-to-end through the UI): 1. Feedback Speed and Isolation: Component unit tests execute in milliseconds and pinpoint the exact line containing a defect. System tests take seconds or minutes and detect emergent integration defects across layers. 2. Failure Attribution: When a test fails at the component integration level, engineers immediately know the defect lies in the interface contract, avoiding deep system-level debugging. 3. Cost and Stability: Running tests at the lowest viable level dramatically reduces flakiness, execution time, and infrastructure costs across continuous delivery pipelines."},{"t":"Test Types: Functional, Non-Functional, Black-Box, and White-Box","i":"CONCEPT-istqb-v1-005-01-test-types","h":"doc/catalogs/istqb/concepts/CONCEPT-istqb-v1-005-01-test-types.html","s":"accepted","x":"Test Types: Functional, Non-Functional, Black-Box, and White-Box Definition Test types characterize testing activities according to their specific quality objectives and basis of test derivation, as defined in CTFL v4.0 §2.2.2: Functional Testing: Evaluates functions that the system performs (\"what\" the system does). Tests evaluate correctness, completeness, and appropriateness against functional specifications, user stories, and business requirements. Non-Functional Testing: Evaluates quality characteristics of system behavior (\"how well\" the system does it). Categories include performance efficiency (load, stress, latency), security (vulnerability, authorization), reliability (fault tolerance, recoverability), usability, accessibility, and maintainability. Black-Box Testing (Specification-Based): Test design techniques derived from analyzing documentation, requirements, or behavior without knowledge of internal program structure or code implementation. White-Box Testing (Structure-Based): Test design techniques derived from analyzing internal code structure, architecture, statements, control-flow branches, and data flows. text +----------------------------------------------+ Test Types +----------------------------------------------+ / [Objective] / [Derivation] v v +-----------------------------+ +-----------------------------+ Functional vs Non-Functional Black-Box vs White-Box +-----------------------------+ +-----------------------------+ Functional: \"What it does\" Black-Box: Spec-based, User journeys internal code hidden Business rules White-Box: Structure-based, Non-Functional: \"How well\" statement & branch paths Latency, load, security +-----------------------------+ +-----------------------------+ Why it exists Test types provide a multi-dimensional taxonomy that prevents teams from over-indexing on simple functional happy-paths: 1. Orthogonal Coverage: A system may be 100% functionally correct according to black-box requirements while failing catastrophically under concurrent load (non-functional failure) or containing dead, unreachable code branches (white-box structural gap). 2. Technique Selection: Identifying whether a test is black-box or white-box dictates the applicable design techniques (e.g., Equivalence Partitioning and Boundary Value Analysis for black-box vs. Statement/Branch Coverage for white-box). 3. Comprehensive Quality Assurances: Production readiness requires verifying functional correctness, architectural execution paths, and operational performance metrics across every release."},{"t":"Static Testing vs. Dynamic Testing","i":"CONCEPT-istqb-v1-006-01-static-vs-dynamic-testing","h":"doc/catalogs/istqb/concepts/CONCEPT-istqb-v1-006-01-static-vs-dynamic-testing.html","s":"accepted","x":"Static Testing vs. Dynamic Testing Definition Testing approaches are divided into static and dynamic methods based on whether code execution is required, as defined in CTFL v4.0 §3.1: Static Testing: Evaluates software work products (source code, schemas, architecture designs, user stories, requirements) without executing the software. Techniques include informal reviews, walkthroughs, inspections, linters, abstract syntax tree (AST) analyzers, and compiler type-checking. Static testing discovers defects directly in the artifacts themselves. Dynamic Testing: Evaluates software by executing the program on a machine with defined test inputs and observing its dynamic execution behavior, state transformations, outputs, and performance metrics. Dynamic testing discovers failures caused by executed defects. python Static Testing: Analyzed by a linter/type-checker WITHOUT running code def calculatetax(amount: float, rate: float) - float: Static analyzer flags unreachable code, unassigned variables, or type mismatches: if rate < 0: raise ValueError(\"Rate must be non-negative\") return amount rate unreachablevalue = 42 Static check flags: Unreachable code defect Dynamic Testing: Evaluates runtime behavior by executing the compiled/interpreted program def testcalculatetaxruntimeexecution(): result = calculatetax(100.0, 0.05) assert result == 5.0 Dynamic check: asserts expected output on executed inputs Why it exists Static and dynamic testing find different kinds of flaws and serve different economic roles: 1. Defect vs. Failure Detection: Static testing finds defects directly (e.g., syntax errors, uncalled functions, security vulnerabilities, missing requirements) without needing a test harness. Dynamic testing detects failures that emerge only during execution, such as concurrency race conditions, memory leaks, and distributed network latency. 2. Economic Shift-Left: Running static linters and semantic validators takes milliseconds and costs virtually nothing, catching defects before compilation or container deployment. 3. Complementary Coverage: Static verification cannot confirm runtime environmental behavior, database query performance, or third-party service integration; dynamic automated tests remain indispensable for runtime verification."},{"t":"Equivalence Partitioning","i":"CONCEPT-istqb-v1-007-01-equivalence-partitioning","h":"doc/catalogs/istqb/concepts/CONCEPT-istqb-v1-007-01-equivalence-partitioning.html","s":"accepted","x":"Equivalence Partitioning Definition Equivalence Partitioning (EP) is a black-box test design technique defined in CTFL v4.0 §4.2.1 in which input data and output values are divided into partitions (equivalence classes) such that all members of a given partition are expected to be processed in the same way by the system under test. Valid Partitions: Values that must be accepted and processed normally by the application according to specifications. Invalid Partitions: Values that must be rejected, ignored, or handled with explicit error handling routines. If a test case uncovers a defect using one representative value from a partition, other values from that same partition are assumed to uncover the same defect. Conversely, if one representative value passes, other values within that partition are assumed to pass. text Specification: Age must be an integer between 18 and 65 (inclusive) to register. Partitions: Invalid Partition 1: age Representative: 12 -- Expected: Rejection error Valid Partition: 18 Representative: 35 -- Expected: Registration success Invalid Partition 2: age 65 -- Representative: 72 -- Expected: Rejection error Invalid Partition 3: Non-numeric -- Representative: \"a\" -- Expected: Format error python import pytest @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"age,expectedstatus\", [ (12, \"ERRORTOOYOUNG\"), Invalid partition 1 (35, \"REGISTERED\"), Valid partition (72, \"ERRORTOOOLD\"), Invalid partition 2 ]) def testuserregistrationagepartitions(age, expectedstatus): result = registeraccount(name=\"Alex\", age=age) assert result.status == expectedstatus Why it exists Equivalence partitioning solves the fundamental impossibility of exhaustive testing: 1. Test Suite Minimization: Rather than testing thousands of arbitrary integers, EP systematically compresses an infinite input domain into a finite, provably representative set of test cases. 2. Eliminating Redundancy: Testing inputs 31, 32, 33, 34, and 35 provides zero additional defect-detection capability when all belong to the same linear valid partition; EP avoids wasted test execution cycles. 3. Structured Negative Testing: EP forces explicit identification of invalid partitions, ensuring automated test suites verify negative error paths and edge validations alongside happy paths."},{"t":"Boundary Value Analysis","i":"CONCEPT-istqb-v1-008-01-boundary-value-analysis","h":"doc/catalogs/istqb/concepts/CONCEPT-istqb-v1-008-01-boundary-value-analysis.html","s":"accepted","x":"Boundary Value Analysis Definition Boundary Value Analysis (BVA) is a black-box test design technique defined in CTFL v4.0 §4.2.2 that focuses on testing the boundaries of equivalence partitions. BVA is based on the empirical observation that software defects cluster heavily at the edges of input and output domains due to off-by-one errors (such as versus =). CTFL v4.0 recognizes two boundary value approaches: 2-Value BVA: For each boundary, test two values: 1. The boundary value itself. 2. Its closest neighbor on the other side of the boundary (the value just across the threshold). 3-Value BVA: For each boundary, test three values: 1. The boundary value itself. 2. Its closest neighbor inside the valid partition. 3. Its closest neighbor outside the valid partition. text Partition: Valid range from 1 to 100 inclusive. 2-Value BVA Test Set: Lower boundary: 0 (invalid), 1 (valid) Upper boundary: 100 (valid), 101 (invalid) Total tests: 4 3-Value BVA Test Set: Lower boundary: 0 (invalid), 1 (valid boundary), 2 (valid interior) Upper boundary: 99 (valid interior), 100 (valid boundary), 101 (invalid) Total tests: 6 python import pytest 2-Value BVA on range [1, 100] @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"quantity,isvalid\", [ (0, False), Just below min boundary (1, True), At min boundary (100, True), At max boundary (101, False), Just above max boundary ]) def testorderquantitybva(quantity, isvalid): result = validateorderquantity(quantity) assert result.isvalid == isvalid Why it exists Boundary value analysis maximizes defect detection density per executed test case: 1. Targeting Off-By-One Logic Bugs: Developers frequently confuse strict inequalities ( ) with inclusive inequalities ( =), or miscalculate array bounds and loop terminators. Testing middle values (e.g., 50 in a [1, 100] range) rarely catches these errors, whereas testing 0, 1, 100, and 101 exposes them immediately. 2. Deterministic Coverage Standards: Specifying 2-value or 3-value BVA gives teams an unambiguous, measurable completion metric for parameter validation. 3. Synergy with Equivalence Partitioning: While EP verifies general domain behavior, BVA stresses the precision of domain demarcation lines."},{"t":"Decision Table Testing","i":"CONCEPT-istqb-v1-009-01-decision-table-testing","h":"doc/catalogs/istqb/concepts/CONCEPT-istqb-v1-009-01-decision-table-testing.html","s":"accepted","x":"Decision Table Testing Definition Decision Table Testing is a black-box test design technique defined in CTFL v4.0 §4.2.3 used to specify and test complex business rules that depend on combinations of multiple input conditions and yield specific actions. A decision table organizes logic into four quadrants: 1. Conditions: Inputs, states, or prerequisites that evaluate to boolean (True/False) or discrete values. 2. Condition Alternatives: Rows specifying combinations of condition states. 3. Actions: Outcomes, state updates, or outputs produced by the system. 4. Action Entries: Marks (X or True/False) indicating which actions must occur for each combination of conditions. Each column in the table represents a distinct business rule and corresponds to at least one concrete test case. Tables can be collapsed by merging columns where certain conditions are \"don't care\" (-), while preserving coverage of business permutations. text Decision Table: Loan Application Approval ============================================================ Conditions / Rules R1 R2 R3 R4 R5 -------------------------------+----+----+----+----+----- Credit score = 700? Y Y N N Income = 50,000? Y N Y N Existing default on file? N N N N Y -------------------------------+----+----+----+----+----- Actions -------------------------------+----+----+----+----+----- Approve automatically (X) X Send for manual review (X) X X Reject loan application (X) X X python import pytest @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"creditscore,income,hasdefault,expecteddecision\", [ (750, 60000, False, \"APPROVE\"), Rule 1 (720, 35000, False, \"MANUALREVIEW\"), Rule 2 (650, 80000, False, \"MANUALREVIEW\"), Rule 3 (580, 30000, False, \"REJECT\"), Rule 4 (780, 95000, True, \"REJECT\"), Rule 5: Default trumps score ]) def testloandecisiontable(creditscore, income, hasdefault, expecteddecision): decision = evaluateloan(creditscore, income, hasdefault) assert decision == expecteddecision Why it exists Decision table testing eliminates ambiguity and combinatorial blindness in multi-condition business logic: 1. Systematic Completeness: Complex prose specifications often omit edge cases (e.g., what happens when condition A is true but condition B is false and condition C is missing). Tabular formalization exposes gaps in specifications before code is written. 2. Direct Mapping to Parameterized Tests: Each column maps directly to a parameterized automated test row, ensuring 100% rule coverage with zero guesswork. 3. Clarity on Rule Interaction: When multiple business rules interact, decision tables clearly delineate precedence, override rules, and default behaviors."},{"t":"State Transition Testing","i":"CONCEPT-istqb-v1-010-01-state-transition-testing","h":"doc/catalogs/istqb/concepts/CONCEPT-istqb-v1-010-01-state-transition-testing.html","s":"accepted","x":"State Transition Testing Definition State Transition Testing is a black-box test design technique defined in CTFL v4.0 §4.2.4 used when the behavior of a system is stateful—where the outcome of an event depends not only on current inputs but on past history and current system state. The system is modeled as a Finite State Machine (FSM) comprising: States: Observable conditions or modes of existence of the system (e.g., Draft, Submitted, Approved, Archived). Events (Triggers): Inputs or occurrences that stimulate the system (e.g., clicksubmit, timeoutfired). Guard Conditions: Boolean expressions that must evaluate to True for a transition to fire. Actions: Output effects or operations triggered by the transition. Transitions: State shifts from a source state to a target state. Testing covers: 1. Valid Transitions: Verifying expected state changes when valid events fire. Standard coverage criteria include 0-switch coverage (all states and all individual transitions visited) and 1-switch coverage (all sequences of two consecutive transitions). 2. Invalid Transitions: Verifying that attempting an illegal event from a given state is rejected (e.g., trying to pay an order that is in Cancelled state) and does not corrupt the system state. text +-------+ submit() +-----------+ approve() +----------+ ----- DRAFT ----------------- SUBMITTED ---------------- APPROVED +-------+ +-----------+ +----------+ cancel() cancel() refund() v v v +-----------------------------------------------------------------------+ CANCELLED +-----------------------------------------------------------------------+ python def testordervalidstateprogression(orderfsm): assert orderfsm.state == \"DRAFT\" orderfsm.submit() assert orderfsm.state == \"SUBMITTED\" orderfsm.approve() assert orderfsm.state == \"APPROVED\" def testorderinvalidtransitionrejected(orderfsm): orderfsm.submit() orderfsm.cancel() assert orderfsm.state == \"CANCELLED\" Attempting to approve a cancelled order must raise an exception without altering state import pytest with pytest.raises(IllegalStateTransitionError): orderfsm.approve() assert orderfsm.state == \"CANCELLED\" Why it exists State transition testing is essential for testing event-driven, workflow-oriented, and interactive systems: 1. State-Dependent Defects: Many critical defects only surface when specific operations are performed in a specific chronological order (e.g., clicking 'Back' after payment authorization). 2. Robustness Against Illegal Actions: Systems frequently crash or corrupt database state when users or clients execute out-of-order API calls; testing invalid transitions guarantees system resilience. 3. Foundation for Model-Based Testing: Formalizing the state graph allows test generators to walk the graph and automatically synthesize deterministic test suites covering paths and edge transitions."},{"t":"White-Box Statement and Branch Coverage","i":"CONCEPT-istqb-v1-011-01-statement-and-branch-coverage","h":"doc/catalogs/istqb/concepts/CONCEPT-istqb-v1-011-01-statement-and-branch-coverage.html","s":"accepted","x":"White-Box Statement and Branch Coverage Definition Statement coverage and branch coverage are structural (white-box) test metrics defined in CTFL v4.0 §4.3 that evaluate the thoroughness of test suites by measuring which parts of the source code are exercised during dynamic execution: Statement Coverage: The percentage of executable statements in the code that have been exercised by the test suite: $$ text{Statement Coverage} = frac{ text{Number of executed statements}}{ text{Total number of executable statements}} times 100 %$$ Branch Coverage: The percentage of control-flow branches (decision outcomes, such as the True and False branches of an if statement or cases in a switch block) that have been exercised by the test suite: $$ text{Branch Coverage} = frac{ text{Number of executed decision outcomes}}{ text{Total number of decision outcomes}} times 100 %$$ Subsumption Relationship: 100% branch coverage strictly subsumes 100% statement coverage. Traversing every branch outcome guarantees that every statement on those branches is executed. However, 100% statement coverage does not guarantee 100% branch coverage. If an if block has no explicit else clause, executing the True branch executes 100% of statements while leaving the False branch outcome completely unexercised (50% branch coverage). python def processrefund(amount: float, isvip: bool) - float: fee = 5.0 if isvip: Decision: True or False fee = 0.0 Statement executed only on True branch return amount fee A single test with isvip=True achieves 100% Statement Coverage: def testviprefund(): assert processrefund(100.0, isvip=True) == 100.0 Every statement in processrefund was reached! But the False branch (isvip=False) was NEVER tested. Branch coverage is only 50%. To achieve 100% Branch Coverage, we must also exercise the False branch: def teststandardrefund(): assert processrefund(100.0, isvip=False) == 95.0 Why it exists Statement and branch coverage provide objective, quantified insights into automated test suite thoroughness: 1. Exposing Blind Spots: Relying solely on passing tests creates a false sense of security; code coverage tools pinpoint unexecuted error handlers, missed fallback conditions, and unexercised branches. 2. Subsumption Rigor: Because 100% statement coverage misses entire control paths where no code was written (the implicit else), engineering standards require branch coverage as the stronger quality baseline. 3. Refactoring Safety: High branch coverage ensures that legacy code refactorings are guarded by tests that exercise all decision logic, preventing regression defects when control flow is reorganized."},{"t":"Test Oracle and Expected Result Determination","i":"CONCEPT-istqb-v1-012-01-test-oracle","h":"doc/catalogs/istqb/concepts/CONCEPT-istqb-v1-012-01-test-oracle.html","s":"accepted","x":"Test Oracle and Expected Result Determination Definition A Test Oracle is an authoritative source or mechanism used to determine whether the observed runtime outcome of a software execution matches the expected outcome, as defined in CTFL v4.0 §1.4. An oracle provides the criteria for evaluating the correctness of test execution. Sources of test oracles include: Specifications and Requirements: Formal requirement documents, user story acceptance criteria, API specifications (e.g., OpenAPI schemas), and domain standards. Reference Implementations and Pre-existing Systems: An older verified system, a mathematical model, or a high-level reference implementation used as a benchmark. Golden Files and Historical Baselines: Previously recorded and human-validated outputs (e.g., golden snapshots or serialized database dumps). Property-Based Oracles: Invariants and algebraic properties that must hold true for all inputs (e.g., sorting an array produces an array of the same length whose elements are in monotonic non-decreasing order). Domain Experts: Human users or subject matter experts verifying business nuances. python API schema and deterministic calculation serving as test oracles def testinvoicecalculationagainstoracle(taxoracletable): invoice = createinvoice(subtotal=100.0, region=\"EU-DE\") Oracle Source 1: Strict mathematical calculation from regulatory tax law expectedtax = taxoracletable.lookup(region=\"EU-DE\", amount=100.0) 19.00 expectedtotal = 119.00 Oracle Source 2: Contractual schema validation assert invoice.isvalidschema() assert invoice.tax == expectedtax assert invoice.total == expectedtotal Why it exists The test oracle is the core distinguishing feature between running code and actually testing it: 1. No Oracle, No Test: Executing a workflow without an assertion or oracle check is merely exercising code (a smoke or stress execution), not testing it; it only catches unhandled crashes or process exit codes. 2. The Oracle Problem: In complex domains (such as machine learning, computer graphics, or scientific calculations), determining the exact expected result is computationally expensive or ambiguous. Explicitly declaring the oracle mechanism allows teams to choose between exact-value matching, property verification, and metamorphic relations. 3. Automated Verification: In automated CI/CD pipelines, oracles must be encoded deterministically into assertions and schemas to eliminate ambiguous or subjective pass/fail judgments."},{"t":"Confirmation Testing vs. Regression Testing","i":"CONCEPT-istqb-v1-013-01-confirmation-vs-regression-testing","h":"doc/catalogs/istqb/concepts/CONCEPT-istqb-v1-013-01-confirmation-vs-regression-testing.html","s":"accepted","x":"Confirmation Testing vs. Regression Testing Definition Confirmation testing and regression testing are change-related testing activities defined in CTFL v4.0 §2.2.3 performed after software modifications (bug fixes, enhancements, refactorings, or environment upgrades): Confirmation Testing (Re-Testing): Confirms that a specific reported defect has been successfully fixed. It executes the exact test cases and steps that previously failed, verifying that the defect is no longer reproducible. Regression Testing: Confirms that code modifications have not introduced unintended side effects, broken existing functionality, or exposed new defects in unchanged areas of the software. text Change Event: Bug 402 fixed in Payment Gateway Module ====================================================================== 1. Confirmation Testing (Targeted & Narrow): Re-run Bug 402 reproducer: -- Verify payment with prepaid card now completes successfully. 2. Regression Testing (Broad & Comprehensive): Run full automated regression suite: -- Verify standard credit card checkout is not broken. -- Verify shopping cart balance calculations remain accurate. -- Verify order fulfillment notifications are still sent. Why it exists Understanding the dichotomy between confirmation testing and regression testing shapes continuous integration and release validation: 1. High Risk of Fix Collateral Damage: Empirical software engineering demonstrates that a significant percentage of bug fixes inadvertently introduce new defects into adjacent code. Passing confirmation tests is necessary but never sufficient for release readiness. 2. Regression as Primary Target for Automation: Because confirmation testing is typically performed once or twice per bug fix, it is often initiated interactively. In contrast, regression test suites are executed hundreds of times across builds, making regression testing the highest-return investment for automated testing. 3. Optimizing CI Feedback Loops: Modern test pipelines partition test suites into fast smoke/PR regression packs and comprehensive nightly regression runs, balancing fast feedback on immediate changes with thorough protection against collateral regressions."},{"t":"Entry Criteria and Exit Criteria","i":"CONCEPT-istqb-v1-014-01-entry-and-exit-criteria","h":"doc/catalogs/istqb/concepts/CONCEPT-istqb-v1-014-01-entry-and-exit-criteria.html","s":"accepted","x":"Entry Criteria and Exit Criteria Definition Entry criteria and exit criteria are governance gates defined in CTFL v4.0 §5.1.3 that establish objective preconditions for starting and completing a test activity or project phase: Entry Criteria (Definition of Ready / DoR): The set of conditions that must be formally satisfied before a testing phase, test suite execution, or development iteration can legitimately begin. Typical entry criteria include: Testable requirements or user stories available. Test environment provisioned, configured, and accessible. Test data seeded and fixtures loaded. Build deployed successfully and passing automated smoke/health-check tests. Test tools and licenses configured. Exit Criteria (Definition of Done / DoD): The set of conditions that must be fulfilled to declare a testing activity, test level, or release phase successfully complete. Typical exit criteria include: Planned test execution completed (e.g., 100% of critical tests executed). Defined coverage thresholds reached (e.g., 90% branch coverage, 100% acceptance criteria covered). No open unresolved defects with severity Critical or High. Residual risk assessed and accepted by product stakeholders. All automated regression test suites green. text +-----------------------+ Entry Criteria Met +------------------------+ Requirements, Code, -------------------------- Test Execution and Target Build (Preflight / DoR) (Automated Suites) +-----------------------+ +------------------------+ Exit Criteria Met (Gate / DoD) v +------------------------+ Release / Sign-off +------------------------+ python Automated Preflight enforcing Entry Criteria in CI runner def testpreflightentrycriteria(envclient, databasepool): 1. Environment reachable assert envclient.gethealthstatus() == \"UP\" 2. Database migrated to target revision assert databasepool.getschemaversion() == \"v2.14.0\" 3. Test fixtures seeded assert databasepool.countseedusers() 0 Why it exists Entry and exit criteria establish clear boundaries that protect engineering velocity and product quality: 1. Preventing Flaky Pipeline Churn: Attempting to run hundreds of end-to-end tests against an environment that has not passed basic entry criteria (e.g., database unavailable, backend unauthenticated) wastes pipeline resources and generates massive noise. 2. Objective Release Gatekeeping: Exit criteria replace subjective opinions (\"it feels ready to ship\") with verifiable metrics, preventing unverified code from reaching production. 3. Continuous Alignment: In modern CI/CD, entry criteria are codified into automated preflight checks and smoke gates, while exit criteria are codified into deployment pipeline branch protection policies."},{"t":"Defect Management and Defect Report Lifecycle","i":"CONCEPT-istqb-v1-015-01-defect-lifecycle","h":"doc/catalogs/istqb/concepts/CONCEPT-istqb-v1-015-01-defect-lifecycle.html","s":"accepted","x":"Defect Management and Defect Report Lifecycle Definition Defect Management is the systematic process of identifying, logging, assessing, prioritizing, resolving, and closing defects as defined in CTFL v4.0 §5.5. A complete Defect Report must capture unambiguous technical evidence: Identification & Title: Unique ID and concise descriptive summary. Environment: OS version, browser build, runtime environment, backend commit hash. Steps to Reproduce: Minimal, deterministic sequence of actions required to trigger the failure. Observed (Actual) Result: Exact failure state, error message, stack trace, or unexpected behavior. Expected Result: The behavior required by the specification or test oracle. Severity vs. Priority: Severity: The degree of technical or operational impact the defect has on the system (e.g., Critical/Blocker, Major, Minor). Priority: The business urgency of fixing the defect (e.g., P0-Immediate, P1-High, P2-Normal). A cosmetic typo on a company landing page has Low Severity but High Priority; a memory leak in an internal nightly batch job has High Severity but Normal Priority. Diagnostic Artifacts: Screenshots, network HAR logs, execution traces, DOM snapshots, and console outputs. The Defect Lifecycle State Transitions: text +-------+ assign() +------+ resolve() +-------+ --------- NEW ------------------- OPEN ------------------- FIXED +-------+ +------+ +-------+ reject() defer() verify() v v v +----------+ +----------+ +--------+ REJECTED DEFERRED CLOSED +----------+ +----------+ +--------+ re-fail() v +----------+ REOPENED +----------+ text Defect Report Example: ID: DEF-2026-0891 Title: Double-click on \"Checkout\" button creates duplicate order records Environment: Linux x8664, Chromium 128.0, Backend commit 4f9b8a2 Severity: Critical (Data corruption) Priority: P0 (Immediate release blocker) Steps to Reproduce: 1. Authenticate with seeded user 'test-shopper'. 2. Add item SKU-99 to cart. 3. Navigate to /checkout. 4. Rapidly double-click \"Submit Order\" button within 150ms. Expected Result: Exactly one order is created; second click is ignored or button disabled. Actual Result: Two identical orders generated with IDs 1041 and 1042; credit card billed twice. Artifacts Attached: failuretrace.zip, networkhar.json, screenshoterror.png Why it exists Defect management provides transparency, accountability, and traceability across engineering workflows: 1. Root-Cause Remediation: Logging standardized defect reports allows quality teams to conduct defect cluster analysis and identify systemic engineering weaknesses. 2. Preventing Information Loss: Vague bug reports (\"it doesn't work\") cause friction and prolonged resolution times; requiring steps to reproduce and diagnostic artifacts enables rapid developer triage. 3. Closing the Feedback Loop: Automated test failures feed directly into defect management systems as reproducible artifacts, connecting automated test executions directly to resolution tracking."},{"t":"JUnit 5 Lifecycle Annotations","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-001-01-junit-lifecycle-annotations","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-001-01-junit-lifecycle-annotations.html","s":"accepted","x":"JUnit 5 Lifecycle Annotations Definition JUnit 5 (Jupiter) defines scoped lifecycle annotations (@BeforeEach, @AfterEach, @BeforeAll, @AfterAll, @Test, @Nested) that structure test execution, fixture setup, and state teardown per test method or per container. java import org.junit.jupiter.api.AfterEach; import org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeEach; import org.junit.jupiter.api.Nested; import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test; import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals; class OrderProcessorTest { private OrderProcessor processor; @BeforeEach void setUp() { processor = new OrderProcessor(); } @AfterEach void tearDown() { processor.close(); } @Test void processesValidOrder() { OrderResult result = processor.process(new Order(\"item-42\", 1)); assertEquals(OrderStatus.SUCCESS, result.status()); } @Nested class WhenInventoryIsLow { @Test void rejectsExcessOrder() { OrderResult result = processor.process(new Order(\"item-rare\", 999)); assertEquals(OrderStatus.OUTOFSTOCK, result.status()); } } } Why it exists Replaces JUnit 4's runner model and less expressive lifecycle naming (@Before, @After, @BeforeClass, @AfterClass). JUnit 5 makes fixture lifecycle explicit and enables hierarchical context testing via @Nested. By creating a fresh test instance per @Test method (the default PERMETHOD lifecycle), tests remain isolated and avoid shared mutable state leakage. @BeforeAll and @AfterAll allow initialization across the test container when required. Use scoped lifecycle annotations to ensure deterministic fixture allocation and clean resource teardown."},{"t":"Java Records","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-002-01-records","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-002-01-records.html","s":"accepted","x":"Java Records Definition Records are transparent, shallowly immutable carrier classes for nominal data tuples. Declaring a record automatically produces a final class with final fields, a canonical constructor, accessors matching component names, equals(), hashCode(), and toString(). Records support compact constructors for validation and normalization without field assignment boilerplate. java public record UserAccount(String id, String email, int score) { public UserAccount { Objects.requireNonNull(id, \"id must not be null\"); Objects.requireNonNull(email, \"email must not be null\"); if (score = 1000; } } Why it exists Replaces verbose JavaBean and POJO ceremony (private fields, getters, equals(), hashCode(), toString()) and external annotation processors like Lombok @Value for data carriers. Unlike traditional classes, the compiler enforces semantic transparency: the state vector is the header. The compact constructor executes prior to instance initialization without re-assigning this.field = field. Use records for DTOs, domain value objects, map keys, and intermediate pipeline carriers. Avoid records when mutable identity, entity tracking, or class inheritance is required, as records cannot extend other classes."},{"t":"Local Variable Type Inference","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-003-01-local-variable-type-inference","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-003-01-local-variable-type-inference.html","s":"accepted","x":"Local Variable Type Inference Definition Local variable type inference (var) allows the compiler to infer the static type of a local variable from its initializer expression. Static type safety is fully preserved; var is not dynamic typing or Object. java var usersById = new HashMap (); var client = HttpClient.newHttpClient(); try (var lines = Files.lines(Path.of(\"config.txt\"))) { var activeConfigs = lines .filter(line - !line.isBlank()) .map(String::strip) .toList(); } Why it exists Eliminates redundant type declarations where the type is already explicit on the right-hand side, reducing visual clutter and emphasizing meaningful variable names over ceremonial type signatures. It also captures non-denotable intersection types from lambdas and anonymous classes. Use var when variable names clearly convey intent and the inferred type is self-evident from the constructor call or immaterial to local logic. Avoid var when the initializer is ambiguous, with primitive numeric literals requiring specific precision, or when assigning to interface types where concrete type implementation details should not be leaked."},{"t":"Sealed Classes and Interfaces","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-004-01-sealed-classes","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-004-01-sealed-classes.html","s":"accepted","x":"Sealed Classes and Interfaces Definition Sealed classes and interfaces restrict which other classes or interfaces may extend or implement them. The permits clause declares the exhaustive set of allowed direct subtypes, which must be declared in the same module or package. Subtypes must be declared final, sealed, or non-sealed. java public sealed interface PaymentResult permits PaymentResult.Success, PaymentResult.Declined, PaymentResult.Error { record Success(String transactionId, long amountCents) implements PaymentResult {} record Declined(String reasonCode) implements PaymentResult {} record Error(String message, Throwable cause) implements PaymentResult {} } Why it exists Before sealed types, inheritance had only two extremes: completely open (public class) or completely closed (final class or private constructors). Domain modeling often requires algebraic sum types (closed type unions) where the set of variants is finite and well-known. Sealed hierarchies provide compiler-enforced exhaustiveness checks in switch expressions without requiring a defensive, unreachable default clause. If a new permitted subtype is added, the compiler flags every unhandled switch site across the codebase. Use sealed hierarchies combined with records to model domain events, commands, AST nodes, and workflow states."},{"t":"Switch Expressions","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-005-01-switch-expressions","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-005-01-switch-expressions.html","s":"accepted","x":"Switch Expressions Definition Switch expressions evaluate to a value and use arrow syntax (case - ) that prevents fall-through without requiring explicit break statements. In multi-statement branches, the yield statement produces the value. Switch expressions require exhaustiveness when used as expressions. java DayType dayType = switch (day) { case MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY, THURSDAY, FRIDAY - DayType.WEEKDAY; case SATURDAY, SUNDAY - DayType.WEEKEND; }; int score = switch (status) { case ACTIVE - 100; case PENDING - 50; case SUSPENDED - { auditLogger.warn(\"Suspended account accessed: {}\", accountId); yield 0; } }; Why it exists Legacy switch statements were modeled on C: fall-through by default, error-prone break requirements, mutable accumulator variables declared outside the switch, and scoping leaking variables across cases. Switch expressions turn branching into pure, side-effect-free expressions, enforce exhaustiveness checking at compile time, eliminate accidental fall-through bugs, and allow declaring final variables directly initialized from switch branches."},{"t":"Pattern Matching for instanceof and switch","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-006-01-pattern-matching-instanceof-and-switch","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-006-01-pattern-matching-instanceof-and-switch.html","s":"accepted","x":"Pattern Matching for instanceof and switch Definition Pattern matching extends instanceof and switch to test against types, bind scoped pattern variables, guard conditions with when clauses, and handle null explicitly without boilerplate type casting. java public String formatValue(Object obj) { return switch (obj) { case null - \"null\"; case Integer i - \"int: \" + i; case Long l - \"long: \" + l; case String s when !s.isBlank() - \"non-empty string: \" + s.trim(); case String - \"blank string\"; default - \"unknown: \" + obj.toString(); }; } public void handle(Object obj) { if (obj instanceof String s && s.length() 5) { System.out.println(\"Long string: \" + s.toUpperCase()); } } Why it exists Legacy code forced repetitive ceremony: an if (obj instanceof Target) followed immediately by an explicit cast Target t = (Target) obj;. In multi-branch type dispatch, developers relied on chains of if-else or visitor patterns that obscured logic. Pattern matching binds strongly-typed variables safely, scopes them to the matching branch, supports boolean pattern refinement via when, and allows explicit case null instead of throwing unexpected NullPointerException before switch evaluation begins."},{"t":"Record Patterns and Nested Destructuring","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-007-01-record-patterns","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-007-01-record-patterns.html","s":"accepted","x":"Record Patterns and Nested Destructuring Definition Record patterns deconstruct record instances directly in pattern positions (instanceof and switch), binding individual record components to local variables in a single declarative step. Deconstruction can be arbitrarily nested. java public record Point(int x, int y) {} public record ColoredPoint(Point point, String color) {} public record Window(ColoredPoint topLeft, ColoredPoint bottomRight) {} public void render(Object shape) { if (shape instanceof Window( ColoredPoint(Point(int x1, int y1), String c1), ColoredPoint(Point(int x2, int y2), String c2))) { System.out.printf(\"Window [%s,%s] from (%d,%d) to (%d,%d)%n\", c1, c2, x1, y1, x2, y2); } } Why it exists Extracting nested data from immutable carriers historically required repetitive accessor calls (window.topLeft().point().x()) paired with defensive null checks or manual type assertions at each layer of the hierarchy. Record patterns invert construction: where a constructor takes arguments and builds an object, a record pattern matches an object and extracts its constituent parts into variables. When combined with sealed types and switch expressions, record patterns enable concise, type-safe data manipulation without boilerplate getters."},{"t":"Multi-Line Text Blocks","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-008-01-text-blocks","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-008-01-text-blocks.html","s":"accepted","x":"Multi-Line Text Blocks Definition Text blocks are multi-line string literals enclosed in triple quotes (\"\"\") that preserve code indentation while automatically stripping incidental leading whitespace. They support line continuation escapes ( ) and explicit trailing space escapes ( s). java String query = \"\"\" SELECT id, name, email FROM users WHERE status = 'ACTIVE' AND score %d ORDER BY createdat DESC \"\"\".formatted(threshold); String jsonPayload = \"\"\" { \"name\": \"Widget\", \"tags\": [\"alpha\", \"beta\"], \"enabled\": true } \"\"\"; Why it exists Prior to text blocks, multi-line strings (SQL queries, JSON, HTML, XML, Markdown) required concatenation with +, escaped quotes \", and manual n line delimiters, making embedded DSLs difficult to read and format. The compiler strips incidental indentation determined by the leftmost non-whitespace character or closing delimiter, preserving essential nested indentation without requiring awkward flush-left string formatting."},{"t":"Virtual Threads","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-009-01-virtual-threads","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-009-01-virtual-threads.html","s":"accepted","x":"Virtual Threads Definition Virtual threads are lightweight, JVM-managed threads designed by Project Loom that decouple Java threads from operating system kernel threads. Millions of virtual threads can run concurrently using standard blocking I/O APIs without starving kernel resources. java try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) { IntStream.range(0, 10000).forEach(i - { executor.submit(() - { HttpResponse response = client.send( HttpRequest.newBuilder(uri).GET().build(), HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString() ); return response.body(); }); }); } // waits for all tasks to complete upon closing Why it exists Platform threads map 1:1 to OS threads, which consume significant stack memory (1 MB) and incur heavy kernel context-switching overhead. High-throughput servers previously abandoned simple thread-per-request blocking architectures in favor of reactive or asynchronous frameworks, which introduced complex callback chains, fragmented stack traces, and unreadable debugging profiles. Virtual threads unmount from carrier threads whenever blocking I/O occurs and mount again when data is ready, preserving the simple thread-per-task model and standard debugging tools with near-zero overhead. Never pool virtual threads; create them per task."},{"t":"Sequenced Collections","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-010-01-sequenced-collections","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-010-01-sequenced-collections.html","s":"accepted","x":"Sequenced Collections Definition Sequenced collections (SequencedCollection , SequencedSet , SequencedMap ) unify collection types that possess a defined encounter order, providing uniform methods to access first/last elements and obtain reversed views. java SequencedCollection items = new ArrayList< (); items.addFirst(\"head\"); items.addLast(\"tail\"); String first = items.getFirst(); String last = items.getLast(); for (String item : items.reversed()) { System.out.println(item); } SequencedMap map = new LinkedHashMap< (); map.putFirst(\"root\", 0); Map.Entry oldest = map.firstEntry(); Why it exists Collections previously had no common supertype for ordered collections. Accessing the first or last element required disparate, clunky APIs: list.get(0) vs iterator.next(), list.get(list.size() 1) vs deque.getLast() vs sortedSet.last(). Reversing iteration often required awkward indexing or full defensive copying. Sequenced collections bridge List, Deque, LinkedHashSet, and SortedSet under a unified hierarchy with symmetric addFirst/addLast, getFirst/getLast, and reversed() view methods."},{"t":"Unmodifiable Collection Factories","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-011-01-immutable-collection-factories","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-011-01-immutable-collection-factories.html","s":"accepted","x":"Unmodifiable Collection Factories Definition Static factory methods on List, Set, and Map (List.of(...), Set.of(...), Map.of(...), Map.ofEntries(...)) and Stream.toList() produce unmodifiable, compact, null-hostile collection instances. java List roles = List.of(\"ADMIN\", \"OPERATOR\", \"AUDITOR\"); Set allowedCodes = Set.of(200, 201, 204); Map headers = Map.of( \"Accept\", \"application/json\", \"Content-Type\", \"application/json\" ); List upper = roles.stream() .map(String::toLowerCase) .toList(); // returns unmodifiable list Why it exists Replaces verbose patterns like Collections.unmodifiableList(new ArrayList< (Arrays.asList(...))) or hazardous patterns like double-brace initialization. Collections produced by .of() methods are memory-optimized (storing elements in internal fields rather than array-wrapped nodes for small sizes), reject null elements immediately at construction, randomize set and map iteration order to prevent code from depending on unspecified ordering, and throw UnsupportedOperationException on any mutation attempt."},{"t":"Deterministic try-with-resources, AutoCloseable, and Cleaner","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-012-01-try-with-resources-and-cleaner","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-012-01-try-with-resources-and-cleaner.html","s":"accepted","x":"Deterministic try-with-resources, AutoCloseable, and Cleaner Definition try-with-resources guarantees deterministic release of external resources managed by AutoCloseable types upon block exit, properly suppressing secondary exceptions. For low-level off-heap resources where explicit cleanup might be omitted, java.lang.ref.Cleaner provides a garbage-collection-triggered fallback without resurrection hazards. java public void transferData(Path source, Path target) throws IOException { try (var in = Files.newInputStream(source); var out = Files.newOutputStream(target)) { in.transferTo(out); } // in and out closed deterministically in reverse acquisition order } public class NativeBufferHandle implements AutoCloseable { private static final Cleaner CLEANER = Cleaner.create(); private final Cleaner.Cleanable cleanable; public NativeBufferHandle(long address) { this.cleanable = CLEANER.register(this, new Releaser(address)); } @Override public void close() { cleanable.clean(); } private static record Releaser(long address) implements Runnable { @Override public void run() { // Free off-heap buffer at address } } } Why it exists Replaces brittle finally { if (res != null) res.close(); } blocks where an exception thrown during close() would mask the primary exception that caused the block failure. try-with-resources suppresses secondary exceptions and attaches them via Throwable.getSuppressed(). Furthermore, it supersedes Object.finalize(), which was unpredictable, stalled GC sweeps, allowed object resurrection, and was deprecated for removal. Use try-with-resources for all deterministic scoping and Cleaner solely as a defensive safeguard for off-heap allocations."},{"t":"Optional Return Discipline","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-013-01-optional-discipline","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-013-01-optional-discipline.html","s":"accepted","x":"Optional Return Discipline Definition Optional is a value-based container explicitly designed to represent the absence of a return value in public API methods. Idiomatic usage relies on functional transformations (map, flatMap, filter, or) and terminal extractors (orElseThrow, orElseGet), never bare .get() calls. java public Optional findById(UUID id) { return Optional.ofNullable(storage.get(id)); } // Idiomatic call site: String email = findById(userId) .filter(UserAccount::isActive) .map(UserAccount::email) .orElseThrow(() - new NoSuchElementException(\"User not found: \" + userId)); Why it exists Returning null leaves absence undeclared in method signatures, forcing callers to guess whether a check is required and inevitably leading to NullPointerException. Optional forces the caller to confront potential absence at compile time. However, misusing Optional causes performance and design regressions: Optional must never be used as a field type (it is not Serializable and adds object wrapper overhead), method parameter (forces callers to wrap arguments in Optional.of()), or inside collection elements."},{"t":"Lambdas, Method References, and Functional Interfaces","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-014-01-lambdas-and-method-references","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-014-01-lambdas-and-method-references.html","s":"accepted","x":"Lambdas, Method References, and Functional Interfaces Definition Functional interfaces (@FunctionalInterface, containing a single abstract method) allow behavior to be passed as first-class citizens using lambda expressions ((...) - ...) and method references (Class::method or instance::method). java Function parser = Integer::parseInt; Predicate isValid = s - s != null && !s.isBlank(); Consumer notifier = auditService::recordAccess; List parsedNumbers = rawStrings.stream() .filter(isValid) .map(parser) .sorted(Comparator.naturalOrder()) .toList(); Why it exists Prior to Java 8, passing behavior required anonymous inner classes with heavy ceremony (e.g. new Runnable() { public void run() { ... } }), which allocated fresh heap objects on every evaluation, captured enclosing this references unintentionally, and cluttered control flow. Lambdas are compiled efficiently using invokedynamic (via LambdaMetafactory), deferring translation strategy to the JVM runtime and reusing call site handles without extraneous class loading. Method references provide clear, concise symbol references to existing methods."},{"t":"Unnamed Variables and Patterns","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-015-01-unnamed-variables-and-patterns","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-015-01-unnamed-variables-and-patterns.html","s":"accepted","x":"Unnamed Variables and Patterns Definition The underscore identifier denotes unnamed variables and patterns in local variable declarations, catch blocks, lambda parameters, and pattern matching. It signifies that the value or component is intentionally ignored and cannot be referenced. java // Unused exception in catch block try { return Integer.parseInt(input); } catch (NumberFormatException ) { return defaultValue; } // Unused lambda parameter registry.forEach((, handler) - handler.warmUp()); // Unused record pattern components if (obj instanceof Order(String id, , double total, )) { System.out.printf(\"Order %s: $%.2f%n\", id, total); } Why it exists Developers previously had to invent dummy names (such as ignored, unused, e1) for variables required by grammar but unused by logic. This confused code reviewers, triggered compiler warnings (unused variable), and interfered with static analysis. Unnamed variables with explicitly communicate intent to compilers, linters, and human readers, suppress unused-variable warnings, and allow multiple unnamed variables in the same scope without variable name collisions."},{"t":"Statements Before super() and Flexible Constructor Bodies","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-016-01-flexible-constructor-bodies","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-016-01-flexible-constructor-bodies.html","s":"accepted","x":"Statements Before super() and Flexible Constructor Bodies Definition Flexible constructor bodies allow statements to appear prior to an explicit constructor invocation (super(...) or this(...)). These statements execute in the pre-initialization context where instance state of the current class cannot yet be accessed. java public class NormalizedSocketChannel extends SocketChannel { private final String sanitizedHost; public NormalizedSocketChannel(String host, int port) { if (host == null host.isBlank()) { throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Host cannot be blank\"); } if (port 65535) { throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Invalid port: \" + port); } String normalized = host.trim().toLowerCase(); super(normalized, port); this.sanitizedHost = normalized; } } Why it exists Java historically enforced the rule that super(...) or this(...) had to be the absolute first statement in any constructor body. This forced developers to contort validation and argument normalization into unreadable static helper methods or inline expressions (e.g. super(validateAndNormalize(host), port)). Flexible constructor bodies permit preparatory code, argument validation, and intermediate calculations before delegating to the superclass constructor, while maintaining safety by prohibiting reads or writes to instance fields before super(...) returns."},{"t":"Stream Gatherers","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-017-01-stream-gatherers","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-017-01-stream-gatherers.html","s":"accepted","x":"Stream Gatherers Definition Stream gatherers (Stream.gather(Gatherer)) extend the Stream API with intermediate operations capable of transforming streams of elements in ways that built-in intermediate operations (map, filter, flatMap) cannot, such as stateful, windowing, sliding, and short-circuiting intermediate transformations. java import java.util.stream.Gatherers; List numbers = List.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8); // Fixed window chunking: chunks of size 3 - (1, 2, 3), (4, 5, 6), (7, 8) List fixedWindows = numbers.stream() .gather(Gatherers.windowFixed(3)) .toList(); // Sliding window of size 2 - (1, 2), (2, 3), (3, 4), ... List slidingWindows = numbers.stream() .gather(Gatherers.windowSliding(2)) .toList(); // Running prefix scan: (1, 3, 6, 10, ...) List runningTotals = numbers.stream() .gather(Gatherers.scan(() - 0, Integer::sum)) .toList(); Why it exists Since Java 8, Collector allowed customizing terminal stream operations, but the intermediate operations set was fixed in java.util.stream.Stream. Custom intermediate transformations—like batching items into fixed-size chunks, sliding windows, deduplicating consecutive items, or stateful accumulation—required breaking out of streams into imperative loops or external libraries. Stream gatherers provide a standardized, composable, parallel-capable SPI (Gatherer.of(...)) with built-in presets (Gatherers.windowFixed, windowSliding, fold, scan, mapConcurrent)."},{"t":"Scoped Values and Structured Concurrency","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-018-01-scoped-values-and-structured-concurrency","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-018-01-scoped-values-and-structured-concurrency.html","s":"accepted","x":"Scoped Values and Structured Concurrency Definition Scoped values (ScopedValue ) provide safe, immutable, bounded data sharing across threads and child tasks without ThreadLocal overhead. Structured concurrency (StructuredTaskScope) treats split concurrent subtasks as a single unit of work, guaranteeing that all forks complete or cancel before the enclosing scope closes. java public final static ScopedValue CURRENTUSER = ScopedValue.newInstance(); public OrderResponse handleOrder(OrderRequest request) throws Exception { return ScopedValue.where(CURRENTUSER, request.securityContext()).call(() - { try (var scope = new StructuredTaskScope.ShutdownOnFailure()) { StructuredTaskScope.Subtask inv = scope.fork(() - checkInventory(request)); StructuredTaskScope.Subtask price = scope.fork(() - calculatePrice(request)); scope.join(); // join forks scope.throwIfFailed(); // propagate error if any task failed return new OrderResponse(inv.get(), price.get()); } }); } Why it exists ThreadLocal has severe flaws: it is unbounded in lifetime (causing memory leaks in thread pools), mutable from any call site, and incurs costly memory duplication when inherited across thousands of virtual threads. ScopedValue is strictly bounded by a lexical execution scope and is immutable. Unstructured concurrency (CompletableFuture, unmanaged threads) leads to thread leaks, orphaned tasks continuing after caller cancellation, and fragmented diagnostics. StructuredTaskScope ensures that when a subtask fails or times out, sibling subtasks are automatically canceled, restoring clean hierarchical task lifetimes."},{"t":"Compile-Time Null Safety with JSpecify and NullAway","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-019-01-jspecify-and-nullaway","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-019-01-jspecify-and-nullaway.html","s":"accepted","x":"Compile-Time Null Safety with JSpecify and NullAway Definition JSpecify (org.jspecify.annotations) standardizes nullability annotations (@NullMarked, @Nullable, @NonNull) across the Java ecosystem. When paired with compiler plugins such as Uber NullAway via Error Prone, it enforces compile-time null safety with zero runtime performance cost. java package com.example.service; import org.jspecify.annotations.NullMarked; import org.jspecify.annotations.Nullable; @NullMarked public class UserService { public UserAccount getActiveUser(String id, @Nullable String fallbackGroup) { // Compile error under NullAway if id is passed as null, // or if fallbackGroup is dereferenced without a null check: int groupLen = fallbackGroup != null ? fallbackGroup.length() : 0; return new UserAccount(id, groupLen); } } Why it exists Java historically suffered from annotation fragmentation: @Nullable and @NotNull existed across multiple competing libraries, each with subtle semantic differences and uneven tooling support. JSpecify delivers a unified, cross-tool standard backed by major industry vendors. Tools like Uber NullAway analyze bytecode and abstract syntax trees during compilation, catching potential NullPointerException bugs before tests run while maintaining fast compilation speeds."},{"t":"Helpful NullPointerException Diagnostics","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-020-01-helpful-nullpointerexceptions","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-020-01-helpful-nullpointerexceptions.html","s":"accepted","x":"Helpful NullPointerException Diagnostics Definition Helpful NullPointerException diagnostics enable the JVM to analyze bytecode at the point of a NullPointerException and describe exactly which variable, method invocation, or array indexing evaluated to null. java // In a chained expression: user.getAddress().getCity().toLowerCase(); // If getAddress() returned null, the JVM exception message displays: // Cannot invoke \"Address.getCity()\" because the return value of // \"User.getAddress()\" is null Why it exists Historically, a NullPointerException on a line with chained calls (e.g. a.b().c().d()) or multiple array indexing operations only reported the line number. Developers were forced to inspect bytecode or split single expressions into multiple lines with temporary variables to identify the null dereference. Helpful NPE diagnostics pinpoint the precise expression that yielded null, dramatically speeding up root-cause analysis in production logs without bytecode instrumentation or performance overhead. Enabled by default since Java 15."},{"t":"Compact Source Files, Instance main, and Module Import Declarations","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-021-01-compact-source-files-and-instance-main","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-021-01-compact-source-files-and-instance-main.html","s":"accepted","x":"Compact Source Files, Instance main, and Module Import Declarations Definition Compact source files allow executable Java programs to omit ceremonial public class declarations, public static void main(String[] args) signatures, and repetitive standard library imports using instance main() methods and module import declarations (import module java.base;). java import module java.base; void main() { IO.println(\"Starting task runner...\"); List items = List.of(\"alpha\", \"beta\", \"gamma\"); items.forEach(IO::println); } Why it exists Writing small utilities, scripts, education examples, or CLI tools in Java historically carried onerous syntactic ceremony: mandatory class enclosures, accessibility modifiers (public static), String array parameter declarations, and numerous explicit imports. Compact source files eliminate this friction, allowing single-file Java applications and CLI tools to be executed directly (java Script.java) with syntax as lean as scripting languages while retaining Java's static typing and performance."},{"t":"Modern Java HTTP Client","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-022-01-java-http-client","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-022-01-java-http-client.html","s":"accepted","x":"Modern Java HTTP Client Definition The built-in java.net.http.HttpClient provides modern HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 client capabilities supporting synchronous blocking calls, asynchronous non-blocking calls (CompletableFuture), reactive streams, and pluggable body handlers. java HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder() .version(HttpClient.Version.HTTP2) .connectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5)) .build(); HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder() .uri(URI.create(\"https://api.example.com/v1/metrics\")) .header(\"Accept\", \"application/json\") .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10)) .GET() .build(); HttpResponse response = client.send( request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString() ); if (response.statusCode() == 200) { System.out.println(\"Payload: \" + response.body()); } Why it exists Replaces the archaic, cumbersome HttpURLConnection API from Java 1.1, which lacked HTTP/2 support, had awkward streaming mechanics, conflated connections with requests, and lacked modern timeout configurations. It also removes the mandatory dependency on external HTTP libraries for standard enterprise microservices and utilities."},{"t":"Immutable java.time API","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-023-01-java-time-api","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-023-01-java-time-api.html","s":"accepted","x":"Immutable java.time API Definition The java.time package (JSR-310) provides an immutable, thread-safe domain model for dates, times, durations, and intervals based on the ISO-8601 calendar, separating machine time (Instant) from human-oriented wall-clock time (LocalDate, LocalTime, ZonedDateTime, OffsetDateTime). java Instant nowUtc = Instant.now(); LocalDate date = LocalDate.of(2026, Month.NOVEMBER, 6); ZonedDateTime eventTime = ZonedDateTime.now(ZoneId.of(\"America/NewYork\")); Duration timeout = Duration.ofMinutes(15); Instant deadline = nowUtc.plus(timeout); Period gracePeriod = Period.ofDays(30); LocalDate renewalDate = date.plus(gracePeriod); Why it exists Replaces legacy java.util.Date, java.util.Calendar, and java.text.SimpleDateFormat. Legacy date classes were notoriously mutable (leading to race conditions and defensive copy requirements), zero-indexed months confusingly (January was 0), conflated timestamps with time zones, and formatting classes were not thread-safe. The java.time types are completely immutable, thread-safe, mathematically sound, and aligned with ISO standards."},{"t":"Math.clamp and Exact Arithmetic","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-024-01-math-clamp-and-exact-arithmetic","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-024-01-math-clamp-and-exact-arithmetic.html","s":"accepted","x":"Math.clamp and Exact Arithmetic Definition Math.clamp() bounds a value between a minimum and maximum limit in a single optimized operation. Overflow-safe arithmetic methods (Math.addExact, Math.subtractExact, Math.multiplyExact, Math.toIntExact) throw ArithmeticException upon numeric overflow rather than silently wrapping values. java // Clamping values within [min, max] int boundedVolume = Math.clamp(requestedVolume, 0, 100); double boundedRatio = Math.clamp(rate, 0.0, 1.0); // Overflow-safe calculations try { long totalCredits = Math.addExact(accountA, accountB); int safeInt = Math.toIntExact(longCounter); } catch (ArithmeticException e) { logger.error(\"Integer overflow detected in arithmetic operation\", e); throw new IllegalStateException(\"Overflow encountered\", e); } Why it exists Clamping values was historically coded using nested ternaries or Math.min(max, Math.max(min, val)), which was error-prone, hard to read, and prone to argument order bugs. Silent primitive overflow in standard Java arithmetic (such as Integer.MAXVALUE + 1 wrapping to negative values) is a notorious source of subtle security vulnerabilities, loop corruptions, and financial calculation defects. The Exact operations provide fail-fast guarantees when dealing with critical bounds and metrics."},{"t":"Modern Files Helpers and Path Operations","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-025-01-files-readstring-and-writestring","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-025-01-files-readstring-and-writestring.html","s":"accepted","x":"Modern Files Helpers and Path Operations Definition The java.nio.file.Files utility class provides concise methods for file operations, including Files.readString(Path), Files.writeString(Path, CharSequence), Files.mismatch(Path, Path), and modern factory methods like Path.of(...). java Path configPath = Path.of(\"etc\", \"app-config.yaml\"); // Read entire file as UTF-8 String in one call String content = Files.readString(configPath, StandardCharsets.UTF8); // Write String atomically with standard options Path outputPath = Path.of(\"var\", \"output.json\"); Files.writeString(outputPath, jsonPayload, StandardOpenOption.CREATE, StandardOpenOption.TRUNCATEEXISTING); // Find offset of first mismatched byte between two files (-1 if identical) long mismatchOffset = Files.mismatch(configPath, Path.of(\"etc\", \"backup.yaml\")); Why it exists Reading or writing a small text file historically required ceremonies involving BufferedReader, InputStreamReader, FileInputStream, or third-party utility libraries. Developers frequently mishandled character encodings (defaulting to platform encoding instead of UTF-8) or neglected proper stream closure. Files.readString and Files.writeString guarantee proper UTF-8 decoding, buffered chunk transfers, and deterministic file descriptor release."},{"t":"Records and Jackson JSON Serialization","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-026-01-records-and-jackson-serialization","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-026-01-records-and-jackson-serialization.html","s":"accepted","x":"Records and Jackson JSON Serialization Definition Jackson 2.12+ provides native, zero-boilerplate serialization and deserialization for Java records without requiring @JsonCreator or @JsonProperty annotations on individual components. Jakarta Validation annotations can be applied directly to record components for clean DTO validation. java import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper; import jakarta.validation.constraints.Email; import jakarta.validation.constraints.NotBlank; import jakarta.validation.constraints.Positive; public record CreateUserRequest( @NotBlank String username, @Email String email, @Positive int quotaLimit ) {} // Deserialization requires no extra configuration: ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper(); CreateUserRequest req = mapper.readValue(jsonInput, CreateUserRequest.class); Why it exists Traditional POJOs required mutable no-argument constructors, getters, setters, or repetitive @JsonProperty annotations on constructors for immutable models. Jackson detects record components through the reflection API for records (Class.getRecordComponents()), directly invoking the canonical constructor for deserialization and the accessor methods for serialization. Combining records with Jakarta Validation produces minimal, robust, and immutable API boundary transfer objects."},{"t":"Selective Lombok Usage in Modern Java","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-027-01-selective-lombok-with-modern-java","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-027-01-selective-lombok-with-modern-java.html","s":"accepted","x":"Selective Lombok Usage in Modern Java Definition In modern Java, Project Lombok should be used selectively. Annotations like @Data, @Value, and @Getter are superseded by native records. Lombok remains situational for specific features like @Builder(toBuilder = true) on records and logger injection (@Slf4j). java import lombok.Builder; import lombok.extern.slf4j.Slf4j; @Slf4j public class OrderService { @Builder(toBuilder = true) public record OrderConfig( String endpoint, int timeoutMillis, int maxRetries, boolean debugMode ) {} public void run() { var config = OrderConfig.builder() .endpoint(\"https://api.gateway.internal\") .timeoutMillis(5000) .maxRetries(3) .debugMode(false) .build(); log.info(\"Initialized order config: {}\", config); } } Why it exists Lombok was created when Java was verbose and lacked modern syntactic sugar. However, heavy Lombok usage relies on private compiler internal APIs, causing fragility during major JDK upgrades. With Java records, text blocks, and pattern matching natively supported, the necessity for @Data and @Value has vanished. Restricting Lombok to @Builder on records and @Slf4j preserves convenience while minimizing compiler coupling and IDE annotation processing conflicts."},{"t":"Manifold Compiler Plugin Trade-Offs","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-028-01-manifold-compiler-plugin-tradeoffs","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-028-01-manifold-compiler-plugin-tradeoffs.html","s":"accepted","x":"Manifold Compiler Plugin Trade-Offs Definition Manifold is an experimental Java compiler plugin system providing language extensions such as string interpolation, extension methods, operator overloading, and structural typing via compiler bytecode transformation. While expressive, non-standard compiler plugins introduce severe architectural trade-offs. java package com.example.extensions; import manifold.ext.rt.api.Extension; @Extension public class StringExtensions { public static String echo(@Extension String text) { return text + \" ... \" + text; } } // Call site appears to add methods to java.lang.String: // \"hello\".echo(); Why it exists Manifold addresses the desire for expressive features found in alternative JVM languages within standard Java source files. However, it operates by hooking deep internal compiler implementation classes rather than following the Java Community Process or OpenJDK standard APIs. This produces brittle build toolchains that break across JDK minor updates, degrades IDE analysis performance, creates confusing deviations from standard Java language semantics, and prevents libraries from being consumed cleanly by standard Java projects. Its standing is discouraged for production enterprise codebases and portable libraries."},{"t":"Foreign Function and Memory API","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-029-01-foreign-function-and-memory-api","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-029-01-foreign-function-and-memory-api.html","s":"accepted","x":"Foreign Function and Memory API Definition The Foreign Function and Memory (FFM) API (java.lang.foreign) enables Java programs to interoperate with code and data outside the Java runtime (off-heap memory and native C libraries) safely and efficiently without brittle JNI glue code. java import java.lang.foreign.Arena; import java.lang.foreign.FunctionDescriptor; import java.lang.foreign.Linker; import java.lang.foreign.MemorySegment; import java.lang.foreign.ValueLayout; import java.lang.invoke.MethodHandle; // Call C standard library strlen Linker linker = Linker.nativeLinker(); MethodHandle strlen = linker.downcallHandle( linker.defaultLookup().find(\"strlen\").orElseThrow(), FunctionDescriptor.of(ValueLayout.JAVALONG, ValueLayout.ADDRESS) ); try (Arena arena = Arena.ofConfined()) { MemorySegment cString = arena.allocateFrom(\"Modern Java FFM\"); long len = (long) strlen.invokeExact(cString); System.out.println(\"C strlen: \" + len); } // Off-heap memory is freed deterministically upon arena exit Why it exists The Java Native Interface (JNI) was notoriously difficult, unsafe, and slow: it required writing custom C/C++ stubs, compiling platform-specific shared libraries, and suffered high transition overhead with no memory safety guarantees. The FFM API introduces Arena for deterministic off-heap memory lifecycle management and Linker for direct pure-Java native function downcalls, providing type safety, memory safety, and high performance without writing a single line of C code."},{"t":"Double-Brace Initialization Anti-Pattern","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-030-01-double-brace-initialization-antipattern","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-030-01-double-brace-initialization-antipattern.html","s":"accepted","x":"Double-Brace Initialization Anti-Pattern Definition Double-brace initialization (new HashMap () {{ put(\"k\", \"v\"); }}) creates an anonymous inner subclass with an instance initializer block. It is a dangerous anti-pattern that must be avoided in favor of modern unmodifiable factory methods. java // ANTI-PATTERN: DO NOT USE Map badMap = new HashMap< () {{ put(\"env\", \"production\"); put(\"tier\", \"frontend\"); }}; // MODERN IDIOMATIC REPLACEMENT: Map goodMap = Map.of( \"env\", \"production\", \"tier\", \"frontend\" ); Why it exists Prior to modern unmodifiable collection factories like Map.of() and List.of(), developers used double-brace initialization as a pseudo-literal syntax. However, each double-brace expression creates a new .class file on disk, retains a hidden reference to the enclosing this instance (preventing garbage collection of the outer object and causing severe memory leaks), breaks equals() contract comparisons against normal collection instances, and causes serialization issues. It is completely obsolete and strongly discouraged."},{"t":"Environment Variable Binding via System.getenv","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-031-01-system-getenv-binding","h":"doc/catalogs/java/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-031-01-system-getenv-binding.html","s":"accepted","x":"Environment Variable Binding via System.getenv Definition Binding configuration from process environment variables via System.getenv(String) with explicit Optional mapping and sensible fallback defaults guarantees twelve-factor application compatibility and robust container startup. java public record DatabaseConfig(String host, int port, String database) { public static DatabaseConfig fromEnvironment() { String host = Optional.ofNullable(System.getenv(\"DBHOST\")) .filter(s - !s.isBlank()) .orElse(\"localhost\"); int port = Optional.ofNullable(System.getenv(\"DBPORT\")) .filter(s - !s.isBlank()) .map(Integer::parseInt) .orElse(5432); String database = Optional.ofNullable(System.getenv(\"DBNAME\")) .filter(s - !s.isBlank()) .orElse(\"appdb\"); return new DatabaseConfig(host, port, database); } } Why it exists Hardcoding configuration in code or static property files inside deployment archives violates cloud-native twelve-factor principles. System.getenv() allows deployment manifests to inject configuration per environment. Combining System.getenv with Optional.ofNullable and .filter(!String::isBlank) prevents silent empty-string bugs, provides clear default fallbacks, and produces immutable configuration records without external framework overhead."},{"t":"Frozen Plain Object and Destructuring","i":"CONCEPT-js-v1-001-01-object-freeze-and-destructuring","h":"doc/catalogs/js/concepts/CONCEPT-js-v1-001-01-object-freeze-and-destructuring.html","s":"accepted","x":"Frozen Plain Object and Destructuring Definition In JavaScript, shallowly immutable data carriers are constructed using plain object literals sealed with Object.freeze(). A frozen object prevents the addition of new properties, removal of existing properties, and modification of property values. Paired with ES2015 destructuring assignment, properties can be unpacked cleanly into local variables. js export function createPoint(x, y) { return Object.freeze({ x, y }); } const point = createPoint(10, 20); // point.x = 99; // Fails silently or throws TypeError in strict mode ('use strict') const { x, y } = point; console.log(Point at (${x}, ${y})); Why it exists Plain JavaScript objects are open and mutable by default. Passing mutable objects across module boundaries or callback pipelines frequently causes bugs when a consumer modifies an object's properties in place or attaches undocumented fields. Object.freeze() and destructuring realize CONCEPT-prog-v1-001-01-immutable-data-carrier: 1. Mutation prevention: Throws an immediate TypeError in strict mode if any code attempts to alter, add, or delete object fields. 2. Ergonomic unpacking: Destructuring syntax allows consumers to extract required properties directly in parameter lists or assignments. 3. Engine optimizations: JavaScript virtual machines (like V8) can optimize property lookups on frozen objects by freezing the hidden class transitions."},{"t":"Tagged Object Discriminant","i":"CONCEPT-js-v1-002-01-tagged-object-discriminant","h":"doc/catalogs/js/concepts/CONCEPT-js-v1-002-01-tagged-object-discriminant.html","s":"accepted","x":"Tagged Object Discriminant Definition A tagged object discriminant is a JavaScript design pattern for modeling closed union variants. Each variant object includes a dedicated, constant property (conventionally named kind, type, or status) with a unique string literal value. Factory functions ensure each instance receives its correct tag alongside variant-specific payload fields. js export function createSuccess(payload) { return Object.freeze({ kind: 'success', payload }); } export function createFailure(error, message) { return Object.freeze({ kind: 'failure', error, message }); } export function createPending() { return Object.freeze({ kind: 'pending' }); } Why it exists In dynamically typed JavaScript, functions often return ambiguous results (such as false, null, or an error object) to indicate failure. Inspecting return values via typeof, checking for the existence of arbitrary keys (if (res.error)), or using instanceof across different execution realms (like iframes or worker threads) is notoriously brittle and error-prone. The tagged discriminant realizes CONCEPT-prog-v1-002-01-closed-type-union in JavaScript: 1. Explicit variant distinction: Inspecting obj.kind provides unambiguous, uniform routing without checking for arbitrary properties. 2. Realm-independent: Unlike instanceof, string tag comparison works reliably across VM contexts, web workers, and serialization boundaries (JSON). 3. Foundation for exhaustiveness: Acts as the primary discriminant key for switch dispatch statements and TypeScript discriminated unions."},{"t":"Switch on Discriminant with Default Guard","i":"CONCEPT-js-v1-003-01-switch-with-default-throw","h":"doc/catalogs/js/concepts/CONCEPT-js-v1-003-01-switch-with-default-throw.html","s":"accepted","x":"Switch on Discriminant with Default Guard Definition In JavaScript, exhaustive branching over tagged objects is implemented using a switch statement on the discriminant tag (obj.kind) where the default branch throws an explicit error (such as a TypeError or RangeError) containing the unrecognized tag value. This ensures that any unhandled variant causes an immediate runtime failure rather than silently proceeding with undefined values. js export function formatResult(result) { switch (result.kind) { case 'success': return Loaded: ${result.payload}; case 'failure': return Failed (${result.error}): ${result.message}; case 'pending': return 'Loading in progress...'; default: throw new TypeError(Unhandled result kind: ${result?.kind}); } } Why it exists JavaScript runtimes do not perform compile-time exhaustiveness checking. Without an explicit guard in the default branch, unhandled variants silently fall through, causing functions to return undefined. Downstream code will later fail with confusing TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined errors far from the source. Switch on discriminant with a throwing default realizes CONCEPT-prog-v1-003-01-exhaustive-branching: 1. Fail-fast behavior: Any new variant or malformed object is caught immediately at runtime with a clear, descriptive message. 2. Defensive robustness: Protects systems against desynchronized API contracts or missed branch cases during progressive migrations. 3. TypeScript compatibility: Prepares code for static exhaustiveness checking when migrating to TypeScript or using @ts-check JSDoc annotations."},{"t":"Nullish Coalescing and Optional Chaining","i":"CONCEPT-js-v1-004-01-nullish-coalescing-and-optional-chaining","h":"doc/catalogs/js/concepts/CONCEPT-js-v1-004-01-nullish-coalescing-and-optional-chaining.html","s":"accepted","x":"Nullish Coalescing and Optional Chaining Definition Introduced in ES2020, optional chaining (?.) and nullish coalescing (??) provide syntax for safely navigating and providing fallbacks for potentially absent values (null or undefined). Optional chaining short-circuits property access, method calls, or array indexing to undefined if the target is nullish. The nullish coalescing operator returns the right-hand operand only if the left-hand operand evaluates to null or undefined (unlike the logical OR operator which coalesces all falsy values). js export function resolvePort(config) { // config?.server?.port short-circuits to undefined if config or server is null/undefined // ?? 8080 assigns 8080 only if the resolved port is null or undefined (allowing port 0) return config?.server?.port ?? 8080; } Why it exists Before ES2020, inspecting deeply nested structures required repetitive guard expressions (if (config && config.server && config.server.port)) or utility libraries like Lodash get. Furthermore, relying on the logical OR operator for defaults introduced bugs by unintentionally replacing valid falsy values (such as 0, \"\", or false) with defaults. These operators realize CONCEPT-prog-v1-004-01-null-safe-optionality: 1. Falsy distinction: Clearly differentiates intentional empty states (0, \"\", false) from uninitialized absence (null, undefined). 2. Short-circuiting ergonomics: Eliminates deeply nested ternary operators or verbose conditional checks. 3. Safe function invocation: Supports callback?.(data) to safely invoke optional hooks without manual typeof callback === 'function' checks."},{"t":"Try-Finally and Explicit Resource Management","i":"CONCEPT-js-v1-005-01-try-finally-and-explicit-resource-management","h":"doc/catalogs/js/concepts/CONCEPT-js-v1-005-01-try-finally-and-explicit-resource-management.html","s":"accepted","x":"Try-Finally and Explicit Resource Management Definition In JavaScript, deterministic cleanup is historically implemented via try ... finally blocks. In modern ECMAScript (ES2024 / Stage 3 Explicit Resource Management proposal), deterministic cleanup is formalized through using and await using declarations alongside Symbol.dispose and Symbol.asyncDispose. When a resource variable declared with using exits its lexical block, its disposal method is called automatically. js // ES2024 / Stage 3 Explicit Resource Management class FileHandle { constructor(path) { this.path = path; } Symbol.dispose { console.log(Closing file handle: ${this.path}); } } export function processFile(path) { using handle = new FileHandle(path); console.log(Processing ${handle.path}); } // handleSymbol.dispose is invoked deterministically upon leaving this block Why it exists JavaScript engines rely on garbage collection for memory, but garbage collection cannot be triggered deterministically to close sockets, database connection pools, file descriptors, or browser automation contexts. In complex workflows with multiple early returns or async rejections, manual cleanup calls are easily bypassed, leading to resource leaks. Explicit resource management realizes CONCEPT-prog-v1-005-01-scoped-resource-cleanup: 1. Deterministic release: Guarantees disposal runs the instant execution leaves the lexical block scope. 2. Reverse acquisition order: When multiple using variables are declared in a single scope, disposal methods run in reverse order (LIFO), preserving resource dependency hierarchies. 3. Async compatibility: Supports await using and Symbol.asyncDispose for asynchronous tear-downs (such as closing remote browser connections or committing network transactions)."},{"t":"Async Fixture Callback Lifecycle","i":"CONCEPT-js-v1-006-01-async-fixture-callback","h":"doc/catalogs/js/concepts/CONCEPT-js-v1-006-01-async-fixture-callback.html","s":"accepted","x":"Async Fixture Callback Lifecycle Definition The async fixture callback pattern (popularized by Playwright Test and modern test runners) manages fixture lifecycle through a higher-order function that receives an asynchronous callback (traditionally named use). The fixture performs setup, invokes await use(resource) to transfer control to the executing test, and subsequently executes teardown logic inside a finally block once the test completes. js export async function withTempDirectory(callback) { const dir = await fs.promises.mkdtemp('/tmp/test-'); try { await callback(dir); } finally { await fs.promises.rm(dir, { recursive: true, force: true }); } } // In a test runner or custom suite: await withTempDirectory(async (dir) = { await fs.promises.writeFile(${dir}/spec.txt, 'data'); assert.ok(fs.existsSync(${dir}/spec.txt)); }); Why it exists Traditional JavaScript test frameworks (Mocha, Jasmine, early Jest) relied heavily on global mutable state shared between beforeEach and afterEach hooks (let context; beforeEach(() = ...)). When a test failed unexpectedly or an asynchronous timeout occurred, teardown hooks were frequently skipped, or failed while attempting to clean up partially initialized variables. The async fixture callback realizes CONCEPT-prog-v1-006-01-scoped-test-fixture: 1. Guaranteed teardown: The finally block ensures that cleanup runs reliably even if the test assertion fails or an error is thrown inside use(). 2. Local variable scope: Test resources remain bound to local closure parameters rather than leaking into module or suite-level variables. 3. Seamless async flow: Eliminates race conditions by leveraging native async/await execution stacks."},{"t":"Concurrent Promise Fan-Out","i":"CONCEPT-js-v1-007-01-promise-all-and-allsettled","h":"doc/catalogs/js/concepts/CONCEPT-js-v1-007-01-promise-all-and-allsettled.html","s":"accepted","x":"Concurrent Promise Fan-Out Definition In JavaScript, concurrent fan-out across multiple asynchronous tasks is managed through Promise.all and Promise.allSettled (ES2020). Promise.all executes an iterable of promises concurrently, returning an array of resolved values if all succeed, or immediately rejecting with the first encountered rejection (fail-fast). Promise.allSettled waits for all promises to either resolve or reject, returning an array of outcome descriptors ({ status: 'fulfilled', value } or { status: 'rejected', reason }). js export async function fetchUserData(userId) { const [profile, preferences, notifications] = await Promise.all([ fetchProfile(userId), fetchPreferences(userId), fetchNotifications(userId), ]); return { profile, preferences, notifications }; } Why it exists In asynchronous code, running independent I/O tasks sequentially with sequential await statements introduces unnecessary latency bottlenecks. However, initiating unmanaged promises without awaiting their aggregation risks unhandled rejections and lost error traces. Promise.all and Promise.allSettled realize CONCEPT-prog-v1-007-01-parallel-fan-out: 1. Parallel throughput: Dispatches independent network, disk, or API operations simultaneously on the event loop. 2. Fail-fast synchronization: Promise.all ensures that if any prerequisite fails, the caller is notified immediately rather than waiting for slow sibling operations. 3. Resilient aggregation: Promise.allSettled provides complete visibility over batch jobs where partial failures are acceptable and must be individually inspected without short-circuiting."},{"t":"Frozen Array Literals and Read-Only Views","i":"CONCEPT-js-v1-008-01-frozen-array-and-map-set","h":"doc/catalogs/js/concepts/CONCEPT-js-v1-008-01-frozen-array-and-map-set.html","s":"accepted","x":"Frozen Array Literals and Read-Only Views Definition In JavaScript, immutable array collections are constructed by passing array literals to Object.freeze([...]). Freezing an array prevents adding elements (push, unshift), removing elements (pop, shift), splicing, or reassigning existing indices. For keyed collections (Map and Set), immutability is realized by wrapping instances in read-only proxy objects that throw errors when mutating methods (set, delete, add, clear) are invoked. js export const HTTPMETHODS = Object.freeze(['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'DELETE']); // HTTPMETHODS.push('PATCH'); // Throws TypeError in strict mode export function createReadOnlySet(iterable) { const innerSet = new Set(iterable); return { has: (item) = innerSet.has(item), get size() { return innerSet.size; }, [Symbol.iterator]: () = innerSet.values(), }; } Why it exists Arrays and collections in JavaScript are mutable by default. Mutating methods like sort() and reverse() modify the calling array in place rather than returning a copy. When collections are shared as configuration sets or cached lookup lists, inadvertent in-place mutation causes unpredictable regressions. Frozen arrays and read-only views realize CONCEPT-prog-v1-008-01-immutable-collection-literal: 1. Mutation immunity: Prevents accidental modification of constant lookup tables and global configuration collections. 2. Safe sharing: Enables modules to return arrays directly without defensive copying ([...items]). 3. API clarity: Clearly communicates that a returned collection is unmodifiable and represents a fixed snapshot."},{"t":"Arrow Functions and Lexical Closures","i":"CONCEPT-js-v1-009-01-arrow-functions-and-closures","h":"doc/catalogs/js/concepts/CONCEPT-js-v1-009-01-arrow-functions-and-closures.html","s":"accepted","x":"Arrow Functions and Lexical Closures Definition Arrow functions ((param) = expression) in JavaScript provide concise syntax for anonymous callables with lexical this, arguments, and super bindings. Paired with JavaScript's lexical scoping, arrow functions effortlessly capture variables from their outer environment, enabling functional pipelines (map, filter, reduce), currying, and parameterized higher-order control structures. js export const createMultiplier = (factor) = (value) = factor value; export const withTimeout = (ms, fn) = async (...args) = { const timeoutPromise = new Promise((, reject) = setTimeout(() = reject(new Error(Timed out after ${ms}ms)), ms) ); return Promise.race([fn(...args), timeoutPromise]); }; Why it exists Before ES2015 arrow functions, passing anonymous functions into callbacks or event listeners required manual workarounds for dynamic this rebinding (such as var self = this or .bind(this)). Verbose function syntax (function(x) { return x 2; }) also discouraged functional programming styles and cluttered stream transformations. Arrow functions and closures realize CONCEPT-prog-v1-009-01-higher-order-function: 1. Lexical context preservation: The callable retains its enclosing lexical scope and this reference without manual binding. 2. Compact syntax: Single-expression functions eliminate braces and explicit return statements, making data pipelines readable. 3. Encapsulation via closures: Outer variables remain private to the returned callable, enabling clean factory and decorator patterns."},{"t":"Process Environment Binding with Nullish Fallback","i":"CONCEPT-js-v1-010-01-process-env-binding","h":"doc/catalogs/js/concepts/CONCEPT-js-v1-010-01-process-env-binding.html","s":"accepted","x":"Process Environment Binding with Nullish Fallback Definition In Node.js and modern JavaScript server runtimes, environment variables are exposed via the global process.env object. Configuration binding combines process.env lookups with the nullish coalescing operator (??) to provide explicit defaults, convert strings to numeric or boolean types, and freeze the resulting configuration object at module initialization. js export function loadServerConfig(env = process.env) { const port = parseInt(env.PORT ?? '3000', 10); const nodeEnv = env.NODEENV ?? 'development'; const apiTimeout = parseInt(env.APITIMEOUTMS ?? '5000', 10); if (Number.isNaN(port)) { throw new TypeError(Invalid PORT environment variable: ${env.PORT}); } return Object.freeze({ port, nodeEnv, apiTimeout }); } Why it exists Directly accessing process.env in business logic leads to fragmented defaults, hidden type conversions (since all process.env properties are strings), and missed validation errors. An invalid port string like \"abc\" would fail deep within server startup rather than during initial config parsing. Centralized process.env binding realizes CONCEPT-prog-v1-010-01-external-environment-binding: 1. Type coercion and validation: Converts raw strings into validated numbers and booleans with immediate error checking. 2. Deterministic defaults: Provides fallback values for local developer machines while respecting deployment overrides. 3. Immutable settings: Freezing the configuration object ensures runtime modules cannot tamper with global application settings."},{"t":"Per-Method Test Instance Lifecycle and Fixtures","i":"CONCEPT-junit-v1-001-01-per-method-test-instance-lifecycle","h":"doc/catalogs/junit/concepts/CONCEPT-junit-v1-001-01-per-method-test-instance-lifecycle.html","s":"accepted","x":"Per-Method Test Instance Lifecycle and Fixtures Definition JUnit Jupiter adheres by default to the TestInstance.Lifecycle.PERMETHOD lifecycle model. For every individual test method annotated with @Test, @RepeatedTest, or @ParameterizedTest, JUnit creates a brand new instance of the test class. Under this model: Instance fields are freshly instantiated for each test execution, eliminating mutable state leakage between methods. @BeforeEach methods execute prior to each test method on the freshly constructed instance. @AfterEach methods execute after each test method completes (even if the test throws an exception), guaranteeing teardown. Class-level setup (@BeforeAll, @AfterAll) requires static methods unless class-level lifecycle (@TestInstance(Lifecycle.PERCLASS)) is explicitly declared. java import org.junit.jupiter.api.AfterEach; import org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeEach; import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test; import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals; class CartServiceTest { private CartService cartService; // Fresh reference created per test method @BeforeEach void setUp() { cartService = new CartService(); cartService.initialize(); } @AfterEach void tearDown() { cartService.clear(); } @Test void addsItemSuccessfully() { cartService.addItem(\"item-1\", 2); assertEquals(2, cartService.getItemCount()); } @Test void startsWithZeroItems() { // Safe from contamination: cartService is a brand new instance here assertEquals(0, cartService.getItemCount()); } } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-prog-v1-006-01-scoped-test-fixture and CONCEPT-qa-v1-004-01-session-and-context-isolation. Reusing object state across multiple test methods is a primary cause of test order dependencies and intermittent test failures. Creating a distinct object instance per test method guarantees clean slate isolation. Contrasts with CONCEPT-pytest-v1-001-01-dependency-injected-fixtures and CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-001-01-test-extend-fixtures. In JUnit Jupiter, fixture scoping is tied directly to Java object orientation and method annotation lifecycles (@BeforeEach/@AfterEach). In contrast, pytest and Playwright Test use functional dependency injection where tests declare named fixtures as parameters, allowing arbitrary multi-level fixture composition without class hierarchy inheritance."},{"t":"Parameterized Tests and Value Sources","i":"CONCEPT-junit-v1-002-01-parameterized-test-and-sources","h":"doc/catalogs/junit/concepts/CONCEPT-junit-v1-002-01-parameterized-test-and-sources.html","s":"accepted","x":"Parameterized Tests and Value Sources Definition JUnit Jupiter's @ParameterizedTest executes a single test method multiple times with different arguments. It pairs with various argument source annotations: @ValueSource: Supplies arrays of literal primitives, strings, or classes. @CsvSource: Supplies comma-delimited strings parsed into method parameters with automatic type conversion. @CsvFileSource: Loads tabular test vectors from external CSV classpath resources. @MethodSource: References a static factory method that returns a Stream , Collection, or Iterable. Each parameter set is executed as an independent test invocation in test reports with configurable display names (name = \"[{index}] {arguments}\"). java import java.util.stream.Stream; import org.junit.jupiter.params.ParameterizedTest; import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.Arguments; import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.CsvSource; import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.MethodSource; import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals; class DiscountCalculatorTest { @ParameterizedTest(name = \"Tier {0} with quantity {1} gives discount {2}\") @CsvSource({ \"STANDARD, 1, 0.00\", \"STANDARD, 10, 0.05\", \"VIP, 1, 0.10\", \"VIP, 5, 0.15\" }) void computesDiscount(String tier, int quantity, double expectedDiscount) { DiscountCalculator calc = new DiscountCalculator(); assertEquals(expectedDiscount, calc.calculate(tier, quantity), 0.001); } @ParameterizedTest @MethodSource(\"boundaryScenarios\") void checksBoundaries(int inputAge, boolean expectedEligibility) { EligibilityService service = new EligibilityService(); assertEquals(expectedEligibility, service.isEligible(inputAge)); } static Stream boundaryScenarios() { return Stream.of( Arguments.of(17, false), // Boundary: just below minimum Arguments.of(18, true), // Boundary: minimum eligible age Arguments.of(65, true), // Boundary: maximum working age Arguments.of(66, false) // Boundary: above maximum ); } } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-istqb-v1-007-01-equivalence-partitioning and CONCEPT-istqb-v1-008-01-boundary-value-analysis. Testing equivalence classes and boundary values by duplicating identical @Test methods creates maintenance overhead and visual clutter. Using parameterized tests ensures comprehensive coverage of input partitions while retaining distinct reporting per input vector. Contrasts with CONCEPT-pytest-v1-002-01-mark-parametrize and CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-002-01-data-driven-test-loop. JUnit provides strongly typed argument conversion and rich annotation-driven sources integrated directly into the Java type system. In pytest, parameterization is achieved via decorators on functions, while in Playwright Test, parameterization is achieved through native JavaScript iteration loops."},{"t":"Grouped Soft Assertions via assertAll","i":"CONCEPT-junit-v1-003-01-assertall-grouped-assertions","h":"doc/catalogs/junit/concepts/CONCEPT-junit-v1-003-01-assertall-grouped-assertions.html","s":"accepted","x":"Grouped Soft Assertions via assertAll Definition JUnit Jupiter's Assertions.assertAll groups multiple assertion lambdas into a single execution block. Unlike standard \"hard\" assertions—which throw an AssertionError immediately upon the first failed condition and abort the remainder of the test method—assertAll executes every supplied Executable closure regardless of intermediate failures. If one or more assertions fail, assertAll bundles them together and throws a single MultipleFailuresError listing every violation and its distinct stack trace. java import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test; import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertAll; import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals; import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertNotNull; class UserProfileTest { @Test void verifiesCompleteUserPayload() { User user = UserService.fetchUser(42); // All assertions execute even if the first one fails assertAll(\"User profile fields verification\", () - assertNotNull(user.getId(), \"User ID must not be null\"), () - assertEquals(\"Jordan\", user.getFirstName(), \"First name mismatch\"), () - assertEquals(\"Lee\", user.getLastName(), \"Last name mismatch\"), () - assertEquals(\"jordan.lee@example.com\", user.getEmail(), \"Email mismatch\"), () - assertEquals(UserRole.ADMIN, user.getRole(), \"User role mismatch\") ); } } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-007-01-hard-vs-soft-assertions. In complex verification scenarios—such as inspecting large domain objects, API response payloads, or multi-field form outputs—a single failure in an early assert statement prematurely hides failures in subsequent assertions. This forces engineers into an inefficient cycle of fixing one defect, rerunning the test, discovering the next defect, and repeating. Contrasts with CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-003-01-expect-soft-and-poll. In Playwright Test, soft assertions are imperative statements (expect.soft(locator).toBeVisible()) where test code continues linearly until test end, at which point accumulated failures are reported. JUnit's assertAll uses structured functional closures (Executable...), establishing an explicit lexical boundary for grouped assertions while maintaining standard exception semantics outside the block."},{"t":"TestWatcher Extension for Failure Artifacts","i":"CONCEPT-junit-v1-004-01-testwatcher-failure-extension","h":"doc/catalogs/junit/concepts/CONCEPT-junit-v1-004-01-testwatcher-failure-extension.html","s":"accepted","x":"TestWatcher Extension for Failure Artifacts Definition JUnit Jupiter's TestWatcher interface provides lifecycle callback hooks to monitor test execution outcomes without modifying test method logic. By implementing TestWatcher (or AfterTestExecutionCallback), an extension can intercept test completions: testSuccessful(ExtensionContext context): Invoked when a test passes. testFailed(ExtensionContext context, Throwable cause): Invoked when an assertion fails or an unexpected exception occurs. testAborted(ExtensionContext context, Throwable cause): Invoked when an assumption fails (Assumptions.assumeTrue). testDisabled(ExtensionContext context, Optional reason): Invoked when a test is skipped. In automated UI suites, testFailed extracts the active browser driver or context from the ExtensionContext.Store and dumps screenshots, page source, and network logs to the artifact directory. java import java.io.File; import org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.ExtensionContext; import org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.TestWatcher; public class FailureScreenshotExtension implements TestWatcher { @Override public void testFailed(ExtensionContext context, Throwable cause) { String testMethod = context.getRequiredTestMethod().getName(); String testClass = context.getRequiredTestClass().getSimpleName(); System.err.println(\"Test failed: \" + testClass + \" \" + testMethod + \" - Capturing diagnostics...\"); // Retrieve the driver or context stored in the Jupiter Store ExtensionContext.Store store = context.getStore(ExtensionContext.Namespace.create(getClass(), context)); ArtifactCollector collector = store.get(\"collector\", ArtifactCollector.class); if (collector != null) { collector.saveScreenshot(\"target/failures/\" + testClass + \"\" + testMethod + \".png\"); } } } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-008-01-failure-funnel-and-artifacts. Wrapping every individual test method in try-catch blocks to capture failure screenshots pollutes test code and risks suppressing assertion exceptions. Jupiter extensions decouple diagnostic infrastructure from test business logic. Registering TestWatcher via @ExtendWith or the ServiceLoader SPI guarantees that whenever any test method fails across the suite, rich diagnostic artifacts are automatically captured and routed to the failure funnel."},{"t":"Tag Filtering and Parallel Execution","i":"CONCEPT-junit-v1-005-01-tag-filtering-and-parallel-execution","h":"doc/catalogs/junit/concepts/CONCEPT-junit-v1-005-01-tag-filtering-and-parallel-execution.html","s":"accepted","x":"Tag Filtering and Parallel Execution Definition JUnit Jupiter provides @Tag for categorizing test classes and methods, combined with an integrated parallel execution engine: Filtering via @Tag: Tests are annotated with tags (e.g. @Tag(\"smoke\"), @Tag(\"regression\"), @Tag(\"slow\")). Build tools like Maven Surefire or Gradle filter executions using boolean expressions (e.g., -Dgroups=\"smoke & !slow\"). Concurrent Execution: Configured in junit-platform.properties (junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.enabled = true), Jupiter executes test classes and methods concurrently using a configurable fork-join thread pool. Method synchronization can be controlled via @Execution(ExecutionMode.CONCURRENT) and @ResourceLock. java import org.junit.jupiter.api.Tag; import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test; import org.junit.jupiter.api.parallel.Execution; import org.junit.jupiter.api.parallel.ExecutionMode; import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertTrue; @Tag(\"regression\") @Execution(ExecutionMode.CONCURRENT) class PaymentGatewayTest { @Test @Tag(\"smoke\") void processesCreditCardDirectly() { assertTrue(PaymentService.charge(100.00)); } @Test @Tag(\"nightly\") void processesInternationalWire() { assertTrue(PaymentService.transferWire(5000.00)); } } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-istqb-v1-013-01-confirmation-vs-regression-testing and CONCEPT-prog-v1-007-01-parallel-fan-out. Running an entire comprehensive regression suite on every developer commit causes CI bottlenecks. Tag filtering allows executing fast smoke tests on PR checks while deferring extensive regressions to nightly scheduled pipelines. Concurrent execution parallelizes independent test threads across available multi-core CPU architectures, drastically reducing total execution wall-clock time while ensuring test isolation via per-thread contexts or ThreadLocal storage."},{"t":"Browser, BrowserContext, and Page Isolation Hierarchy","i":"CONCEPT-playwright-v1-001-01-browser-context-page-hierarchy","h":"doc/catalogs/playwright/concepts/CONCEPT-playwright-v1-001-01-browser-context-page-hierarchy.html","s":"accepted","x":"Browser, BrowserContext, and Page Isolation Hierarchy Definition Playwright structures browser automation into a three-tier execution hierarchy: Browser - BrowserContext - Page: Browser: A single OS-level browser process (Chromium, Firefox, or WebKit) launched once by the test runner. Starting a browser process carries significant CPU and memory overhead. BrowserContext: An isolated incognito-equivalent session inside a running browser instance. Each context maintains completely isolated cookies, local storage, indexedDB, session cache, and HTTP credentials. Creating a context takes negligible time (typically under 20 milliseconds). Page: A single browser tab or window within a BrowserContext. Multiple pages within the same context share session storage and cookies, modeling multi-tab user journeys. typescript import { chromium, Browser, BrowserContext, Page } from 'playwright'; // 1. Launch a single heavyweight browser process const browser: Browser = await chromium.launch(); // 2. Spawn two completely isolated sessions with distinct storage const adminContext: BrowserContext = await browser.newContext(); const userContext: BrowserContext = await browser.newContext(); // 3. Open pages in each context without session leakage const adminPage: Page = await adminContext.newPage(); await adminPage.goto('https://app.example.com/admin'); const userPage: Page = await userContext.newPage(); await userPage.goto('https://app.example.com/dashboard'); // 4. Teardown contexts independently in milliseconds await adminContext.close(); await userContext.close(); await browser.close(); Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-004-01-session-and-context-isolation. In automated end-to-end testing, tests must execute with zero state leakage while maximizing execution throughput. Contrasts with CONCEPT-selenium-v1-001-01-webdriver-session-lifecycle. In traditional WebDriver architectures, isolating tests requires launching an entirely new browser process per test (new ChromeDriver()), incurring seconds of process spin-up latency, or attempting manual state purges (driver.manage().deleteAllCookies()) which fail to clear indexedDB, local storage, or worker caches reliably. Playwright's hierarchy allows test runners to keep a warm browser process pool running while provisioning fresh, fully sandboxed contexts per test in single-digit milliseconds."},{"t":"Semantic Locators and Strict Element Resolution","i":"CONCEPT-playwright-v1-002-01-semantic-locators","h":"doc/catalogs/playwright/concepts/CONCEPT-playwright-v1-002-01-semantic-locators.html","s":"accepted","x":"Semantic Locators and Strict Element Resolution Definition Playwright Locator objects represent lazy search definitions over the live DOM rather than static node references. They prioritize accessible, user-facing semantics over DOM structural implementation details: page.getByRole(role, options): Matches elements by WAI-ARIA role and accessible name. page.getByLabel(text): Matches form inputs associated with a . page.getByText(text): Matches visible text contents. page.getByTestId(id): Matches dedicated test identifiers (configured via testIdAttribute). Locators enforce strict single-element resolution: executing an action like .click() against a locator that resolves to multiple elements throws an ambiguity error immediately rather than picking an element arbitrarily. typescript import { Page, Locator } from 'playwright'; export async function submitOrder(page: Page) { // Lazy locator definitions targeting accessible semantics const submitButton: Locator = page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Place Order' }); const emailInput: Locator = page.getByLabel('Billing Email'); const termsCheckbox: Locator = page.getByRole('checkbox', { name: 'I accept terms' }); // Evaluation occurs strictly at action time await emailInput.fill('customer@example.com'); await termsCheckbox.check(); await submitButton.click(); } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-002-01-the-locator-ladder. Reliable automated UI tests interact with applications through the same affordances that end users and assistive technologies perceive, insulating test suites from benign CSS refactoring or wrapper element churn. Contrasts with CONCEPT-selenium-v1-002-01-by-locators-and-webelement. In Selenium, calling driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(...)) immediately executes an eager DOM query and returns a WebElement reference pointing to a single DOM node. If the page re-renders, subsequent operations throw StaleElementReferenceException. Furthermore, Selenium's findElement silently returns the first matching element if a locator resolves to multiple nodes, masking locator ambiguity defects that Playwright's strictness mode catches instantly."},{"t":"Built-in Auto-Waiting and Actionability Checks","i":"CONCEPT-playwright-v1-003-01-auto-waiting-and-actionability","h":"doc/catalogs/playwright/concepts/CONCEPT-playwright-v1-003-01-auto-waiting-and-actionability.html","s":"accepted","x":"Built-in Auto-Waiting and Actionability Checks Definition Playwright performs comprehensive actionability checks automatically prior to executing any user interaction (such as .click(), .fill(), .check(), or .hover()). Before performing an action, Playwright verifies that the targeted element satisfies a strict set of prerequisites: 1. Attached: Present in the Document Object Model. 2. Visible: Non-empty bounding box, not hidden (display: none, visibility: hidden), and viewport-visible. 3. Stable: Not undergoing CSS transitions or geometric animations. 4. Receives Events: Not covered or occluded by modal overlays, backdrop scrims, or sticky banners. 5. Enabled: Form controls are not disabled (disabled attribute). 6. Editable: Form inputs are enabled and not marked readOnly. If any check fails, Playwright retries continuously until all conditions pass or the configured action timeout expires. typescript import { Page } from 'playwright'; export async function completeCheckout(page: Page) { // Automatically waits for confirm-btn to be attached, visible, // stationary, enabled, and unoccluded by loading spinners await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Confirm Purchase' }).click(); // Automatically waits for input to be editable before typing await page.getByLabel('Cardholder Name').fill('Alex Morgan'); } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-003-01-synchronization-and-actionability. Modern web applications are fundamentally asynchronous, rendering components, animating layouts, and mounting client-side state dynamically. Manual timing coordination is the leading cause of test flakiness in end-to-end suites. Contrasts with CONCEPT-selenium-v1-003-01-webdriverwait-and-expected-conditions. In Selenium WebDriver, invoking element.click() attempts an immediate dispatch against the element. If an animation is in flight or a modal backdrop has not fully dissolved, WebDriver throws ElementClickInterceptedException or ElementNotInteractableException. Test authors must manually interleave explicit WebDriverWait statements and expected conditions before every interaction. Playwright eliminates this boilerplate entirely by baking actionability verification into the runtime dispatch pipeline."},{"t":"Auto-Retrying Web-First Assertions","i":"CONCEPT-playwright-v1-004-01-web-first-assertions","h":"doc/catalogs/playwright/concepts/CONCEPT-playwright-v1-004-01-web-first-assertions.html","s":"accepted","x":"Auto-Retrying Web-First Assertions Definition Web-first assertions are asynchronous verification matchers designed specifically for the dynamic nature of web applications. Instead of checking DOM state once at an arbitrary instant, web-first assertions poll the targeted locator repeatedly until the expected condition is satisfied or a timeout is reached. Standard web-first assertions include: expect(locator).toBeVisible() expect(locator).toHaveText(expected) expect(locator).toBeEnabled() expect(locator).toHaveValue(value) expect(page).toHaveURL(urlOrRegex) typescript import { Page, expect } from '@playwright/test'; export async function verifyOrderProcessed(page: Page, expectedOrderNumber: string) { const confirmationBanner = page.getByRole('alert'); const orderNumberDisplay = page.getByTestId('order-confirmation-id'); // Retries continuously until the alert appears or timeout expires (e.g., 5000ms) await expect(confirmationBanner).toBeVisible(); // Retries until client-side hydration or API response updates the text content await expect(orderNumberDisplay).toHaveText(expectedOrderNumber); } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-007-01-hard-vs-soft-assertions. Web verification requires oracles that understand the eventual consistency of user interfaces. Assertions that evaluate too early produce false-negative test failures. Contrasts with CONCEPT-selenium-v1-004-01-one-shot-dom-assertions. In Selenium frameworks, standard testing assertions (such as JUnit's assertEquals or TestNG's assertTrue) are one-shot synchronous checks. They extract a string or boolean from a WebElement at a single instant in time and immediately fail if the state has not settled. To make one-shot assertions reliable, test authors are forced to write verbose explicit wait loops prior to each assert statement. Playwright integrates retrying verification directly into the assertion syntax, drastically simplifying test structure."},{"t":"API Request Context for State Seeding","i":"CONCEPT-playwright-v1-005-01-api-request-context","h":"doc/catalogs/playwright/concepts/CONCEPT-playwright-v1-005-01-api-request-context.html","s":"accepted","x":"API Request Context for State Seeding Definition Playwright provides APIRequestContext for executing out-of-band HTTP requests directly from the test runner. It allows tests to seed test fixtures, authenticate users, reset server state, or query backend databases via REST or GraphQL APIs prior to or during browser-driven UI steps. An APIRequestContext can either exist independently (playwright.request.newContext()) or inherit cookie and authentication headers directly from a BrowserContext via context.request. typescript import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test'; test('creates and edits resource via seeded state', async ({ request, page }) = { // 1. Seed backend entity out-of-band via fast HTTP POST const response = await request.post('https://api.example.com/items', { data: { name: 'Seed Product', price: 99.00 }, headers: { 'Authorization': 'Bearer test-token' } }); expect(response.ok()).toBeTruthy(); const item = await response.json(); // 2. Open browser UI directly to the seeded entity page await page.goto(https://app.example.com/items/${item.id}); await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Seed Product' })).toBeVisible(); }); Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-006-01-api-fixture-seeding. Navigating multi-step web forms solely through the browser UI to set up test prerequisites is slow and prone to cascading flakiness. Fast, reliable end-to-end tests seed preconditions out-of-band over HTTP and drive only the specific feature under test through the graphical user interface. Contrasts with CONCEPT-selenium-v1-005-01-external-http-seeding. In Selenium setups, executing API requests requires pulling in an external third-party HTTP client (such as Apache HttpClient, OkHttp, or python-requests). Synchronizing authentication session cookies between the external HTTP client and the Selenium WebDriver instance requires tedious manual cookie extraction and driver.manage().addCookie(...) calls. Playwright's APIRequestContext is unified with the browser engine and handles session and network primitives natively."},{"t":"Trace Viewer and Execution Artifacts","i":"CONCEPT-playwright-v1-006-01-trace-viewer-and-artifacts","h":"doc/catalogs/playwright/concepts/CONCEPT-playwright-v1-006-01-trace-viewer-and-artifacts.html","s":"accepted","x":"Trace Viewer and Execution Artifacts Definition Playwright's tracing subsystem records an exhaustive, holistic log of a test execution into a single portable zip archive. Rather than recording just a video stream or post-mortem screenshot, a Playwright Trace captures: Full DOM snapshots recorded before and after every action. Complete network activity, including HTTP request/response payloads, timing waterfalls, and headers. Browser console output (console.log, console.error) and uncaught page exceptions. Action timeline metadata indicating exact locator resolution times, auto-wait duration, and call parameters. The generated trace file can be inspected locally or in a web browser using the Playwright Trace Viewer GUI. typescript import { BrowserContext, Page } from 'playwright'; export async function runTracedScenario(context: BrowserContext, page: Page) { // Start recording trace with screenshots, DOM snapshots, and network await context.tracing.start({ screenshots: true, snapshots: true, sources: true }); try { await page.goto('https://app.example.com/checkout'); await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Submit' }).click(); } finally { // Stop tracing and export bundle to disk for diagnosis await context.tracing.stop({ path: 'artifacts/trace-checkout-failure.zip' }); } } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-008-01-failure-funnel-and-artifacts. When automated tests fail in headless continuous integration runners, reproducing and root-causing failures without comprehensive temporal telemetry is costly and frustrating. Contrasts with CONCEPT-selenium-v1-006-01-takes-screenshot-and-logs. In traditional Selenium environments, diagnosing failures relies on TakesScreenshot and static driver.getPageSource(). These artifacts provide only a static post-failure snapshot: by the time the failure handler runs, toast messages may have vanished, error banners may have closed, and underlying network failures are lost. Playwright's trace provides an interactive time-travel timeline where engineers can inspect live DOM snapshots, network payloads, and console output at every discrete step of the test."},{"t":"Network Interception and Route Mocking","i":"CONCEPT-playwright-v1-007-01-network-interception-route","h":"doc/catalogs/playwright/concepts/CONCEPT-playwright-v1-007-01-network-interception-route.html","s":"accepted","x":"Network Interception and Route Mocking Definition Playwright's routing subsystem enables in-process interception, inspection, modification, and mocking of network requests issued by the browser. Through page.route(urlPattern, handler) or context.route(...), test scripts intercept HTTP traffic at the browser protocol level without external proxy daemons: route.fulfill(): Returns mock responses with custom status codes, headers, and JSON or string bodies. route.continue(): Allows the request to proceed to the real network, optionally modifying request headers or payload. route.abort(): Simulates network disconnects, failed DNS lookups, or SSL errors. page.waitForResponse(): Awaits specific network responses matching predicate filters. typescript import { Page } from 'playwright'; export async function testPaymentFailureHandling(page: Page) { // Intercept payment gateway API call and stub 500 error await page.route('/api/v1/charge', async (route) = { await route.fulfill({ status: 500, contentType: 'application/json', body: JSON.stringify({ error: 'Gateway timeout', code: 'GATEWAYERROR' }) }); }); await page.goto('https://app.example.com/billing'); await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Pay Now' }).click(); // Verify that UI gracefully handles the stubbed server error await page.getByText('Payment gateway unavailable. Please retry.').waitFor(); } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-009-01-deterministic-replay-and-flakiness. E2E tests often flake when relying on volatile third-party services (such as payment gateways, SMS verifications, or analytics beacons). Mocking or controlling network responses allows isolating the frontend under test and deterministically evaluating rare edge cases and error states. Contrasts with CONCEPT-selenium-v1-007-01-bidi-network-interception. In Selenium, network interception historically required spinning up separate external HTTP proxy servers (like BrowserMob Proxy), modifying browser proxy settings, and decrypting HTTPS traffic with custom certificates. While Selenium 4 introduced CDP and BiDi network support, it requires managing low-level WebSocket listeners and event handlers. Playwright provides a declarative, synchronous routing API integrated natively across all supported browser engines."},{"t":"Authenticated Storage State Snapshot and Reuse","i":"CONCEPT-playwright-v1-008-01-storage-state-reuse","h":"doc/catalogs/playwright/concepts/CONCEPT-playwright-v1-008-01-storage-state-reuse.html","s":"accepted","x":"Authenticated Storage State Snapshot and Reuse Definition Playwright's storageState mechanism serializes all authentication artifacts—including HTTP cookies, HTML5 local storage keys, and session storage entries—into a structured JSON file on disk. Subsequent BrowserContext instances can be initialized with this pre-populated storage state, allowing tests to launch directly into an authenticated session without traversing the application's login UI or re-authenticating over the network. typescript import { Browser, BrowserContext, Page, chromium } from 'playwright'; // Setup phase: Authenticate once and persist session state export async function authenticateAndSaveState(browser: Browser, statePath: string) { const context: BrowserContext = await browser.newContext(); const page: Page = await context.newPage(); await page.goto('https://app.example.com/login'); await page.getByLabel('Username').fill('admin'); await page.getByLabel('Password').fill('secret'); await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Log In' }).click(); await page.waitForURL('/dashboard'); // Dump cookies and localStorage to disk await context.storageState({ path: statePath }); await context.close(); } // Test phase: Initialize a clean context pre-loaded with authenticated state export async function launchAuthenticatedSession(browser: Browser, statePath: string): Promise { const context = await browser.newContext({ storageState: statePath }); const page = await context.newPage(); await page.goto('https://app.example.com/dashboard'); return page; } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-004-01-session-and-context-isolation. Standard UI login workflows typically take 2 to 5 seconds per test to load forms, perform cryptographic key exchanges, set session tokens, and redirect. In a suite of hundreds of tests, repeating UI logins adds tens of minutes of execution time and introduces substantial flakiness risk. By executing the authentication procedure once during setup (or creating the storage file directly via API), dozens of parallel test workers can instantiate isolated authenticated browser contexts instantly. Because each context receives a distinct clone of the storage state, tests remain completely isolated from one another while enjoying fast startup times."},{"t":"Automatic Shadow DOM Piercing and Frame Locators","i":"CONCEPT-playwright-v1-009-01-frame-and-shadow-dom-piercing","h":"doc/catalogs/playwright/concepts/CONCEPT-playwright-v1-009-01-frame-and-shadow-dom-piercing.html","s":"accepted","x":"Automatic Shadow DOM Piercing and Frame Locators Definition Playwright provides native transparency across encapsulation boundaries in modern web architectures: Automatic Shadow DOM Piercing: All standard CSS and role locators pierce open Shadow DOM boundaries automatically by default. Locating an element inside nested Web Components requires no custom traversal logic or shadow root piercing syntax. FrameLocator API: The page.frameLocator(selector) interface provides lazy, declarative locator chaining into elements without stateful context switching. typescript import { Page } from 'playwright'; export async function interactWithEncapsulatedComponents(page: Page) { // 1. Transparent Shadow DOM traversal: // Automatically pierces through open web component shadow roots await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save Settings' }).click(); // 2. Declarative FrameLocator chaining: // Locates payment form inside a secure hosted iframe without stateful switching const paymentFrame = page.frameLocator('iframe stripe-payment-frame'); await paymentFrame.getByLabel('Card Number').fill('4242424242424242'); await paymentFrame.getByRole('button', { name: 'Authorize' }).click(); // Subsequent actions immediately target the main frame without resetting context await page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Order Summary' }).waitFor(); } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-002-01-the-locator-ladder. Encapsulation features like Shadow DOM and embedded elements are common in modern web design, micro-frontends, and third-party payment widgets. Automation engines that treat these boundaries as impenetrable walls introduce fragile, stateful workarounds. Contrasts with CONCEPT-selenium-v1-008-01-switchto-frame-and-shadow-root. In Selenium WebDriver, frames require stateful global switches (driver.switchTo().frame(...)). Once switched, all subsequent calls target that frame until explicitly restored via driver.switchTo().defaultContent(). An unhandled exception inside a frame leaves the driver in an inconsistent state. For Shadow DOM, Selenium 4 requires invoking element.getShadowRoot() and querying within that root. Playwright's declarative locators handle both boundaries seamlessly while keeping test definitions stateless and robust."},{"t":"Deterministic Browser Clock Control","i":"CONCEPT-playwright-v1-010-01-clock-and-deterministic-time","h":"doc/catalogs/playwright/concepts/CONCEPT-playwright-v1-010-01-clock-and-deterministic-time.html","s":"accepted","x":"Deterministic Browser Clock Control Definition Playwright's Clock API enables complete programmatic control over time inside the browser page. By virtualizing the browser's internal timer subsystem, tests can manipulate Date.now(), setTimeout, setInterval, requestAnimationFrame, and performance.now() deterministically. Key operations include: page.clock.install({ time: new Date(...) }): Initializes mock clock mode with an optional fixed timestamp. page.clock.fastForward(millis): Advances time instantaneously, firing all registered timer callbacks in chronological order without real-world waiting. page.clock.pauseAt(date): Freezes execution time at an exact date or moment. page.clock.resume(): Resumes standard wall-clock progression. typescript import { Page, expect } from '@playwright/test'; export async function testDebouncedSearch(page: Page) { // Install deterministic clock before navigating await page.clock.install(); await page.goto('https://app.example.com/search'); const searchInput = page.getByRole('searchbox', { name: 'Search' }); await searchInput.fill('playwright'); // Input uses a 500ms debounce; verify no network query dispatched yet await expect(page.getByTestId('search-spinner')).not.toBeVisible(); // Instantly fast-forward 600ms without sleeping or wall-clock delay await page.clock.fastForward(600); // Debounced callback has fired instantaneously await expect(page.getByTestId('search-results')).toBeVisible(); } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-009-01-deterministic-replay-and-flakiness. Real-world time is one of the most persistent sources of flakiness and performance degradation in test suites. Verifying time-dependent behaviors—such as input debouncing, countdown timers, session timeouts, toast auto-dismissal, or polling intervals—traditionally required sleep delays (Thread.sleep, setTimeout). Sleeping inflates suite runtime and still causes race conditions under varying CI system load. Deterministic clock control eliminates sleep calls, enabling instantaneous validation of complex temporal flows while guaranteeing byte-for-byte reproducibility across runs."},{"t":"Immutable Data Carrier","i":"CONCEPT-prog-v1-001-01-immutable-data-carrier","h":"doc/catalogs/prog/concepts/CONCEPT-prog-v1-001-01-immutable-data-carrier.html","s":"accepted","x":"Immutable Data Carrier Definition An immutable data carrier is a transparent, shallowly unmodifiable value aggregate whose identity is entirely determined by its constituent components. Once constructed, its member fields cannot be reassigned. Instances provide structural equality (equals and hashCode derived directly from component values) and canonical component extraction (destructuring or accessor functions). carrier GeoPoint(latitude: Float64, longitude: Float64) val origin = GeoPoint(0.0, 0.0) val copy = GeoPoint(0.0, 0.0) assert origin == copy Why it exists Traditional object-oriented classes conflate data representation with encapsulated mutating behavior. When mutable data structures are shared across boundaries (threads, asynchronous tasks, or distinct subsystems), unintended mutations cause aliasing bugs, race conditions, and defensive copying overhead. The immutable data carrier archetype solves this by decoupling pure data transmission from behavioral state machines: 1. Thread safety: Being immutable, instances can be freely shared across concurrent execution contexts without synchronization locks. 2. Predictable equality: Value-based semantics allow carriers to safely serve as hash map keys or set members. 3. Intent clarity: Eliminates boilerplate setters, ensures constructor invariants cannot be bypassed post-construction, and forms the fundamental leaf node for algebraic data types."},{"t":"Closed Type Union","i":"CONCEPT-prog-v1-002-01-closed-type-union","h":"doc/catalogs/prog/concepts/CONCEPT-prog-v1-002-01-closed-type-union.html","s":"accepted","x":"Closed Type Union Definition A closed type union (or algebraic sum type) defines a type whose valid instances belong to a finite, declared set of distinct variants. Unlike open class hierarchies where arbitrary subclasses can be introduced anywhere in a codebase, a closed type union establishes a sealed boundary that prohibits unauthorized extension. union AsyncResult = Success(value: T) Failure(error: ErrorCode, message: String) Pending Why it exists Open inheritance models make it impossible for a compiler or static analyzer to verify whether all possible cases of a domain model are handled. When domain states evolve, developers must rely on runtime fallback clauses or defensive default branches that mask unhandled states until runtime. Closed type unions enforce domain boundaries at design time: 1. Accurate domain modeling: Real-world entities frequently exist in mutually exclusive states (such as payment statuses, command outcomes, or AST node kinds) where open subclassing is semantically invalid. 2. Prerequisite for exhaustive branching: By declaring the complete set of valid variants, the closed union provides compilers and linters with the information needed to verify exhaustiveness without default catch-all clauses. 3. Type safety across boundaries: Eliminates error-prone sentinel values (such as returning null or negative integers on failure) in favor of explicit, strongly typed variant payloads."},{"t":"Exhaustive Pattern Branching","i":"CONCEPT-prog-v1-003-01-exhaustive-branching","h":"doc/catalogs/prog/concepts/CONCEPT-prog-v1-003-01-exhaustive-branching.html","s":"accepted","x":"Exhaustive Pattern Branching Definition Exhaustive pattern branching is a language or static-analysis mechanism that verifies whether a conditional dispatch covers every variant of a closed type union. If any variant is omitted, the compiler or checker rejects the program before execution. Catch-all default branches are intentionally avoided or restricted so that future additions to the union trigger immediate compile-time errors. function renderStatus(result: AsyncResult ): String { return match (result) { Success(data) = \"Loaded: \" + data.summary(), Failure(err, m) = \"Failed with \" + err + \": \" + m, Pending = \"Loading...\" } } Why it exists In traditional dispatch mechanisms (like cascading if/else ladders or switches with arbitrary default clauses), adding a new variant to a domain model silently falls into the default handler. This causes subtle defects to escape into production environments when new features are partially implemented. Exhaustive pattern branching turns domain evolution into a compiler-assisted refactoring workflow: 1. Refactoring safety: Adding a new variant to a closed union flags every unhandled call site across the codebase as a compile failure. 2. Defensive elimination: Eliminates runtime throw new IllegalStateException(\"Unexpected type\") guards in business logic. 3. Data extraction synergy: Works together with destructuring patterns to unpack payload fields directly into typed lexical variables within the branch scope."},{"t":"Null-Safe Optionality","i":"CONCEPT-prog-v1-004-01-null-safe-optionality","h":"doc/catalogs/prog/concepts/CONCEPT-prog-v1-004-01-null-safe-optionality.html","s":"accepted","x":"Null-Safe Optionality Definition Null-safe optionality is a type system discipline where references are non-nullable by default. The possibility of absence must be explicitly expressed in the type declaration (such as T Null or Optional ). Dereferencing an optional value without checking for existence or using null-safe traversal (such as optional chaining and nullish coalescing) is treated as a static error. function getHostPort(config: ServerConfig?): Int { val port = config?.network?.port ?? 8080 return port } Why it exists Unchecked null references represent one of the most prolific causes of production outages. When any reference can invisibly be null, every layer of code either performs redundant defensive checks or risks unexpected null pointer dereferences deep within execution stacks. Null-safe optionality brings structural predictability to absent data: 1. Explicit API contracts: Callers immediately recognize whether an operation can legitimately yield nothing, without relying on ambiguous docstrings. 2. Elimination of defensive boilerplate: Because non-optional references are guaranteed present by construction, intermediate domain services do not need repetitive validation checks. 3. Safe composition: Safe navigation operators (?.) and fallback coalescing (??) allow concise traversal of nested optional data without deep conditional nesting."},{"t":"Scoped Deterministic Resource Cleanup","i":"CONCEPT-prog-v1-005-01-scoped-resource-cleanup","h":"doc/catalogs/prog/concepts/CONCEPT-prog-v1-005-01-scoped-resource-cleanup.html","s":"accepted","x":"Scoped Deterministic Resource Cleanup Definition Scoped deterministic resource cleanup binds the lifecycle of an external system handle (such as an open file descriptor, network socket, database transaction, or browser session) directly to the boundary of a lexical scope. When execution exits the scope—whether through normal completion, an early return, or an uncaught exception—the resource's registered disposal routine is guaranteed to execute immediately without relying on garbage collection. scoped (val conn = DatabasePool.acquire()) { conn.execute(\"UPDATE accounts SET active = true\") } // conn.release() invoked deterministically upon leaving this block Why it exists Garbage collectors manage memory allocations effectively, but they provide no timely guarantees for operating system handles, file locks, or network connections. Waiting for a finalizer or GC cycle to close a connection pool or socket leads to file descriptor exhaustion, deadlocks, and corrupted state under load. Deterministic scope cleanup resolves resource contention: 1. Guaranteed release under errors: Exceptions thrown during intermediate operations cannot leak acquired resources. 2. Predictable release timing: Resources are freed the moment they are no longer needed, minimizing lock durations and memory footprints. 3. Composability: Scopes can be nested or chained, ensuring cleanup occurs in reverse order of acquisition."},{"t":"Scoped Test Fixture Lifecycle","i":"CONCEPT-prog-v1-006-01-scoped-test-fixture","h":"doc/catalogs/prog/concepts/CONCEPT-prog-v1-006-01-scoped-test-fixture.html","s":"accepted","x":"Scoped Test Fixture Lifecycle Definition A scoped test fixture provides a controlled, isolated execution environment for automated test cases. It encapsulates the prerequisite setup (such as seeding test databases, starting mock servers, or spinning up browser contexts) and couples it with guaranteed teardown logic executed when the test or suite boundary completes. Fixtures are composable and can be scoped hierarchically (per-suite, per-file, per-worker, or per-test). fixture temporaryDirectory(): Directory { setup: val dir = Files.createTempDirectory(\"test-run\") yield dir teardown: Files.deleteRecursively(dir) } Why it exists Automated tests frequently suffer from test pollution, order-dependency, and flakiness when side effects from one test leak into subsequent tests. Manual setup and teardown methods (such as uncoordinated beforeAll / afterAll hooks) easily drift, skip cleanup on assertion failures, or accumulate global mutable state. Scoped test fixtures establish isolated, reproducible test runs: 1. Isolated environments: Each test receives pristine state, preventing cascading failures across test suites. 2. Deterministic teardown: Cleanup runs reliably even when assertions fail or unexpected timeouts occur. 3. Dependency injection: Tests declare only the fixtures they need as parameters, keeping tests concise and decoupled from infrastructure orchestration."},{"t":"Structured Parallel Fan-Out","i":"CONCEPT-prog-v1-007-01-parallel-fan-out","h":"doc/catalogs/prog/concepts/CONCEPT-prog-v1-007-01-parallel-fan-out.html","s":"accepted","x":"Structured Parallel Fan-Out Definition Structured parallel fan-out is a concurrency paradigm where a parent execution unit dispatches multiple concurrent or asynchronous child tasks and guarantees that the parent scope does not complete until all child tasks have completed, succeeded, or been cancelled. If any child task fails, sibling tasks are proactively cancelled, preventing orphaned background operations and ensuring clean error aggregation. scoped taskgroup { spawn fetchUserProfile(userId) spawn fetchOrderHistory(userId) spawn fetchRecommendations(userId) } // Awaits all three tasks; cancels survivors if any task fails Why it exists Unstructured concurrency (such as fire-and-forget threads or unlinked background promises) causes subtle leaks: background jobs continue running after their parent request has timed out, consuming CPU and database connections. Furthermore, exceptions thrown in background workers frequently get swallowed or logged without proper propagation to the originating request. Structured fan-out brings lexical discipline to concurrency: 1. Lifetime containment: No child task outlives its enclosing lexical boundary. 2. Coordinated cancellation: Failure in one branch cancels pending siblings immediately, avoiding wasted computational work. 3. Transparent error aggregation: Multiple concurrent failures are gathered and presented together without losing stack trace provenance."},{"t":"Immutable Collection Literal","i":"CONCEPT-prog-v1-008-01-immutable-collection-literal","h":"doc/catalogs/prog/concepts/CONCEPT-prog-v1-008-01-immutable-collection-literal.html","s":"accepted","x":"Immutable Collection Literal Definition An immutable collection literal provides concise, first-class syntax or canonical factory methods to construct collections (lists, sets, maps) whose size and element references cannot be modified after instantiation. Any operation that attempts to add, remove, or replace elements is rejected at compile time or raises an immediate unmodifiable collection error at runtime. val allowedRoles = setOf(\"admin\", \"operator\", \"viewer\") val configMap = mapOf(\"timeout\" - 5000, \"retries\" - 3) // allowedRoles.add(\"guest\") - rejected / illegal operation Why it exists Mutable collections passed across service boundaries encourage hidden coupling. One component can alter a list or dictionary while another component is iterating over it or caching its previous contents, resulting in concurrent modification exceptions, corrupted internal states, and hard-to-trace bugs. Immutable collection literals eliminate defensive copying: 1. Safe sharing: Components can expose their internal collection state directly as unmodifiable views without cloning overhead. 2. Memory efficiency: Immutable collections can share structural nodes or compact their internal representations since dynamic resizing overhead is unnecessary. 3. Deterministic behavior: Cache invalidation and change-detection logic become trivial because collection identity and contents remain constant over time."},{"t":"First-Class Callable and Higher-Order Function","i":"CONCEPT-prog-v1-009-01-higher-order-function","h":"doc/catalogs/prog/concepts/CONCEPT-prog-v1-009-01-higher-order-function.html","s":"accepted","x":"First-Class Callable and Higher-Order Function Definition A first-class callable is a function or lambda expression that is treated as a regular value: it possesses a distinct type signature, can be bound to identifiers, stored in data structures, passed as parameters into other routines, and returned from function invocations. A function that accepts or returns one or more callables is termed a higher-order function. function retry (attempts: Int, action: () - T): T { var lastError: Error? = null for (i in 1..attempts) { try { return action() } catch (e: Error) { lastError = e } } throw lastError! } Why it exists Without first-class callables, parameterizing algorithms requires heavyweight design patterns (such as the Strategy, Command, or Template Method patterns) requiring dedicated classes and verbose boilerplate for simple behavioral tweaks. Higher-order functions enable expressive and reusable abstractions: 1. Separation of policy and mechanism: Control structures (iteration, retry loops, authentication wrappers, transaction demarcations) can be written once and parameterized with arbitrary behavior. 2. Concise data transformation: Enables declarative processing pipelines (map, filter, fold) over streams and collections. 3. Lexical closure capture: Callables capture their enclosing environment variables cleanly without needing manual field-binding boilerplate."},{"t":"External Environment Binding","i":"CONCEPT-prog-v1-010-01-external-environment-binding","h":"doc/catalogs/prog/concepts/CONCEPT-prog-v1-010-01-external-environment-binding.html","s":"accepted","x":"External Environment Binding Definition External environment binding is the pattern of reading process execution parameters, secrets, and runtime flags from the external operating system environment (or configuration stores) and binding them into strongly typed, immutable configuration objects with deterministic default fallbacks and validation at startup. val dbUrl = env.get(\"DATABASEURL\") ?? \"jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/dev\" val port = env.get(\"PORT\")?.toInt() ?? 8080 val config = AppConfig(dbUrl = dbUrl, port = port) Why it exists Hardcoding configuration parameters into application source code violates Twelve-Factor App principles and causes severe security liabilities when secrets or environment-specific endpoints are committed to version control. Conversely, scattering raw string lookups (getenv(\"APIKEY\")) throughout business code leads to brittle failures deep inside request handlers when variables are missing or misconfigured. External environment binding centralizes configuration concerns: 1. Twelve-Factor portability: Enables identical application artifacts to run in development, CI, staging, and production by merely varying environment variables. 2. Early validation: Missing mandatory variables or malformed types are rejected at boot time (fail-fast) before the application begins servicing traffic. 3. Security hygiene: Secrets are injected at runtime without being written to persistent source repositories."},{"t":"Typed Fixtures via test.extend","i":"CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-001-01-test-extend-fixtures","h":"doc/catalogs/pwtest/concepts/CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-001-01-test-extend-fixtures.html","s":"accepted","x":"Typed Fixtures via test.extend Definition Playwright Test Runner (@playwright/test) structures test environments through typed, composable fixtures defined via test.extend (). Fixtures can be scoped per test (default) or per worker process (scope: 'worker'). A fixture function receives its prerequisite fixtures, performs asynchronous setup, hands control to the test via await use(fixtureValue), and executes cleanup logic once the test (and any dependent fixtures) finishes. Because fixtures are typed via TypeScript interfaces, test functions receive autocomplete, type-checking, and automatic parameter destructuring. typescript import { test as base, Page } from '@playwright/test'; // Define custom fixture types type CustomFixtures = { authenticatedPage: Page; userRole: string; }; // Extend base test runner with custom scoped fixtures export const test = base.extend ({ userRole: ['editor', { option: true }], authenticatedPage: async ({ page, userRole }, use) = { // 1. Setup: Perform login or session seeding await page.goto('https://app.example.com/login'); await page.getByLabel('Role').fill(userRole); await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign In' }).click(); // 2. Yield fixture to the test body await use(page); // 3. Teardown: Clean up remote session or log out await page.evaluate(() = window.localStorage.clear()); }, }); // Test consumes typed fixture via argument destructuring test('edits article as editor', async ({ authenticatedPage }) = { await authenticatedPage.goto('https://app.example.com/articles/new'); await authenticatedPage.getByLabel('Title').fill('Automated Test Article'); }); Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-prog-v1-006-01-scoped-test-fixture and CONCEPT-qa-v1-004-01-session-and-context-isolation. Traditional before/after test hooks encourage sharing mutable variables across hook methods, leading to order dependencies and brittle setup flows. Contrasts with CONCEPT-junit-v1-001-01-per-method-test-instance-lifecycle and CONCEPT-pytest-v1-001-01-dependency-injected-fixtures. JUnit uses class inheritance and @BeforeEach methods that mutate class instance fields. While pytest popularized dependency-injected fixtures in Python, it relies on runtime dynamic keyword argument resolution. Playwright Test brings fixture dependency injection to TypeScript with complete static type safety, worker-level caching, and explicit async lifecycle boundaries."},{"t":"Data-Driven Parameterized Test Loops","i":"CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-002-01-data-driven-test-loop","h":"doc/catalogs/pwtest/concepts/CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-002-01-data-driven-test-loop.html","s":"accepted","x":"Data-Driven Parameterized Test Loops Definition In Playwright Test Runner (@playwright/test), parameterized or data-driven tests are defined using standard JavaScript iteration constructs (for...of, .forEach()) at top-level module evaluation time. Rather than relying on proprietary annotations or runner decorators, each iteration dynamically registers a distinct test(title, testFn) invocation with interpolated test case names. Each iteration runs as an independent test with isolated fixture allocations, individual retry policies, and granular reporting entries. typescript import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test'; interface TestCase { tier: string; quantity: number; expectedDiscount: number; } const discountTestCases: TestCase[] = [ { tier: 'STANDARD', quantity: 1, expectedDiscount: 0.00 }, { tier: 'STANDARD', quantity: 10, expectedDiscount: 0.05 }, { tier: 'VIP', quantity: 1, expectedDiscount: 0.10 }, { tier: 'VIP', quantity: 5, expectedDiscount: 0.15 }, ]; for (const { tier, quantity, expectedDiscount } of discountTestCases) { test(calculates discount for ${tier} with quantity ${quantity}, async ({ page }) = { await page.goto('https://app.example.com/pricing'); await page.getByLabel('Customer Tier').selectOption(tier); await page.getByLabel('Item Quantity').fill(String(quantity)); await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Calculate' }).click(); const result = page.getByTestId('discount-display'); await expect(result).toHaveText(${(expectedDiscount 100).toFixed(0)}%); }); } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-istqb-v1-007-01-equivalence-partitioning and CONCEPT-istqb-v1-008-01-boundary-value-analysis. Testing equivalence classes and boundary values requires executing the same test logic across multiple input vectors while generating distinct, identifiable test results for each vector. Contrasts with CONCEPT-junit-v1-002-01-parameterized-test-and-sources and CONCEPT-pytest-v1-002-01-mark-parametrize. In JUnit 5 and pytest, test runners require specialized framework annotations (@ParameterizedTest, @pytest.mark.parametrize) and reflection to supply data matrices to functions. Playwright Test treats test registration as standard JavaScript runtime execution: because test() is a regular function call, standard loops, arrays, JSON imports, and generator functions naturally generate test suites without framework-specific DSLs."},{"t":"Soft and Polling Assertions","i":"CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-003-01-expect-soft-and-poll","h":"doc/catalogs/pwtest/concepts/CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-003-01-expect-soft-and-poll.html","s":"accepted","x":"Soft and Polling Assertions Definition Playwright Test Runner provides advanced assertion primitives that extend beyond standard terminating assertions: expect.soft(locator). (): Soft assertions evaluate conditions without immediately throwing an error or aborting test execution. If a soft assertion fails, the runner records the failure details and continues running the remaining test steps. At the completion of the test, any accumulated soft failures are reported together. expect.poll(asyncFn, options). (): Polling assertions repeatedly invoke an asynchronous callback function until the returned value satisfies the target matcher or a timeout expires. typescript import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test'; test('validates full user card without aborting on first mismatch', async ({ page }) = { await page.goto('https://app.example.com/users/42'); const name = page.getByTestId('user-name'); const role = page.getByTestId('user-role'); const email = page.getByTestId('user-email'); // Soft assertions: All checks run even if name or role fails await expect.soft(name).toHaveText('Jordan Lee'); await expect.soft(role).toHaveText('Admin'); await expect.soft(email).toHaveText('jordan@example.com'); // Polling assertion: Polls an asynchronous backend API until status becomes 'ready' await expect.poll(async () = { const res = await page.request.get('https://api.example.com/sync-status'); const data = await res.json(); return data.status; }, { intervals: [500, 1000], timeout: 10000 }).toBe('ready'); }); Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-007-01-hard-vs-soft-assertions. Complex UI screens display multiple interrelated pieces of data (e.g. dashboards, summaries, or reports). Using standard hard assertions aborts the test on the first mismatch, hiding information about whether the remaining 10 fields on the screen are valid or broken. Contrasts with CONCEPT-junit-v1-003-01-assertall-grouped-assertions. In JUnit Jupiter, soft assertions are constrained within a localized closure block (Assertions.assertAll(...)). In Playwright Test, expect.soft() can be interleaved linearly anywhere within natural test workflows, allowing tests to navigate across pages and perform multi-step interactions while accumulating validation findings throughout the entire scenario."},{"t":"Automatic Trace and Artifact Configuration","i":"CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-004-01-automatic-trace-and-screenshot-config","h":"doc/catalogs/pwtest/concepts/CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-004-01-automatic-trace-and-screenshot-config.html","s":"accepted","x":"Automatic Trace and Artifact Configuration Definition Playwright Test Runner (@playwright/test) provides declarative configuration in playwright.config.ts for automated diagnostic artifact collection upon failure: trace: 'retain-on-failure': In-memory recording of complete execution traces (DOM snapshots, network waterfall, console logs) that are persisted to disk only when a test fails. On passing tests, trace buffers are discarded, incurring zero disk I/O overhead. screenshot: 'only-on-failure': Automatically captures a full-page or viewport screenshot at the instant an assertion fails or an unexpected exception occurs. video: 'retain-on-failure': Records screen video in the background and saves the video file only for failed runs. typescript // playwright.config.ts import { defineConfig, devices } from '@playwright/test'; export default defineConfig({ testDir: './tests', outputDir: 'test-results', // Destination for failure artifacts use: { baseURL: 'https://app.example.com', // Zero-overhead passing tests, rich diagnostics on failure trace: 'retain-on-failure', screenshot: 'only-on-failure', video: 'retain-on-failure', }, projects: [ { name: 'chromium', use: { ...devices['Desktop Chrome'] } }, ], }); Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-008-01-failure-funnel-and-artifacts. Collecting screenshots, full execution traces, and video recordings for every passing test wastes gigabytes of CI artifact storage and degrades test execution speeds due to continuous file compression and disk writes. Declarative conditional capture (retain-on-failure) solves the trade-off between execution speed and diagnostic depth. Passing tests complete with minimal overhead, while failed tests automatically emit full-fidelity trace packages directly into CI reports without requiring any manual try/catch or screenshot code in test files."},{"t":"Multi-Project Matrix and Worker Sharding","i":"CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-005-01-projects-matrix-and-sharding","h":"doc/catalogs/pwtest/concepts/CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-005-01-projects-matrix-and-sharding.html","s":"accepted","x":"Multi-Project Matrix and Worker Sharding Definition Playwright Test Runner (@playwright/test) provides matrix orchestration and distributed parallel execution primitives: projects Matrix: Configured in playwright.config.ts, projects define distinct test environments (e.g. Chromium, Firefox, WebKit, Mobile Safari) sharing the same test codebase, with specific browser options, viewports, or device emulations. Tag Filtering: Tests are tagged using @tag syntax in test titles or the tag option (test('verifies order', { tag: '@smoke' }, ...)), selectable via CLI --grep \"@smoke\". Sharding: The --shard=x/y command splits the total test execution suite evenly across multiple independent CI worker nodes (e.g., --shard=1/4, --shard=2/4), providing horizontal linear scaling. typescript // playwright.config.ts import { defineConfig, devices } from '@playwright/test'; export default defineConfig({ fullyParallel: true, // Run individual tests inside files concurrently workers: process.env.CI ? 4 : undefined, projects: [ { name: 'desktop-chrome', use: { ...devices['Desktop Chrome'] }, }, { name: 'desktop-firefox', use: { ...devices['Desktop Firefox'] }, }, { name: 'mobile-safari', use: { ...devices['iPhone 14'] }, }, ], }); CLI commands: bash npx playwright test --grep \"@smoke\" Execute only smoke tagged tests across the project matrix npx playwright test --shard=1/4 Shard execution across 4 parallel CI machines (Worker 1 of 4) Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-istqb-v1-013-01-confirmation-vs-regression-testing and CONCEPT-prog-v1-007-01-parallel-fan-out. Large test suites running across multiple browser engines and device form factors quickly become too slow for single-machine sequential execution. Multi-project configuration unifies cross-browser and cross-device testing within a single declarative configuration file. Native --shard support enables zero-configuration parallel horizontal scaling across distributed CI job matrices, cutting total pipeline execution time to minutes."},{"t":"Dependency-Injected Fixtures and Scoped Lifecycles","i":"CONCEPT-pytest-v1-001-01-dependency-injected-fixtures","h":"doc/catalogs/pytest/concepts/CONCEPT-pytest-v1-001-01-dependency-injected-fixtures.html","s":"accepted","x":"Dependency-Injected Fixtures and Scoped Lifecycles Definition Pytest fixtures are modular, composable dependency injection providers declared via @pytest.fixture. Test functions and higher-level fixtures request dependencies explicitly by listing fixture names as function arguments. Fixtures support distinct lifecycle scopes: function (default): Created and torn down for each individual test function. class: Shared across methods in a test class. module: Created once per test file. package: Shared across tests within a subpackage directory. session: Created once per entire test invocation. Teardown logic is expressed using Python generators: code prior to yield runs during setup, the yielded value is injected into the test, and code following yield executes during teardown even if test execution raises an exception. python import pytest from playwright.syncapi import syncplaywright, Browser, BrowserContext, Page @pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\") def browser(): \"\"\"Heavyweight browser launched once per test run.\"\"\" with syncplaywright() as p: browserinstance = p.chromium.launch() yield browserinstance browserinstance.close() @pytest.fixture(scope=\"function\") def page(browser: Browser) - Page: \"\"\"Lightweight isolated context and page per test function.\"\"\" context: BrowserContext = browser.newcontext() pageinstance: Page = context.newpage() yield pageinstance context.close() def testuserdashboard(page: Page): page.goto(\"https://app.example.com/dashboard\") assert page.isvisible(\"header dashboard-header\") Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-prog-v1-006-01-scoped-test-fixture and CONCEPT-qa-v1-004-01-session-and-context-isolation. Reusable test state requires granular lifecycle control without tight coupling or rigid class inheritance trees. Contrasts with CONCEPT-junit-v1-001-01-per-method-test-instance-lifecycle and CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-001-01-test-extend-fixtures. JUnit relies on class inheritance and @BeforeEach/@AfterEach annotations that bind state to class fields. Pytest uses functional dependency injection with automatic resolution of directed acyclic graph (DAG) fixture dependencies, automatic caching per scope, and centralized discovery via conftest.py. Playwright Test adopts a similar philosophy in TypeScript via test.extend()."},{"t":"Parameterized Test Matrix via mark.parametrize","i":"CONCEPT-pytest-v1-002-01-mark-parametrize","h":"doc/catalogs/pytest/concepts/CONCEPT-pytest-v1-002-01-mark-parametrize.html","s":"accepted","x":"Parameterized Test Matrix via mark.parametrize Definition Pytest's @pytest.mark.parametrize decorator defines multi-variable, data-driven test matrices. It accepts a comma-separated string of argument names, an iterable of test vectors (tuples), and optional custom test identifiers via ids. When multiple @pytest.mark.parametrize decorators are stacked on a single test function, pytest calculates the Cartesian product of all argument sets, generating an isolated test execution for every combination. python import pytest @pytest.mark.parametrize( \"tier,quantity,expecteddiscount\", [ (\"STANDARD\", 1, 0.00), (\"STANDARD\", 10, 0.05), (\"VIP\", 1, 0.10), (\"VIP\", 5, 0.15), ], ids=[\"std-single\", \"std-bulk\", \"vip-single\", \"vip-bulk\"] ) def testdiscountcalculation(tier: str, quantity: int, expecteddiscount: float): discount = calculatediscount(tier, quantity) assert discount == pytest.approx(expecteddiscount, abs=1e-3) @pytest.mark.parametrize( \"age,expectedeligible\", [ pytest.param(17, False, id=\"boundary-below-min\"), pytest.param(18, True, id=\"boundary-exact-min\"), pytest.param(65, True, id=\"boundary-exact-max\"), pytest.param(66, False, id=\"boundary-above-max\"), ] ) def testageeligibilityboundaries(age: int, expectedeligible: bool): assert checkeligibility(age) == expectedeligible Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-istqb-v1-007-01-equivalence-partitioning and CONCEPT-istqb-v1-008-01-boundary-value-analysis. Systematic quality assurance requires validating edge boundaries and partition classes thoroughly without code duplication. Contrasts with CONCEPT-junit-v1-002-01-parameterized-test-and-sources and CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-002-01-data-driven-test-loop. In JUnit 5, parameterization relies on reflection-based value sources (@CsvSource, @MethodSource) and class annotations. In Playwright Test, parameterization is achieved by wrapping tests in native JavaScript loops. Pytest provides a declarative Pythonic decorator syntax with fine-grained control via pytest.param to attach specific markers or expectations (such as marks=pytest.mark.xfail) to individual test vectors."},{"t":"Assert Statement AST Introspection","i":"CONCEPT-pytest-v1-003-01-assert-introspection","h":"doc/catalogs/pytest/concepts/CONCEPT-pytest-v1-003-01-assert-introspection.html","s":"accepted","x":"Assert Statement AST Introspection Definition Pytest intercepts and rewrites Python's native assert statements at module import time using Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) rewriting. Instead of requiring test authors to learn specialized assertion helper methods (such as self.assertEqual, self.assertIn, or self.assertDictEqual from standard unittest), tests write standard Python expressions with plain assert. When an assertion evaluates to false, pytest inspects the rewritten AST to compute intermediate values, perform granular structural diffs on complex dictionaries, lists, and dataclasses, and generate detailed human-readable error reports. python from dataclasses import dataclass @dataclass(frozen=True) class UserProfile: id: int name: str roles: list[str] def testuserprofilepayload(): actual = UserProfile(id=101, name=\"Jordan Lee\", roles=[\"viewer\", \"editor\"]) expected = UserProfile(id=101, name=\"Jordan Lee\", roles=[\"viewer\", \"admin\"]) Pytest AST rewriting intercepts failure and renders precise diff: Differing items: roles: ['viewer', 'editor'] != ['viewer', 'admin'] Use -v to see full diff assert actual == expected Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-007-01-hard-vs-soft-assertions. Clear, actionable assertion diagnostics are necessary for diagnosing test failures quickly. Helper method assertions add mental friction, while plain Python assert without rewriting produces an uninformative AssertionError with no contextual comparison values. AST introspection brings the best of both worlds: tests are authored using clean, unadorned native language syntax, while test runners deliver rich, contextual diff diagnostics upon failure."},{"t":"Failure Reporting Hook via makereport","i":"CONCEPT-pytest-v1-004-01-makereport-failure-hook","h":"doc/catalogs/pytest/concepts/CONCEPT-pytest-v1-004-01-makereport-failure-hook.html","s":"accepted","x":"Failure Reporting Hook via makereport Definition Pytest's plugin architecture exposes pytestruntestmakereport, a generator hook called during each phase of test execution: setup, call, and teardown. By wrapping the hook execution with outcome = yield, an implementation receives the generated TestReport object. If report.when == \"call\" and report.failed evaluates to true, the hook can inspect the failed test item, extract active browser driver or page fixtures, capture screenshots, trace files, or DOM dumps, and attach them directly to HTML reports or test artifact stores. python \"\"\"Fixture and hook definitions in conftest.py.\"\"\" import pytest from pathlib import Path @pytest.hookimpl(tryfirst=True, hookwrapper=True) def pytestruntestmakereport(item, call): Execute all other hooks to obtain the report object outcome = yield report = outcome.getresult() Capture diagnostic artifacts only when the test execution phase fails if report.when == \"call\" and report.failed: page = item.funcargs.get(\"page\") if page: artifactdir = Path(\"reports/artifacts\") artifactdir.mkdir(parents=True, existok=True) screenshotpath = artifactdir / f\"{item.name}.png\" page.screenshot(path=str(screenshotpath), fullpage=True) print(f\" n[FAILURE ARTIFACT] Saved screenshot to {screenshotpath}\") Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-008-01-failure-funnel-and-artifacts. When running tests in headless CI pipelines, diagnosing failures requires capturing visual and network context precisely when an assertion fails. Hardcoding screenshot capture in individual test functions creates duplication and obscures core test logic. The pytestruntestmakereport hook cleanly isolates diagnostic capture logic into the test harness, guaranteeing that every failure across the test suite is captured consistently without modifying test authoring patterns."},{"t":"Markers and xdist Worker Parallelism","i":"CONCEPT-pytest-v1-005-01-markers-and-xdist-parallelism","h":"doc/catalogs/pytest/concepts/CONCEPT-pytest-v1-005-01-markers-and-xdist-parallelism.html","s":"accepted","x":"Markers and xdist Worker Parallelism Definition Pytest provides custom test metadata tagging via @pytest.mark. , combined with multi-process parallel distribution via the pytest-xdist plugin: Test Selection via Markers: Tests are decorated with custom marks (such as @pytest.mark.smoke, @pytest.mark.regression, @pytest.mark.integration). The CLI -m switch filters tests using boolean expressions (e.g. pytest -m \"smoke and not slow\"). Process Parallelism via xdist: Invoking pytest -n auto spawns independent Python worker subprocesses equal to available CPU cores. Tests are distributed across workers, each maintaining an isolated Python runtime and memory space. python import pytest @pytest.mark.smoke def testloginflow(page): page.goto(\"https://app.example.com/login\") assert page.isvisible(\"button login-submit\") @pytest.mark.regression @pytest.mark.slow def testendtoendorderfulfillment(page): page.goto(\"https://app.example.com/orders\") Multi-step checkout verification assert page.isvisible(\"div order-history\") Execution commands: bash pytest -m \"smoke\" Run only fast smoke tests on pull request commits pytest -n 4 -m \"regression\" Execute full test suite in parallel across 4 worker processes Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-istqb-v1-013-01-confirmation-vs-regression-testing and CONCEPT-prog-v1-007-01-parallel-fan-out. Running comprehensive regression suites sequentially on single threads leads to unacceptably long CI feedback cycles. Markers enable selective execution based on testing intent (confirmation checks on hotfixes versus full regressions on integration branches). pytest-xdist achieves linear wall-clock speedups by distributing test workloads across isolated OS processes, preventing shared-memory state cross-contamination between parallel workers."},{"t":"Frozen Dataclass","i":"CONCEPT-python-v1-001-01-frozen-dataclass","h":"doc/catalogs/python/concepts/CONCEPT-python-v1-001-01-frozen-dataclass.html","s":"accepted","x":"Frozen Dataclass Definition A frozen dataclass in Python is an immutable data carrier declared using the @dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True) decorator. It automatically generates init, repr, eq, and hash methods while preventing field reassignment at runtime by intercepting setattr. Combining frozen=True with slots=True eliminates the per-instance dict, reducing memory footprint and preventing dynamic attribute injection. python from dataclasses import dataclass @dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True) class Coordinate: x: float y: float origin = Coordinate(0.0, 0.0) assert origin == Coordinate(0.0, 0.0) assert hash(origin) == hash(Coordinate(0.0, 0.0)) Why it exists Prior to dataclasses, Python developers relied on plain classes (requiring manual init, repr, and eq implementations), namedtuple (which lacks type hinting integration and suffers from unexpected tuple index slicing semantics), or third-party libraries like attrs. Standard mutable classes easily cause bugs when instances are inadvertently modified after being stored in shared caches or passed between worker threads. @dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True) realizes CONCEPT-prog-v1-001-01-immutable-data-carrier for Python: 1. Hashability and immutability: Providing a deterministic hash allows frozen dataclasses to be safely used as dictionary keys and set elements. 2. Memory efficiency: The addition of slots=True in Python 3.10 makes instances lightweight and comparable in memory performance to C structs. 3. Contrast with Java and TypeScript: Unlike Java records (CONCEPT-java-v1-002-01-records), immutability is enforced at the Python bytecode/runtime level rather than JVM final field semantics; and unlike TypeScript readonly interfaces (CONCEPT-ts-v1-001-01-readonly-interface-and-type), frozen dataclasses enforce protection at runtime as well as static type checks."},{"t":"Python Type Union","i":"CONCEPT-python-v1-002-01-union-type","h":"doc/catalogs/python/concepts/CONCEPT-python-v1-002-01-union-type.html","s":"accepted","x":"Python Type Union Definition In Python 3.10+, union types are expressed natively using the pipe operator (X Y, PEP 604) in place of typing.Union[X, Y]. A type union indicates that a variable or return value can hold an instance of any of the specified member types. When combined with type aliases and distinct class variants, it forms Python's idiomatic realization of a closed type union. python from dataclasses import dataclass @dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True) class Success: payload: str @dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True) class Failure: errorcode: int message: str type CommandResult = Success Failure Why it exists Before PEP 604, writing union types required importing typing.Union and wrapping types in verbose subscript notation, hindering adoption of type annotations across Python codebases. Additionally, runtime introspection of unions was awkward and incompatible with isinstance checks. The native X Y syntax realizes CONCEPT-prog-v1-002-01-closed-type-union in Python: 1. Readable algebraic composition: Defining sum types is lightweight and requires no external metaclass or base library. 2. Runtime isinstance support: isinstance(val, int str) is valid at runtime in Python 3.10+, aligning static type syntax with runtime validation. 3. Contrast with Java and TypeScript: Java sealed classes (CONCEPT-java-v1-004-01-sealed-classes) enforce a nominal closed hierarchy through inheritance permits. TypeScript discriminated unions (CONCEPT-ts-v1-002-01-discriminated-union) use structural discriminant fields. Python's union types can operate either nominally across separate dataclass types or structurally via pattern matching."},{"t":"Structural Pattern Matching","i":"CONCEPT-python-v1-003-01-structural-pattern-matching","h":"doc/catalogs/python/concepts/CONCEPT-python-v1-003-01-structural-pattern-matching.html","s":"accepted","x":"Structural Pattern Matching Definition Structural pattern matching in Python (introduced in PEP 634 / Python 3.10) provides match and case statements capable of inspecting the shape, type, and contents of objects. When combined with typing utilities like typing.assertnever, static type checkers (mypy, pyright) verify that all possible variants of a union type are covered, achieving compile-time exhaustiveness guarantees in Python. python from typing import assertnever def handleresult(res: CommandResult) - str: match res: case Success(payload=text): return f\"Processed: {text}\" case Failure(errorcode=code, message=msg): return f\"Failed ({code}): {msg}\" case as unreachable: assertnever(unreachable) Why it exists Before Python 3.10, multi-way branch logic relied on chained if isinstance(x, A): ... elif isinstance(x, B): ... else: ... ladders. This approach lacked structural extraction and had no mechanism to verify whether all cases were handled. Adding a new variant to a data model would often pass silently into a default branch or fall through to None. Structural pattern matching realizes CONCEPT-prog-v1-003-01-exhaustive-branching: 1. Simultaneous check and extraction: A single case Success(payload=text) statement asserts the instance type and binds its internal fields into local variables. 2. Static exhaustiveness via assertnever: If a developer adds a third variant to CommandResult without adding a corresponding case, static type checkers flag the assertnever call as a type error because the unreachable branch received an unhandled variant. 3. Guard clauses: Allows fine-grained pattern guards (case Point(x=x, y=y) if x == y:) without messy nested conditionals."},{"t":"None-Aware Strict Typing","i":"CONCEPT-python-v1-004-01-none-aware-typing","h":"doc/catalogs/python/concepts/CONCEPT-python-v1-004-01-none-aware-typing.html","s":"accepted","x":"None-Aware Strict Typing Definition None-aware strict typing in Python uses the union syntax T None (replacing typing.Optional[T]) under strict static analysis configurations (mypy --strict or pyright). Under strict checking, any type T is non-nullable by default. Attempting to access an attribute or invoke a method on a variable typed as T None without first checking for None (via if x is not None:) triggers a static type analysis error. python def formatgreeting(username: str None) - str: return username.strip() Rejected: Item \"None\" of \"str None\" has no attribute \"strip\" if username is None: return \"Hello, Guest!\" return f\"Hello, {username.strip()}!\" Why it exists AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute '...' is the Python runtime equivalent of the Java NullPointerException. Because Python is dynamically typed, passing None into functions expecting an active object often fails many stack frames away from the original invocation, complicating debugging and root-cause analysis. None-aware typing realizes CONCEPT-prog-v1-004-01-null-safe-optionality: 1. Explicit API contracts: Functions explicitly signal whether None is acceptable as an input or possible as a return value. 2. Static verification without runtime penalty: Type checkers narrow the union within if x is not None: blocks (flow-sensitive typing) with zero runtime performance cost. 3. Contrast with Java and TypeScript: Java requires external checker plugins like NullAway with JSpecify annotations (CONCEPT-java-v1-019-01-jspecify-and-nullaway). TypeScript uses strictNullChecks (CONCEPT-ts-v1-004-01-strict-null-checks) separating null and undefined. Python unifies absent values under the singleton None."},{"t":"Python Context Manager","i":"CONCEPT-python-v1-005-01-context-manager","h":"doc/catalogs/python/concepts/CONCEPT-python-v1-005-01-context-manager.html","s":"accepted","x":"Python Context Manager Definition A context manager in Python manages runtime resource lifecycles through the with statement protocol. An object implementing enter() and exit() (or defined as a generator decorated with @contextlib.contextmanager) guarantees that its cleanup logic executes deterministically upon exiting the with code block, regardless of whether the block finishes normally or terminates via an exception. python from contextlib import contextmanager from typing import Generator @contextmanager def temporarytransaction() - Generator[str, None, None]: txid = \"tx-12345\" print(f\"Beginning transaction {txid}\") try: yield txid finally: print(f\"Rolling back / releasing transaction {txid}\") with temporarytransaction() as tx: print(f\"Executing work within {tx}\") Cleanup is guaranteed upon exiting the with block Why it exists Prior to PEP 343 and the with statement, resource management required manual try ... finally blocks. Missing or misordered finally statements led to resource leaks, file descriptor starvation, and unclosed network handles. Furthermore, CPython's reference-counting garbage collector often masked these leaks during local tests, only for them to emerge under alternative runtimes (PyPy, Jython) or cyclic reference structures. Context managers realize CONCEPT-prog-v1-005-01-scoped-resource-cleanup: 1. Guaranteed release: Encapsulates the try/finally pattern into reusable, composable constructs. 2. Exception interception: exit receives any unhandled exception and can inspect, log, or suppress it if designed to do so. 3. Contrast with Java: While Java's try-with-resources (CONCEPT-java-v1-012-01-try-with-resources-and-cleaner) requires types to implement AutoCloseable, Python's protocol allows both object methods (enter/exit) and generator-based yields via @contextmanager, providing flexible inline lifecycle management."},{"t":"Pytest Yield Fixture","i":"CONCEPT-python-v1-006-01-pytest-yield-fixture","h":"doc/catalogs/python/concepts/CONCEPT-python-v1-006-01-pytest-yield-fixture.html","s":"accepted","x":"Pytest Yield Fixture Definition A pytest yield fixture is a testing fixture function decorated with @pytest.fixture that yields a value or resource to the test. Code preceding the yield statement executes during test setup, while code following yield executes during test teardown. The teardown phase is guaranteed to run after the test function finishes, even if the test fails an assertion or raises an unexpected exception. python import pytest from pathlib import Path import tempfile import shutil from typing import Generator @pytest.fixture def tempworkspace() - Generator[Path, None, None]: path = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix=\"testws\")) try: yield path finally: shutil.rmtree(path, ignoreerrors=True) def testfilecreation(tempworkspace: Path) - None: target = tempworkspace / \"demo.txt\" target.writetext(\"sample content\") assert target.exists() Why it exists Traditional unittest.TestCase test suites decoupled setup (setUp) and teardown (tearDown) into disconnected class methods. This separation made it hard to track resource allocation, encouraged sharing mutable state across instance variables (self.ws), and caused teardown bugs if setup partially failed before initializing an instance attribute. The yield fixture realizes CONCEPT-prog-v1-006-01-scoped-test-fixture using Python's context management protocol (CONCEPT-python-v1-005-01-context-manager): 1. Colocated lifecycle: Setup and teardown for a given resource reside within a single coherent generator function. 2. Explicit parameter injection: Tests declare dependencies as function parameters, eliminating hidden coupling and global variables. 3. Flexible scoping: Fixtures can be configured with scopes (function, class, module, package, session) and composed hierarchically."},{"t":"TaskGroup and Executor Concurrency","i":"CONCEPT-python-v1-007-01-taskgroup-and-executor","h":"doc/catalogs/python/concepts/CONCEPT-python-v1-007-01-taskgroup-and-executor.html","s":"accepted","x":"TaskGroup and Executor Concurrency Definition In Python, structured concurrency is realized asynchronously via asyncio.TaskGroup (introduced in Python 3.11) and synchronously via concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor (or ProcessPoolExecutor). An asyncio.TaskGroup used as an asynchronous context manager spawns multiple coroutines, awaits their collective completion upon exiting the context, and cancels all remaining sibling tasks immediately if any child task raises an exception, grouping unhandled exceptions into an ExceptionGroup. python import asyncio async def fetchdata(sourceid: int) - str: await asyncio.sleep(0.01) return f\"data-{sourceid}\" async def main() - list[str]: results: list[str] = [] async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as tg: t1 = tg.createtask(fetchdata(1)) t2 = tg.createtask(fetchdata(2)) Context exits only after all tasks complete or an exception occurs return [t1.result(), t2.result()] Why it exists Before Python 3.11, asynchronous fan-out relied primarily on asyncio.gather() or asyncio.wait(). These functions did not cancel other tasks when one failed, frequently leaving orphaned coroutines running in the background. Furthermore, handling multiple exceptions required cumbersome custom filtering, often masking critical bugs. asyncio.TaskGroup realizes CONCEPT-prog-v1-007-01-parallel-fan-out: 1. Guaranteed task termination: The enclosing scope cannot terminate while background coroutines remain active. 2. Proactive cancellation: If one task fails, remaining tasks are promptly cancelled, conserving I/O and CPU resources. 3. Contrast with Java: While Java leverages lightweight virtual threads (CONCEPT-java-v1-009-01-virtual-threads) with StructuredTaskScope across blocking APIs, Python utilizes TaskGroup on top of an explicit cooperative event loop for I/O-bound concurrency, delegating CPU-bound work to ProcessPoolExecutor."},{"t":"Built-in Immutable Collections","i":"CONCEPT-python-v1-008-01-tuple-and-frozenset","h":"doc/catalogs/python/concepts/CONCEPT-python-v1-008-01-tuple-and-frozenset.html","s":"accepted","x":"Built-in Immutable Collections Definition Python provides first-class unmodifiable collections through built-in types and standard library utilities: tuple for ordered sequences, frozenset for unique unordered elements, and types.MappingProxyType for creating read-only proxy views over dictionaries. Once constructed, instances cannot have elements added, removed, or reassigned. python from types import MappingProxyType COORDINATES: tuple[int, ...] = (10, 20, 30) ALLOWEDACTIONS: frozenset[str] = frozenset({\"read\", \"write\", \"execute\"}) rawconfig = {\"env\": \"prod\", \"port\": 8080} APPCONFIG: MappingProxyType[str, object] = MappingProxyType(rawconfig) Why it exists In Python, passing mutable lists or dictionaries (list, dict, set) between functions frequently leads to unintended side effects when a downstream function mutates an argument in place. Module-level mutable variables (ROUTES = []) are also prone to race conditions and test pollution. Immutable built-in collections realize CONCEPT-prog-v1-008-01-immutable-collection-literal: 1. Hashable composites: Tuples and frozensets are hashable (provided their contained elements are hashable), allowing them to serve as cache keys and dictionary indexes. 2. Read-only encapsulation: MappingProxyType allows internal state to be exposed to external callers without copying the underlying dictionary. 3. Memory optimization: CPython optimizes tuples and frozensets with fixed memory layouts and smaller memory footprints compared to dynamic lists and sets."},{"t":"Callable and Protocol Interfaces","i":"CONCEPT-python-v1-009-01-callable-and-protocol","h":"doc/catalogs/python/concepts/CONCEPT-python-v1-009-01-callable-and-protocol.html","s":"accepted","x":"Callable and Protocol Interfaces Definition In Python, first-class functions and callables are statically typed using collections.abc.Callable and structural typing.Protocol interfaces. Functions can be passed as arguments, assigned to variables, returned from closures, or represented by objects implementing the call dunder method. Protocol enables structural subtyping (static duck typing), allowing higher-order functions to accept any callable or class matching a signature without explicit inheritance. python from collections.abc import Callable from typing import TypeVar T = TypeVar(\"T\") def retry(attempts: int, operation: Callable[..., T]) - T: lasterr: Exception None = None for in range(attempts): try: return operation() except Exception as e: lasterr = e if lasterr is not None: raise lasterr raise RuntimeError(\"Unreachable\") Why it exists Historically, Python relied entirely on dynamic runtime duck typing, meaning type mismatches in function arguments or callbacks were only detected when an invalid call was executed at runtime. Additionally, implementing interfaces required formal ABC inheritance, which introduced unwanted coupling and metaclass conflicts. Callable and Protocol realize CONCEPT-prog-v1-009-01-higher-order-function: 1. Accurate signature verification: Static type checkers verify argument and return types of callbacks and decorators without running code. 2. Structural decoupling: A callable or service object does not need to inherit from a shared base class; conforming to the protocol method signature is sufficient. 3. First-class composability: Enables decorators, middleware pipelines, and functional transformers with complete type safety."},{"t":"Environment Lookup with Fallback","i":"CONCEPT-python-v1-010-01-os-environ-get","h":"doc/catalogs/python/concepts/CONCEPT-python-v1-010-01-os-environ-get.html","s":"accepted","x":"Environment Lookup with Fallback Definition In Python, accessing process environment variables is accomplished via the standard os.environ mapping. The os.environ.get(key, default) method inspects the process environment for a specified key, returning the value as a string if present or falling back to a provided default without raising a KeyError. For strict configuration, os.environ[key] can be used to fail immediately when a required variable is missing. python import os from dataclasses import dataclass @dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True) class RuntimeConfig: port: int environment: str @classmethod def load(cls) - \"RuntimeConfig\": return cls( port=int(os.environ.get(\"PORT\", \"8080\")), environment=os.environ.get(\"APPENV\", \"development\"), ) Why it exists Directly reading raw environment variables across disparate modules causes subtle failures when keys are misspelled, missing, or have unexpected formats. Furthermore, unhandled KeyError exceptions occurring during normal request processing lead to unexpected crashes in production. os.environ.get realizes CONCEPT-prog-v1-010-01-external-environment-binding: 1. Deterministic fallbacks: Provides sensible local development defaults while allowing CI and production deployments to override values via external injection. 2. Centralized configuration mapping: Encourages loading and validating environment variables at application startup into typed data carriers. 3. Twelve-Factor alignment: Facilitates separation of code from configuration in accordance with containerized and cloud-native application architectures."},{"t":"Arrange-Act-Assert Pattern","i":"CONCEPT-qa-v1-001-01-arrange-act-assert","h":"doc/catalogs/qa/concepts/CONCEPT-qa-v1-001-01-arrange-act-assert.html","s":"accepted","x":"Arrange-Act-Assert Pattern Definition The Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) pattern is the canonical structural architecture for automated test cases. It partitions each test into three distinct, non-overlapping phases executed in strict chronological order: 1. Arrange: Set up all preconditions, system states, test data, mocks, and environment fixtures required for the scenario. 2. Act: Execute the single, specific behavior, user interaction, command, or method invocation being tested. 3. Assert: Verify that the actual outcomes, state mutations, and returned values match the expected results defined by the test oracle. This pattern is isomorphic to the Given-When-Then structure used in Behavior-Driven Development (BDD): Given the arranged state, When an action is taken, Then assert the observable outcome. python def testusercanpromotetickettotriage(boardfixture): 1. Arrange: Create initial preconditions and seed ticket in backlog ticket = boardfixture.createticket(title=\"Fix CSS layout\", status=\"BACKLOG\") 2. Act: Execute the single action under test ticket.promoteto(\"TRIAGE\") 3. Assert: Verify the postcondition state matches expectation updated = boardfixture.fetchticket(ticket.id) assert updated.status == \"TRIAGE\" assert updated.transitionhistory[-1].tostate == \"TRIAGE\" Why it exists The Arrange-Act-Assert pattern establishes structural discipline across automated test suites: 1. Failure Diagnosis Isolation: Clear separation pinpoints test failure etiology. A failure during the Arrange phase indicates broken test fixtures, stale data, or environment infrastructure issues. A failure during Act indicates interface or runtime execution errors. A failure during Assert indicates a functional defect or regression. 2. Single-Action Focus: Enforcing a single Act phase prevents tests from sprawling into multi-action integration odysseys where a failure in the middle leaves subsequent actions unexecuted and unverified. 3. Readability and Cognitive Load: Any engineer or reviewer reading the test can immediately identify the target behavior (the Act line) without getting lost in boilerplate setup code."},{"t":"The Locator Ladder","i":"CONCEPT-qa-v1-002-01-the-locator-ladder","h":"doc/catalogs/qa/concepts/CONCEPT-qa-v1-002-01-the-locator-ladder.html","s":"accepted","x":"The Locator Ladder Definition The Locator Ladder is an architectural priority hierarchy for selecting UI elements in web and mobile test automation. It orders element locator strategies from most resilient, semantic, and user-facing down to the most brittle and implementation-dependent: 1. Rung 1: User-Facing Accessibility Roles & Accessible Names (Highest Priority): Queries based on accessibility attributes (role=\"button\", name=\"Submit Order\"). Mirrors how assistive technologies and actual human users perceive and navigate the interface. 2. Rung 2: Semantic Labels and Visible Text: Queries based on form labels ( ), placeholder text, or prominent visible text. 3. Rung 3: Explicit Test IDs (data-testid): Dedicated testing attributes (data-testid=\"submit-checkout\") placed specifically for automation. Decoupled from CSS class styling and DOM restructuring, though invisible to real users. 4. Rung 4: CSS Selectors (Low Priority / Discouraged): Queries relying on style classes or structure (button.btn-primary.submit-v2). Fragile; frequently breaks during UI redesigns and CSS refactorings. 5. Rung 5: Structural XPath / DOM Index Paths (Lowest Priority / Strictly Prohibited): Hardcoded DOM hierarchy paths (/html/body/div[2]/div/form/button[1]). Extremely brittle; breaks whenever any ancestor container, wrapper div, or layout element changes. text +-----------------------------------------------------------+ Resilient, User-Facing, 1. Accessibility Role & Name: getbyrole(\"button\", ...) Verifies A11y +-----------------------------------------------------------+ 2. Label & Text: getbylabel(\"Username\") +-----------------------------------------------------------+ 3. Dedicated Test ID: getbytestid(\"submit-order\") Stable fallback +-----------------------------------------------------------+ 4. CSS Class Selectors: locator(\"button.btn-primary\") Brittle +-----------------------------------------------------------+ 5. Absolute XPath: xpath(\"/html/body/div[3]/button[1]\") Extremely fragile +-----------------------------------------------------------+ (PROHIBITED) python Preferred: Rung 1 (Accessibility Role and Name) page.getbyrole(\"button\", name=\"Submit Order\").click() Preferred Fallback: Rung 3 (Explicit Test ID for complex visual canvases) page.getbytestid(\"cart-total-badge\").click() Anti-pattern: Rung 4 & 5 (Brittle CSS / Absolute XPath) page.locator(\".css-1x84a .btn-wrap button:nth-child(2)\").click() BREAKS ON NEXT SPRINT Why it exists The Locator Ladder directly addresses the single largest cause of flakiness and maintenance overhead in UI test automation: 1. Decoupling Tests from Implementation Detail: Tests should verify what the user experiences, not the implementation trivia of CSS frameworks (like Tailwind or styled-components generated hashes). 2. Promoting Web Accessibility: By using accessibility roles and labels as the primary locator strategy, automated tests immediately fail if a UI component is inaccessible to screen readers, turning test automati"},{"t":"UI Synchronization and Actionability Waiting","i":"CONCEPT-qa-v1-003-01-synchronization-and-actionability","h":"doc/catalogs/qa/concepts/CONCEPT-qa-v1-003-01-synchronization-and-actionability.html","s":"accepted","x":"UI Synchronization and Actionability Waiting Definition UI Synchronization and Actionability Waiting is an automation architecture pattern that synchronizes test actions with dynamic, asynchronous browser execution without using arbitrary static sleep intervals (time.sleep() or Thread.sleep()). Before interacting with an element (e.g., clicking, typing, selecting), an automation engine must verify that the target element has reached an Actionable State: 1. Attached: The element is present in the Document Object Model (DOM). 2. Visible: The element has non-empty bounding box dimensions and lacks styles such as display: none or visibility: hidden. 3. Stable: The element has finished CSS transitions and animations and its coordinates are stationary. 4. Enabled: The element does not have the disabled attribute and is not blocked by a form fieldset. 5. Editable: For input fields, the element is not readonly or disabled. 6. Receives Events: The element is not obscured or occluded by overlaying modal backdrops, dialogs, or sticky banners. Rather than pausing for fixed durations, actionability engines poll the DOM and layout engine automatically until all conditions are satisfied or an explicit timeout expires. text Action Request: click(button) v +-------------------------------------------------------------+ Check Actionability Predicates: 1. Is attached to DOM? [Yes] 2. Is visible (bounding box 0)? [Yes] 3. Is stable (animations finished)? [Waiting... Yes] 4. Is enabled (not disabled)? [Yes] 5. Can receive pointer events (unmasked)?[Yes] +-------------------------------------------------------------+ +--- Dispatch Click Event python Bad Anti-pattern: Hardcoded sleep produces brittle or painfully slow tests import time time.sleep(5) Guessing: 5 seconds might be too short on CI, too long locally page.locator(\" submit-btn\").click() Good Archetype: Auto-waiting actionability synchronization The framework waits for attached, visible, stable, enabled, and unmasked state: page.getbyrole(\"button\", name=\"Submit\").click(timeout=5000) Why it exists Synchronization failures represent the leading source of intermittent flakiness in automated UI test suites: 1. Eliminating Race Conditions: Modern web applications render asynchronously via micro-tasks, client-side routing, and background HTTP fetches. A button may appear in the DOM milliseconds before its event handlers are wired or before its CSS animation finishes moving it under the cursor. 2. Maximizing Pipeline Speed: Hardcoded sleep statements accumulate across test suites. A suite of 500 tests with three 2-second sleeps each adds 50 minutes of dead waiting time to CI builds. Actionability checks proceed the exact millisecond the element is ready. 3. Self-Documenting Failure Diagnostics: When an actionability check times out, the framework reports the exact missing predicate (e.g., \"Element is visible but covered by \"), instantly highlighting the root cause."},{"t":"Session and Context Isolation","i":"CONCEPT-qa-v1-004-01-session-and-context-isolation","h":"doc/catalogs/qa/concepts/CONCEPT-qa-v1-004-01-session-and-context-isolation.html","s":"accepted","x":"Session and Context Isolation Definition Session and Context Isolation is an architectural discipline in test automation requiring every individual test case to execute inside a completely isolated, pristine runtime session without state leakage from preceding tests. In browser automation, this means creating an independent Browser Context (an isolated multi-page environment with its own in-memory cookies, local storage, session storage, service workers, and cache) for each test. The heavy browser binary process is launched once per worker process, while lightweight browser contexts are created and destroyed in milliseconds for each test. Isolation guarantees: Zero Cross-Test Pollution: Actions performed in Test A (e.g., logging in, setting cookies, populating indexedDB, filling cart) cannot contaminate or alter the initial state of Test B. Order Independence: Tests can execute in any permutation or order without changing pass/fail outcomes. Parallel Safety: Tests can run concurrently across dozens of worker threads and CPU cores against the same browser process without collision. text [Browser Process] (Heavyweight OS process, launched once per worker) +--- [Context 1] (Pristine Cookies, LocalStorage) --- [Page 1] (Test A) +--- [Context 2] (Pristine Cookies, LocalStorage) --- [Page 2] (Test B) +--- [Context 3] (Pristine Cookies, LocalStorage) --- [Page 3] (Test C) python import pytest @pytest.fixture def isolatedpage(browser): Create a brand new, isolated incognito browser context context = browser.newcontext() page = context.newpage() yield page Teardown: Instantly wipe all cookies, storage, and open tabs context.close() def testloginfirstuser(isolatedpage): isolatedpage.goto(\"/login\") ... executes with zero cached session def testguestuserflow(isolatedpage): isolatedpage.goto(\"/dashboard\") ... guaranteed not logged in as first user Why it exists Session isolation eliminates test pollution, which is the second most common cause of test suite flakiness: 1. Eradicating Mystery Failures: When tests share a browser session or user profile, a failure in Test 3 leaves the browser in an dirty state, causing Tests 4, 5, and 6 to fail as false positives (\"cascading failures\"). 2. Enabling High-Throughput Parallelism: Independent contexts allow test runners to execute 16 or 32 browser tests simultaneously on a single multi-core CI node with zero cross-talk. 3. Reproducibility: When a test fails in CI, a developer can run that single isolated test locally in isolation and reproduce the exact same state without needing to run an entire preceding test suite."},{"t":"Page Object Model and Screenplay Encapsulation","i":"CONCEPT-qa-v1-005-01-page-object-model","h":"doc/catalogs/qa/concepts/CONCEPT-qa-v1-005-01-page-object-model.html","s":"accepted","x":"Page Object Model and Screenplay Encapsulation Definition The Page Object Model (POM) and its evolution, the Screenplay Pattern, are architectural patterns that encapsulate the technical mechanics of interacting with web pages and UI components behind clean, business-oriented object interfaces. In the Page Object Model: UI Structure & Selectors: HTML elements, locators, and interaction protocols are encapsulated within dedicated Page or Component classes (e.g., LoginPage, NavigationHeader, CheckoutModal). Domain Intent Methods: Page objects expose high-level semantic methods representing user interactions (loginas(user), submitorder(), searchcatalog(query)). Assertions: Assertions generally remain inside the test scripts, or page objects expose state query methods and locators for assertions. The test case acts as a readable orchestrator of domain activities without mentioning raw DOM selectors. text +-----------------------------------------------------------------+ Test Case def testcheckout(checkoutpage): checkoutpage.fillshippingdetails(address) checkoutpage.submitpayment() expect(checkoutpage.confirmationheading).tobevisible() +-----------------------------------------------------------------+ v +-----------------------------------------------------------------+ Page Object (CheckoutPage) Encapsulates: getbyrole(\"button\", name=\"Pay\") Encapsulates: getbylabel(\"Street Address\") Exposes: fillshippingdetails(), submitpayment() +-----------------------------------------------------------------+ python class LoginPage: def init(self, page): self.page = page self.usernameinput = page.getbylabel(\"Username\") self.passwordinput = page.getbylabel(\"Password\") self.submitbutton = page.getbyrole(\"button\", name=\"Sign in\") self.errorbanner = page.getbyrole(\"alert\") def login(self, username, password): self.usernameinput.fill(username) self.passwordinput.fill(password) self.submitbutton.click() Test script consumes the clean domain interface: def testinvalidloginshowsalert(page): loginpage = LoginPage(page) page.goto(\"/login\") loginpage.login(\"alex\", \"wrong-password\") assert loginpage.errorbanner.isvisible() Why it exists The Page Object Model is the primary defense against UI test automation code duplication: 1. Single Source of Truth for Locators: When a button ID or accessibility label changes, updating the locator in the single Page Object class automatically fixes all 50 test cases that interact with that button. 2. Separation of Concerns: Test scripts focus on expressing business logic and verifying test oracles; Page Objects handle the underlying DOM traversal, synchronization, and driver mechanics. 3. Reusability and Composability: Page Objects and Component Objects can be composed into complex page hierarchies (e.g., embedding a NavbarComponent inside multiple pages)."},{"t":"Out-of-Band API State Seeding","i":"CONCEPT-qa-v1-006-01-api-fixture-seeding","h":"doc/catalogs/qa/concepts/CONCEPT-qa-v1-006-01-api-fixture-seeding.html","s":"accepted","x":"Out-of-Band API State Seeding Definition Out-of-Band API State Seeding is an architectural pattern for UI and end-to-end testing where preconditions and test data are created directly via backend REST/GraphQL APIs, database fixtures, or state injection endpoints, completely bypassing the user interface during the Arrange phase. In this pattern: Preconditions via API (Out-of-Band): Creating users, populating inventory, creating shopping carts, or setting permissions is executed via direct HTTP API requests or database scripts. This takes a few milliseconds. Session Injection: Authentication tokens or cookies are injected directly into the browser context (storageState), bypassing the multi-step UI login form. UI Reserved for the Act Phase: The browser UI is loaded only at the exact page and state required to exercise the specific interaction under test. text Anti-pattern (UI-only): [Open Browser] - [UI Register Form] - [UI Login Form] - [UI Catalog Search] - [UI Add to Cart] - [TEST CHECKOUT UI] (Slow: 35s, 5 potential failure points before test even starts) Archetype (Out-of-Band API Seeding): [POST /api/seed/cart] (15ms) - [Inject Auth Cookie] (2ms) - [Open /checkout] - [TEST CHECKOUT UI] (Fast: 1.2s, 0 UI flakiness in setup) python def testcheckoutsubmissionwithapiseeding(apiclient, browsercontext): Arrange (Out-of-Band): Seed state directly via backend APIs token = apiclient.createauthenticateduser(\"qauser@example.com\") orderid = apiclient.seedcartwithitems(usertoken=token, items=[\"SKU-1\", \"SKU-2\"]) Inject auth state directly into browser context browsercontext.addcookies([{ \"name\": \"sessiontoken\", \"value\": token, \"domain\": \"localhost\", \"path\": \"/\" }]) Act: Navigate directly to the checkout page under test page = browsercontext.newpage() page.goto(f\"/checkout/{orderid}\") page.getbyrole(\"button\", name=\"Place Order\").click() Assert assert page.getbytext(\"Order Confirmed\").isvisible() Why it exists Out-of-band state seeding is essential for scaling end-to-end test suites: 1. Massive Speedup: A test that sets up data through the UI takes 20 to 45 seconds; setting up the same data via direct API calls takes 50 to 200 milliseconds, resulting in a 10x-50x speedup of the test suite. 2. Defect Localization: If the login UI has a bug, a traditional suite of 200 tests will fail 200 times even though only one test was supposed to test login. With API seeding, only the single login test fails, while the other 199 tests seed their state out-of-band and continue testing their respective features. 3. Resilience Against UI Churn: Preconditions are shielded from front-end styling and form refactorings, making tests far more stable over time."},{"t":"Hard Assertions, Soft Assertions, and Auto-Retrying Assertions","i":"CONCEPT-qa-v1-007-01-hard-vs-soft-assertions","h":"doc/catalogs/qa/concepts/CONCEPT-qa-v1-007-01-hard-vs-soft-assertions.html","s":"accepted","x":"Hard Assertions, Soft Assertions, and Auto-Retrying Assertions Definition Automated test frameworks utilize three distinct assertion paradigms depending on execution context, criticality, and timing: 1. Hard Assertions: Standard assertions that throw an exception immediately upon the first condition failure, halting test execution instantly. Subsequent lines in the test case are never executed. Ideal for prerequisite validations where proceeding would be futile or cause cascading misleading errors. 2. Soft Assertions (Collected Assertions): Assertions that record validation failures in a collector without aborting test execution immediately. The test continues executing subsequent assertions, and all accumulated discrepancies are reported together at the end of the test. Ideal for verifying complex multi-field forms, tables, or dashboards. 3. Auto-Retrying Web-First Assertions: Dynamic assertions designed for asynchronous user interfaces. Instead of checking a condition once at a fixed instant, they repeatedly re-evaluate the expectation against the live DOM until it passes or a configurable timeout expires (e.g., expect(locator).tohavetext(...)). python 1. Hard Assertion: Stop immediately if login failed assert page.getbyrole(\"heading\", name=\"Dashboard\").isvisible(), \"Dashboard failed to load\" 2. Soft Assertion: Verify all user profile fields in one pass def testuserprofilefields(page, softassert): softassert.equal(page.getbytestid(\"first-name\").inputvalue(), \"Jane\") softassert.equal(page.getbytestid(\"last-name\").inputvalue(), \"Doe\") softassert.equal(page.getbytestid(\"email\").inputvalue(), \"jane@example.com\") softassert.assertall() Reports all mismatches at once 3. Auto-Retrying Web-First Assertion: Polls DOM until async API response renders from playwright.syncapi import expect expect(page.getbytestid(\"notification-toast\")).tohavetext(\"Profile Saved\", timeout=5000) Why it exists Matching the appropriate assertion paradigm to the verification task avoids common automation traps: 1. Avoiding the 1-Bug-Per-Run Feedback Loop: With hard assertions alone, verifying a form with 10 fields requires 10 full test runs if multiple fields are broken, because the test aborts on the first error. Soft assertions capture all 10 discrepancies in a single execution. 2. Eliminating Race Conditions in UI Tests: Checking assert element.isvisible() synchronously will fail if the front-end takes 50 milliseconds to finish rendering. Auto-retrying assertions eliminate timing race conditions by polling smoothly until the expected condition is met. 3. Failing Fast on Fatal Preconditions: Hard assertions remain essential when a failure renders further test steps meaningless (e.g., if navigating to the page resulted in a 500 Internal Server Error)."},{"t":"The Failure Funnel: Diagnostic Artifacts","i":"CONCEPT-qa-v1-008-01-failure-funnel-and-artifacts","h":"doc/catalogs/qa/concepts/CONCEPT-qa-v1-008-01-failure-funnel-and-artifacts.html","s":"accepted","x":"The Failure Funnel: Diagnostic Artifacts Definition The Failure Funnel is an automated test infrastructure pattern that progressively captures multi-tiered diagnostic artifacts upon any test failure or timeout, assembling a complete forensic record without requiring manual re-runs. The forensic tiers captured in the funnel include: 1. Console & Runner Logs: Standard out, standard error, browser console messages (console.error, unhandled exceptions), and test framework step logs. 2. Failure Screenshot: A visual raster image (PNG) of the exact viewport state at the instant of failure, revealing modal overlays, clipping, or visual errors. 3. DOM Snapshot: The complete serialized HTML tree at the failure point, enabling inspection of hidden attributes, CSS classes, and aria roles. 4. Network Archive (HAR): A full recording of all HTTP requests, responses, status codes, response payloads, headers, and timing metrics sent or received by the browser. 5. Execution Trace (Timeline Archive): A bundled zip containing millisecond-by-millisecond screencasts, DOM snapshots before and after every action, network events, and call stacks, viewable interactively in a trace viewer. text [ Test Execution Passes ] -------- Discard heavy artifacts (keep minimal logs) +--- [ TEST FAILS ] v +-----------------------------+ The Failure Funnel +-----------------------------+ 1. Capture Console Logs 2. Capture Viewport PNG 3. Dump Serialized DOM 4. Export Network HAR 5. Finalize Execution Trace +-----------------------------+ v [ Attached to CI Run / Defect ] python Fixture capturing forensic artifacts only on test failure import pytest @pytest.fixture def pagewithfailurefunnel(browsercontext, request): Start tracing with screenshots and DOM snapshots browsercontext.tracing.start(screenshots=True, snapshots=True, sources=True) page = browsercontext.newpage() yield page Teardown: inspect test execution result if request.node.repcall.failed: testid = request.node.name page.screenshot(path=f\"artifacts/{testid}failure.png\") browsercontext.tracing.stop(path=f\"artifacts/{testid}trace.zip\") else: Discard trace on success to conserve storage and bandwidth browsercontext.tracing.stop() Why it exists The Failure Funnel eliminates the ambiguity and lost time associated with headless CI test failures: 1. Root-Cause Analysis Without Reproduction: In headless CI environments, developers cannot watch tests execute. Having full network logs, DOM state, and timeline traces allows engineers to diagnose the defect immediately without attempting tricky local reproductions. 2. Distinguishing App Bugs from Infrastructure Drops: A network HAR immediately clarifies whether an assertion failed because of a front-end bug or because an internal microservice returned a 504 Gateway Timeout. 3. Storage Efficiency: Heavy artifacts (video recordings and full traces) are captured conditionally only when tests fail, keeping CI storage and artifact upload bandwidth lean."},{"t":"Deterministic Execution, Flakiness Quarantine, and Retry Discipline","i":"CONCEPT-qa-v1-009-01-deterministic-replay-and-flakiness","h":"doc/catalogs/qa/concepts/CONCEPT-qa-v1-009-01-deterministic-replay-and-flakiness.html","s":"accepted","x":"Deterministic Execution, Flakiness Quarantine, and Retry Discipline Definition Deterministic Execution, Flakiness Quarantine, and Retry Discipline is a reliability governance architecture designed to eliminate intermittent non-deterministic test results (\"flakiness\") and maintain developer trust in automated test suites: 1. Deterministic Execution: Designing tests and test environments such that given the exact same source code commit, the test produces the identical pass or fail outcome 100% of the time. Non-deterministic sources (random data, system clock/timezones, animation timers, network latency, unordered async events) are mocked, seeded, or explicitly synchronized. 2. Strict Retry Discipline: Automated test retries (e.g., retrying a failing test up to 2 times in CI) are used solely as a temporary operational shock-absorber for deployment pipelines. A test that passes on retry is explicitly flagged as Flaky in telemetry, not as a normal clean pass. Retries must never be used to mask or normalize defects. 3. Flakiness Quarantine: Any test exhibiting non-deterministic behavior above a strict threshold is automatically or immediately moved into a non-blocking Quarantine suite. The test continues running to collect diagnostic data, but cannot block pull request merges until its root cause is diagnosed, fixed, and verified over multiple consecutive clean runs. text Test Fails on Run 1 v [ Retry Execution (Run 2) ] +---- Passes? ---- Mark as FLAKY (Record Metric, Alert Team) -- If Flakiness 2%: Move to Quarantine Suite +---- Fails? ---- Mark as TRUE FAILURE (Block PR) python Flaky test quarantined out of the blocking PR pipeline import pytest @pytest.mark.quarantine(reason=\"Flaky race condition on payment webhook, DEF-1082\") def testasyncwebhookpaymentconfirmation(page): page.goto(\"/checkout\") ... Why it exists Test flakiness is toxic to engineering organizations: 1. The \"Cry Wolf\" Effect: When automated tests fail intermittently due to flakiness, engineers begin ignoring CI test failures and re-running failed builds repeatedly, eventually allowing real defects and regressions into production. 2. Protecting CI Velocity: Quarantining flaky tests protects pull request throughput and prevents blocked developer pipelines while investigations proceed. 3. Accountability via Telemetry: Treating flakiness as a defect with dedicated metrics ensures that timing issues, unhandled asynchronous events, and fragile locators receive engineering attention rather than being swept under the rug of blind retries."},{"t":"Model-Based Testing and Seeded Random State-Machine Walk","i":"CONCEPT-qa-v1-010-01-model-based-seeded-walk","h":"doc/catalogs/qa/concepts/CONCEPT-qa-v1-010-01-model-based-seeded-walk.html","s":"accepted","x":"Model-Based Testing and Seeded Random State-Machine Walk Definition Model-Based Testing (MBT) with Seeded Random State-Machine Walks is an automated test generation architecture where tests are derived automatically from a formal behavioral model of the system under test (such as a directed state machine or labeled transition graph). Instead of executing static, hand-written linear scripts, a test generator traverses the state graph using a pseudo-random walk algorithm initialized with a deterministic integer seed: State Graph Model: Nodes represent system states and views; edges represent user actions, API calls, or events, annotated with preconditions, actions, and expected assertions. Seeded Walk Engine: Given a fixed seed (e.g., seed = 48291), the generator computes a deterministic sequence of transitions across the model, asserting state invariants at each step. Coverage Tracking: The engine records visited states, transitions, and combinations (0-switch, 1-switch coverage), terminating when coverage goals or step budgets are reached. Deterministic Replay: If a walk encounters an assertion failure or crash at step 42, running the walk with the exact same seed (--seed 48291) reproduces the identical path and failure every time. text +--------------+ viewcart() +-----------+ +------ CATALOGVIEW -------------- CARTVIEW +--------------+ +-----------+ additem() search() checkout() v v +------- +--------------+ +-----------+ SEARCHVIEW PAYMENT +--------------+ +-----------+ ^ +------- [Seeded Random Walk] --+ python import random class SeededModelWalker: def init(self, model, seed: int): self.model = model self.rng = random.Random(seed) self.path = [] def executewalk(self, maxsteps: int = 50): currentstate = self.model.initialstate() for step in range(maxsteps): availabletransitions = self.model.getvalidtransitions(currentstate) if not availabletransitions: break Deterministic selection based on seed RNG chosentransition = self.rng.choice(availabletransitions) self.path.append(chosentransition.name) currentstate = chosentransition.executeandverify() Why it exists Seeded model walks solve the fundamental tension between exploring new paths and maintaining test determinism: 1. Defeating the Pesticide Paradox: Hand-written tests execute the exact same happy-path sequence on every run, missing subtle bugs triggered by unorthodox navigation flows (e.g., adding an item, searching, deleting an item, pressing back, then checking out). Seeded walks explore diverse, complex permutations that human authors rarely write. 2. Guaranteed 100% Reproducibility: Unlike unseeded chaos monkeys or naive fuzzers whose bugs are notoriously hard to reproduce, a seeded walk failure is 100% reproducible simply by passing the failing seed number. 3. High Return on Investment: Writing a single state machine model enables the generation of hundreds of unique test journeys and automated edge-case exploration with zero additional test authoring overhead."},{"t":"WebDriver Session Lifecycle and Selenium Manager","i":"CONCEPT-selenium-v1-001-01-webdriver-session-lifecycle","h":"doc/catalogs/selenium/concepts/CONCEPT-selenium-v1-001-01-webdriver-session-lifecycle.html","s":"accepted","x":"WebDriver Session Lifecycle and Selenium Manager Definition In the W3C WebDriver specification implemented by Selenium, a browser session corresponds to an independent browser instance controlled over HTTP via the W3C WebDriver wire protocol. Starting in Selenium 4.6+, SeleniumManager automatically resolves, downloads, and configures matching browser driver binaries (such as chromedriver, geckodriver, or msedgedriver) at runtime, removing the need for manual driver path setup or external managers. A session lifecycle follows three explicit phases: 1. Creation: new ChromeDriver(options) negotiates capabilities with the browser driver process and launches an OS browser process. 2. Interaction: Commands are dispatched as standard W3C HTTP wire protocol requests. 3. Termination: driver.quit() dispatches a DELETE /session/{sessionid} command to kill the driver and close all associated browser windows and processes. java import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver; import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver; import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeOptions; public class SessionLifecycleDemo { public static void runTest() { // SeleniumManager automatically locates and launches the matching driver binary ChromeOptions options = new ChromeOptions(); options.addArguments(\"--headless=new\"); WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver(options); try { driver.get(\"https://app.example.com\"); System.out.println(\"Page title: \" + driver.getTitle()); } finally { // Explicitly terminates the W3C session and kills OS browser processes driver.quit(); } } } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-004-01-session-and-context-isolation. Ensuring that test executions execute in pristine environments with isolated cookies and storage is essential for test independence. Contrasts with CONCEPT-playwright-v1-001-01-browser-context-page-hierarchy. In Selenium, session isolation is tightly coupled to the OS process lifecycle: each new ChromeDriver() call spawns an entire browser process, which incurs significant CPU and memory overhead (often 1 to 3 seconds per test startup). In contrast, Playwright decouples the browser process from lightweight incognito BrowserContext instances, allowing fresh isolation in milliseconds without relaunching the host browser binary."},{"t":"By Locators, WebElement, and Stale Element References","i":"CONCEPT-selenium-v1-002-01-by-locators-and-webelement","h":"doc/catalogs/selenium/concepts/CONCEPT-selenium-v1-002-01-by-locators-and-webelement.html","s":"accepted","x":"By Locators, WebElement, and Stale Element References Definition Selenium identifies elements using By query strategies (By.id, By.name, By.cssSelector, By.xpath, By.linkText) passed to driver.findElement(by). Selenium 4 also introduces RelativeLocator (with(by).above(...), .below(...), .toLeftOf(...), .toRightOf(...), .near(...)). Calling findElement executes an eager DOM lookup and returns a WebElement containing an opaque node ID. If the DOM mutates, re-renders, or replaces the referenced node before an action is invoked on that WebElement, the reference becomes invalid, throwing StaleElementReferenceException. java import org.openqa.selenium.By; import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver; import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement; import org.openqa.selenium.support.locators.RelativeLocator; public class LocatorDemo { public static void submitForm(WebDriver driver) { // Eager DOM lookup returning a specific node reference WebElement emailInput = driver.findElement(By.id(\"user-email\")); emailInput.sendKeys(\"user@example.com\"); // Selenium 4 Relative Locator relative to another element By submitBy = RelativeLocator.with(By.tagName(\"button\")).below(emailInput); WebElement submitButton = driver.findElement(submitBy); // If the page dynamically re-rendered submitButton, this call throws StaleElementReferenceException submitButton.click(); } } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-002-01-the-locator-ladder. Structural By mechanisms allow navigating document trees across any browser supporting the W3C WebDriver standard. Contrasts with CONCEPT-playwright-v1-002-01-semantic-locators. In Selenium, WebElement represents an eager snapshot reference to a DOM node; it does not automatically re-query the DOM upon subsequent calls, necessitating manual retry logic or PageFactory proxies to recover from staleness. Furthermore, driver.findElement silently returns the first matching element if a locator matches multiple nodes, whereas Playwright's lazy locators enforce strict single-element resolution and re-evaluate dynamically at action dispatch time."},{"t":"Explicit WebDriverWait and ExpectedConditions","i":"CONCEPT-selenium-v1-003-01-webdriverwait-and-expected-conditions","h":"doc/catalogs/selenium/concepts/CONCEPT-selenium-v1-003-01-webdriverwait-and-expected-conditions.html","s":"accepted","x":"Explicit WebDriverWait and ExpectedConditions Definition Synchronization in Selenium WebDriver relies on explicit polling via WebDriverWait and predefined ExpectedConditions (or lambda predicates). WebDriverWait repeatedly evaluates an expected condition over a configured timeout (e.g., 10 seconds) with a defined polling frequency (typically 500ms), ignoring specific exceptions such as NoSuchElementException until the condition evaluates to a truthy value or the timeout is exceeded. Mixing implicit waits (driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(...)) with explicit waits is an established anti-pattern in Selenium: because both mechanisms poll the browser driver, the W3C specification does not define an order of precedence, causing unpredictable and compounded timeout delays. java import java.time.Duration; import org.openqa.selenium.By; import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver; import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement; import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.ExpectedConditions; import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.WebDriverWait; public class SyncDemo { public static void submitOrder(WebDriver driver) { WebDriverWait wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10)); // Explicitly wait until the button is both visible and enabled for interaction By submitLocator = By.cssSelector(\"button confirm-checkout\"); WebElement submitButton = wait.until(ExpectedConditions.elementToBeClickable(submitLocator)); submitButton.click(); // Wait until success banner appears in the DOM and becomes visible wait.until(ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfElementLocated(By.className(\"success-message\"))); } } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-003-01-synchronization-and-actionability. Web browsers render asynchronously; attempting to interact with elements before they are attached, visible, or enabled causes immediate runtime errors. Contrasts with CONCEPT-playwright-v1-003-01-auto-waiting-and-actionability. In Selenium, actions (click(), sendKeys()) do not auto-wait for interactability checks; if an element is animating or obscured, click() immediately fails with ElementClickInterceptedException. Consequently, Selenium test authors must explicitly write wait.until(...) calls before interacting with elements. In contrast, Playwright automatically performs actionability checks prior to every action, eliminating manual synchronization boilerplate."},{"t":"One-Shot DOM Assertions","i":"CONCEPT-selenium-v1-004-01-one-shot-dom-assertions","h":"doc/catalogs/selenium/concepts/CONCEPT-selenium-v1-004-01-one-shot-dom-assertions.html","s":"accepted","x":"One-Shot DOM Assertions Definition Selenium WebDriver does not include a native assertion framework. Instead, test suites pair Selenium with test runners (such as JUnit or TestNG) or assertion libraries (such as AssertJ). In this pattern, assertions are \"one-shot\": they inspect a snapshot of DOM state at a single moment in time. To avoid race conditions, test authors must first explicitly synchronize the browser state via WebDriverWait before evaluating the one-shot assertion. java import java.time.Duration; import org.openqa.selenium.By; import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver; import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement; import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.ExpectedConditions; import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.WebDriverWait; import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals; public class AssertionDemo { public static void verifyHeader(WebDriver driver) { WebDriverWait wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(5)); // 1. Explicit wait pre-synchronizes until expected text is populated By headerLocator = By.tagName(\"h1\"); wait.until(ExpectedConditions.textToBePresentInElementLocated(headerLocator, \"Dashboard\")); // 2. One-shot runner assertion evaluates static string snapshot WebElement header = driver.findElement(headerLocator); assertEquals(\"Dashboard\", header.getText()); } } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-007-01-hard-vs-soft-assertions. Clear separation of concerns between browser automation driver and test runner assertions has historically allowed WebDriver to remain language- and framework-agnostic. Contrasts with CONCEPT-playwright-v1-004-01-web-first-assertions. One-shot assertions evaluate immediately without polling. If an assertion runs before client-side hydration or rendering completes, it fails immediately even if the state settles milliseconds later. In Selenium, avoiding this flakiness requires manually combining wait.until(...) conditions with separate assertion lines. Playwright resolves this duality by embedding retrying polling logic directly into web-first assertions (expect(locator).toHaveText(...))."},{"t":"External HTTP Seeding and Cookie Injection","i":"CONCEPT-selenium-v1-005-01-external-http-seeding","h":"doc/catalogs/selenium/concepts/CONCEPT-selenium-v1-005-01-external-http-seeding.html","s":"accepted","x":"External HTTP Seeding and Cookie Injection Definition Because Selenium WebDriver controls only browser interactions and provides no built-in HTTP client for raw network requests, state seeding requires pairing Selenium with an external HTTP library (such as Java 11's HttpClient, Apache HttpClient, or OkHttp). The external HTTP client seeds test entities or authenticates user accounts over fast REST/GraphQL endpoints. To bridge the resulting authentication state to the browser, the driver navigates to the target domain, and cookies are injected into the active session via driver.manage().addCookie(...). java import java.net.URI; import java.net.http.HttpClient; import java.net.http.HttpRequest; import java.net.http.HttpResponse; import org.openqa.selenium.Cookie; import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver; public class HttpSeedingDemo { public static void seedAndInject(WebDriver driver) throws Exception { // 1. External HTTP client authenticates out-of-band HttpClient client = HttpClient.newHttpClient(); HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder() .uri(URI.create(\"https://app.example.com/api/login\")) .POST(HttpRequest.BodyPublishers.ofString(\"{ \"user \": \"test \", \"pass \": \"secret \"}\")) .header(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\") .build(); HttpResponse response = client.send(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString()); // 2. WebDriver must navigate to the domain before cookies can be added driver.get(\"https://app.example.com/404\"); // 3. Inject authenticated session token into the browser session driver.manage().addCookie(new Cookie(\"sessionid\", \"auth-token-xyz\", \".example.com\", \"/\", null)); // 4. Open authenticated page directly driver.get(\"https://app.example.com/dashboard\"); } } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-006-01-api-fixture-seeding. Setting up complex database records, user permissions, or multi-item carts via the browser UI is time-consuming and introduces unnecessary failure points. Seeding data via HTTP endpoints enables UI tests to focus purely on the core interaction under test. Contrasts with CONCEPT-playwright-v1-005-01-api-request-context. In Selenium, bridging HTTP client state to the browser is a manual, multi-step process: the browser must first load a page on the domain (to avoid cross-domain cookie exceptions) before addCookie can succeed. Playwright provides unified APIRequestContext instances that can share storage state and cookie jars with browser contexts automatically without intermediate dummy navigations."},{"t":"Failure Diagnostics via TakesScreenshot and BiDi Logs","i":"CONCEPT-selenium-v1-006-01-takes-screenshot-and-logs","h":"doc/catalogs/selenium/concepts/CONCEPT-selenium-v1-006-01-takes-screenshot-and-logs.html","s":"accepted","x":"Failure Diagnostics via TakesScreenshot and BiDi Logs Definition In Selenium WebDriver, diagnosing test failures relies on extracting point-in-time runtime artifacts upon test failure: Screenshots: Cast the WebDriver instance to TakesScreenshot and call getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE) to capture a PNG image of the current viewport. DOM Snapshot: Call driver.getPageSource() to extract the raw HTML string as rendered at the instant of failure. BiDi / CDP Console Logs: In Selenium 4, register listeners via driver.manage().logs() or BiDi log listeners (LogInspector.onConsoleEntry(...)) to capture JavaScript console warnings and uncaught exceptions. java import java.io.File; import java.nio.file.Files; import java.nio.file.Paths; import org.openqa.selenium.OutputType; import org.openqa.selenium.TakesScreenshot; import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver; public class DiagnosticArtifactsDemo { public static void captureFailureState(WebDriver driver, String testName) { try { // 1. Capture viewport screenshot as PNG File screenshotFile = ((TakesScreenshot) driver).getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE); Files.copy(screenshotFile.toPath(), Paths.get(\"target/artifacts/\" + testName + \".png\")); // 2. Dump raw DOM source String domHtml = driver.getPageSource(); Files.writeString(Paths.get(\"target/artifacts/\" + testName + \".html\"), domHtml); } catch (Exception e) { System.err.println(\"Failed to capture diagnostics: \" + e.getMessage()); } } } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-008-01-failure-funnel-and-artifacts. When automated tests fail on unattended CI runners, static stack traces alone are often insufficient to discern whether a failure stemmed from an unexpected modal dialog, a 500 server error page, or an unrendered UI component. Contrasts with CONCEPT-playwright-v1-006-01-trace-viewer-and-artifacts. Selenium artifacts are strictly post-mortem and disjointed: a static screenshot and text HTML dump captured after the failure already occurred. They do not retain the temporal history of interactions, DOM mutations, or network requests leading up to the failure. In contrast, Playwright's Trace Viewer records a comprehensive step-by-step interactive timeline with full DOM states before and after each action, complete network waterfalls, and console outputs in a single trace package."},{"t":"W3C WebDriver BiDi Network Interception","i":"CONCEPT-selenium-v1-007-01-bidi-network-interception","h":"doc/catalogs/selenium/concepts/CONCEPT-selenium-v1-007-01-bidi-network-interception.html","s":"accepted","x":"W3C WebDriver BiDi Network Interception Definition Selenium 4 introduces bidirectional communication via the emerging W3C WebDriver BiDi standard (and Chrome DevTools Protocol / CDP fallback). Through the NetworkInterceptor or HasAuthentication interfaces, tests can intercept HTTP traffic at the browser engine level, register route filters, authenticate via HTTP Basic Auth dialogs, and substitute canned mock responses for specific network requests. java import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver; import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver; import org.openqa.selenium.devtools.NetworkInterceptor; import org.openqa.selenium.remote.http.HttpResponse; import org.openqa.selenium.remote.http.Route; import static org.openqa.selenium.remote.http.Contents.utf8String; public class BiDiNetworkDemo { public static void interceptAndMock(WebDriver driver) { // Intercept network requests matching route and fulfill with mock JSON Route mockApiRoute = Route.matching(req - req.getUri().contains(\"/api/user\")) .to(() - req - new HttpResponse() .setStatus(200) .addHeader(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\") .setContent(utf8String(\"{ \"id \":1, \"name \": \"Mock User \"}\"))); try (NetworkInterceptor ignored = new NetworkInterceptor(driver, mockApiRoute)) { driver.get(\"https://app.example.com/profile\"); // The browser receives the mocked user profile data } } } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-009-01-deterministic-replay-and-flakiness. E2E tests often flake due to external services, transient network hiccups, or rate-limited APIs. Intercepting and stubbing network responses at the browser boundary isolates frontend testing from volatile external backend dependencies. Contrasts with CONCEPT-playwright-v1-007-01-network-interception-route. In earlier Selenium versions (Selenium 2 and 3), network interception was impossible without heavy out-of-process reverse proxies (such as BrowserMob Proxy). Selenium 4's NetworkInterceptor introduces this capability via BiDi/CDP, but still operates as a specialized decorator wrapper around WebDriver. Playwright integrates network routing directly into the core page.route / context.route API as a first-class feature across all supported browsers."},{"t":"Explicit Context Switching for Frames and Shadow DOM","i":"CONCEPT-selenium-v1-008-01-switchto-frame-and-shadow-root","h":"doc/catalogs/selenium/concepts/CONCEPT-selenium-v1-008-01-switchto-frame-and-shadow-root.html","s":"accepted","x":"Explicit Context Switching for Frames and Shadow DOM Definition In Selenium WebDriver, navigating past DOM encapsulation boundaries requires stateful context switches: Frames: To interact with elements inside an , tests must invoke driver.switchTo().frame(...) (by index, name, or WebElement). Once switched, all driver calls operate exclusively inside the frame context. To return to the top-level document, tests must explicitly call driver.switchTo().defaultContent(). Shadow DOM: Selenium 4 adds element.getShadowRoot() returning a SearchContext. Elements enclosed inside open Shadow DOM trees must be explicitly queried from that SearchContext. java import org.openqa.selenium.By; import org.openqa.selenium.SearchContext; import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver; import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement; public class EncapsulationDemo { public static void interactAcrossBoundaries(WebDriver driver) { // 1. Frame switching: stateful switch into iframe WebElement frameElement = driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"iframe checkout-frame\")); driver.switchTo().frame(frameElement); // Action performed inside frame driver.findElement(By.id(\"card-number\")).sendKeys(\"4242424242424242\"); // Explicitly restore driver context back to top-level window driver.switchTo().defaultContent(); // 2. Shadow DOM: acquire shadow root SearchContext WebElement customComponent = driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"user-badge\")); SearchContext shadowRoot = customComponent.getShadowRoot(); WebElement internalLabel = shadowRoot.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"span.status-text\")); System.out.println(\"Status: \" + internalLabel.getText()); } } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-002-01-the-locator-ladder. Encapsulation boundaries isolate DOM subtrees, styles, and document contexts in modern web applications. Automation tools must provide deterministic access mechanisms to reach components nested within frames and web components. Contrasts with CONCEPT-playwright-v1-009-01-frame-and-shadow-dom-piercing. Selenium's context switching is strictly stateful and imperative: forgetting to call driver.switchTo().defaultContent() causes all subsequent lookups to fail with NoSuchElementException. In contrast, Playwright automatically pierces open Shadow DOM roots in all standard locators by default and uses stateless, declarative page.frameLocator(...) chains that never mutate driver session state."},{"t":"Low-Level Actions API for Complex Interactions","i":"CONCEPT-selenium-v1-009-01-actions-api","h":"doc/catalogs/selenium/concepts/CONCEPT-selenium-v1-009-01-actions-api.html","s":"accepted","x":"Low-Level Actions API for Complex Interactions Definition Selenium's Actions class provides an interface for synthesizing low-level W3C input events (pointer, keyboard, and wheel gestures). Unlike high-level convenience methods (element.click(), element.sendKeys()), Actions allows chaining precise discrete physical actions: moving the pointer to element coordinates, pressing and releasing specific mouse buttons, holding keyboard modifiers (Keys.SHIFT, Keys.CONTROL), dragging elements across coordinates, and scrolling scrollable containers. Calling .perform() compiles the sequence into W3C input action payloads and dispatches them across the wire protocol. java import org.openqa.selenium.By; import org.openqa.selenium.Keys; import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver; import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement; import org.openqa.selenium.interactions.Actions; public class ActionsApiDemo { public static void dragAndDropWithModifier(WebDriver driver) { WebElement sourceItem = driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\".draggable-card\")); WebElement targetDropZone = driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\".kanban-column\")); // Build a composite gesture sequence: hover, hold Ctrl, drag, and release new Actions(driver) .moveToElement(sourceItem) .keyDown(Keys.CONTROL) .clickAndHold(sourceItem) .moveToElement(targetDropZone) .release() .keyUp(Keys.CONTROL) .perform(); } } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-003-01-synchronization-and-actionability. Rich client-side web interfaces frequently rely on complex gestures—such as drag-and-drop file upload zones, interactive canvas drawing, custom slider controls, and multi-select lists using modifier keys—that cannot be triggered by basic click or text-entry primitives. The Actions API adheres to the W3C WebDriver Input specification, translating composite user interactions into faithful low-level OS-like input sequences across all conforming browser implementations."},{"t":"RemoteWebDriver and Distributed Selenium Grid","i":"CONCEPT-selenium-v1-010-01-remote-webdriver-and-grid","h":"doc/catalogs/selenium/concepts/CONCEPT-selenium-v1-010-01-remote-webdriver-and-grid.html","s":"accepted","x":"RemoteWebDriver and Distributed Selenium Grid Definition Selenium's RemoteWebDriver decouples the test runner executing the automation code from the physical machine or container hosting the browser binary. By passing the URL of a Selenium Grid Hub (or cloud provider endpoint) alongside Capabilities or ChromeOptions, RemoteWebDriver serializes all W3C commands over HTTP to the grid router. Selenium Grid 4 distributes incoming session requests across registered worker nodes based on requested browser types, platform architectures, and version constraints. java import java.net.URI; import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver; import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeOptions; import org.openqa.selenium.remote.RemoteWebDriver; public class RemoteGridDemo { public static void executeOnGrid() throws Exception { ChromeOptions options = new ChromeOptions(); options.setBrowserVersion(\"120\"); options.setPlatformName(\"Linux\"); // Connect to distributed Selenium Grid router endpoint WebDriver driver = new RemoteWebDriver( URI.create(\"http://selenium-grid.internal:4444\").toURL(), options ); try { driver.get(\"https://app.example.com\"); System.out.println(\"Title: \" + driver.getTitle()); } finally { driver.quit(); } } } Why it exists Realizes CONCEPT-qa-v1-004-01-session-and-context-isolation. Enterprise test suites often require execution against heterogeneous environments—different operating systems (Windows, macOS, Linux), specific browser versions, or scaled ephemeral Docker clusters—that cannot all run locally on a developer workstation or single CI agent. RemoteWebDriver establishes an open network protocol layer, allowing test suites to scale out horizontally across massive distributed server farms while maintaining identical client-side test code."},{"t":"Readonly Interface and Const Assertion Carrier","i":"CONCEPT-ts-v1-001-01-readonly-interface-and-type","h":"doc/catalogs/ts/concepts/CONCEPT-ts-v1-001-01-readonly-interface-and-type.html","s":"accepted","x":"Readonly Interface and Const Assertion Carrier Definition In TypeScript, immutable data carriers are declared using the readonly property modifier, the Readonly utility type, and as const type assertions (introduced in TypeScript 3.4). Marked properties cannot be reassigned after initialization. When applied to object literals, as const recursively marks all properties as readonly and narrows primitive types to literal types. ts export interface UserProfile { readonly id: string; readonly email: string; readonly loginCount: number; } export function createOrigin() { return { x: 0, y: 0 } as const; // Type: { readonly x: 0; readonly y: 0 } } const user: UserProfile = { id: \"u-101\", email: \"dev@example.com\", loginCount: 3 }; // user.loginCount = 4; // Compile error: Cannot assign to 'loginCount' because it is a read-only property. Why it exists In JavaScript and unannotated TypeScript, object properties are mutable by default. Mutating object instances across asynchronous tasks or function pipelines introduces race conditions and aliasing bugs that are difficult to locate. Readonly interfaces and as const realize CONCEPT-prog-v1-001-01-immutable-data-carrier: 1. Compile-time enforcement with zero runtime cost: Immutability is verified statically by the TypeScript compiler; no runtime wrapper code or performance overhead is emitted into the JavaScript output. 2. Deep const assertions: Appending as const automatically applies deep readonly constraints and preserves exact literal types without declaring explicit types for every nested field. 3. Contrast with Java and Python: Unlike Java records (CONCEPT-java-v1-002-01-records) which enforce final fields in JVM bytecode, or Python frozen dataclasses (CONCEPT-python-v1-001-01-frozen-dataclass) which raise FrozenInstanceError at runtime, TypeScript's readonly checks are erased at compile time, providing compile-time type safety while emitting lightweight JavaScript objects."},{"t":"Discriminated Union Type","i":"CONCEPT-ts-v1-002-01-discriminated-union","h":"doc/catalogs/ts/concepts/CONCEPT-ts-v1-002-01-discriminated-union.html","s":"accepted","x":"Discriminated Union Type Definition A discriminated union (or tagged union) in TypeScript is a union type where each member type shares a common singleton discriminant property (such as kind: 'circle' or type: 'success'). When code checks the value of this discriminant property via if or switch, TypeScript automatically narrows the union to the specific variant type, granting access to variant-specific fields without casting. ts export interface SuccessResult { readonly kind: 'success'; readonly data: string; } export interface FailureResult { readonly kind: 'failure'; readonly errorCode: number; readonly message: string; } export type AsyncResult = SuccessResult FailureResult; Why it exists Modeling mutually exclusive domain states with optional fields on a single interface ({ isSuccess?: boolean; data?: string; error?: string }) results in invalid intermediate states (such as an object containing both data and error, or neither). It also forces developers to perform unsafe non-null assertions (result.data!). Discriminated unions realize CONCEPT-prog-v1-002-01-closed-type-union: 1. Impossible states made unrepresentable: Each variant contains only the fields valid for that state, preventing malformed combinations. 2. Automatic type narrowing: Checking result.kind === 'success' narrows result to SuccessResult, making result.data immediately accessible. 3. Contrast with Java and Python: Java sealed classes (CONCEPT-java-v1-004-01-sealed-classes) use nominal class inheritance permits, while Python (CONCEPT-python-v1-002-01-union-type) uses nominal union types or structural match patterns. TypeScript's discriminated unions operate entirely structurally based on literal field values."},{"t":"Exhaustive Switch Narrowing with Never Check","i":"CONCEPT-ts-v1-003-01-exhaustive-switch-never","h":"doc/catalogs/ts/concepts/CONCEPT-ts-v1-003-01-exhaustive-switch-never.html","s":"accepted","x":"Exhaustive Switch Narrowing with Never Check Definition In TypeScript, exhaustive branching over a discriminated union is statically enforced using the bottom type never. Inside the default branch of a switch statement, assigning the discriminant variable to a type annotated as never (or passing it to a helper function function assertNever(x: never): never) forces the TypeScript compiler to confirm that all variants were handled in earlier cases. If an unhandled variant reaches default, TypeScript emits a compile error. ts export function assertNever(x: never): never { throw new Error(Unexpected object: ${JSON.stringify(x)}); } export function handleResult(res: AsyncResult): string { switch (res.kind) { case 'success': return Loaded: ${res.data}; case 'failure': return Failed (${res.errorCode}): ${res.message}; default: return assertNever(res); // Compiler error if any variant of AsyncResult is omitted above } } Why it exists Without the never check, omitting a case in a switch statement causes TypeScript to fall through silently to default, often resulting in returning undefined. When new variants are added to a discriminated union, developers would have to manually find every switch statement across the codebase without compiler guidance. The never exhaustiveness check realizes CONCEPT-prog-v1-003-01-exhaustive-branching: 1. Refactoring safety: Adding a new variant (e.g. PendingResult) to AsyncResult immediately causes compile errors in all functions that do not handle PendingResult. 2. Elimination of dead code: If all cases are handled, the variable in default has type never, proving dead code elimination mathematically. 3. Dual compile-time and runtime protection: Statically caught by TypeScript during build, and throws an informative runtime error if non-typechecked JavaScript passes an invalid object."},{"t":"Strict Null Checks","i":"CONCEPT-ts-v1-004-01-strict-null-checks","h":"doc/catalogs/ts/concepts/CONCEPT-ts-v1-004-01-strict-null-checks.html","s":"accepted","x":"Strict Null Checks Definition In TypeScript, enabling \"strictNullChecks\": true (or \"strict\": true) in tsconfig.json fundamentally alters the type system so that null and undefined are distinct types, not members of every domain type. A variable typed as string can never hold null or undefined. Optionality must be explicitly declared as a union (string null undefined or string?). Accessing properties on nullable types is prohibited until narrowed. ts export function formatEmail(email: string null undefined): string { // return email.toLowerCase(); // Error: 'email' is possibly 'null' or 'undefined'. if (email == null) { return 'no-reply@example.com'; } return email.toLowerCase(); // Type narrowed to string } Why it exists In unstrict TypeScript (and early versions), null and undefined were valid values for every type (let s: string = null; was permitted). This allowed runtime TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined to bypass compiler checks, defeating a primary reason for using a static type system. Strict null checks realize CONCEPT-prog-v1-004-01-null-safe-optionality: 1. Absence is explicit: Function signatures explicitly document whether parameters or return values can be omitted or empty. 2. Flow-sensitive type guards: The compiler tracks conditions like if (val != null) to narrow types within branch scopes automatically. 3. Contrast with Java and Python: Unlike Java which requires bytecode annotation processors (CONCEPT-java-v1-019-01-jspecify-and-nullaway), or Python which unifies absence under None (CONCEPT-python-v1-004-01-none-aware-typing), TypeScript distinguishes between unassigned values (undefined) and intentional blank values (null) within its type algebra."},{"t":"Explicit Resource Management with Using and Disposable","i":"CONCEPT-ts-v1-005-01-using-disposable","h":"doc/catalogs/ts/concepts/CONCEPT-ts-v1-005-01-using-disposable.html","s":"accepted","x":"Explicit Resource Management with Using and Disposable Definition Introduced in TypeScript 5.2 (implementing the TC39 Stage 3 Explicit Resource Management proposal), using and await using declarations provide deterministic scope-based disposal. Objects implementing the global Disposable interface (with Symbol.dispose) or AsyncDisposable interface (with Symbol.asyncDispose) are automatically cleaned up when the lexical block exits, regardless of normal return or thrown exceptions. ts export class DatabaseConnection implements Disposable { constructor(public readonly url: string) {} query(sql: string): string { return Results for ${sql}; } Symbol.dispose: void { console.log(Connection to ${this.url} closed.); } } export function executeWork(): void { using conn = new DatabaseConnection(\"postgres://localhost:5432/app\"); conn.query(\"SELECT 1\"); } // connSymbol.dispose is guaranteed to be invoked here Why it exists Prior to TypeScript 5.2, deterministic cleanup required wrapping every resource acquisition in nested try ... finally blocks. Asynchronous workflows with multiple transient resources suffered from severe boilerplate (often called \"finally pyramid of doom\") and high risk of leaking resources if an exception was thrown before entering a finally block. using and Disposable realize CONCEPT-prog-v1-005-01-scoped-resource-cleanup: 1. Lexical RAII semantics: Brings RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) semantics to TypeScript with zero boilerplate. 2. Reverse disposal order: Multiple using declarations in the same block are disposed of in reverse order of initialization (LIFO). 3. Contrast with Java: Java's try-with-resources (CONCEPT-java-v1-012-01-try-with-resources-and-cleaner) requires an explicit try (...) wrapper block, whereas TypeScript's using declaration attaches directly to any standard block scope (function, loop, or if statement)."},{"t":"Typed Test Fixture Extension","i":"CONCEPT-ts-v1-006-01-typed-test-fixture-extension","h":"doc/catalogs/ts/concepts/CONCEPT-ts-v1-006-01-typed-test-fixture-extension.html","s":"accepted","x":"Typed Test Fixture Extension Definition In TypeScript test runners (such as @playwright/test and Vitest), test fixtures are strongly typed and extended using test.extend (). Each fixture definition provides setup and teardown by destructuring dependencies, performing setup actions, awaiting use(fixtureValue), and executing teardown logic. The runner automatically infers fixture types and validates dependency graphs at compile time. ts // Playwright Test / Modern TS fixture extension export interface TestContext { tempDir: string; } // Extends base test runner with strongly typed fixtures: // export const test = baseTest.extend ({ // tempDir: async ({}, use) = { // const dir = await fs.promises.mkdtemp('/tmp/ts-'); // await use(dir); // await fs.promises.rm(dir, { recursive: true, force: true }); // }, // }); ts export type FixtureFunction = (use: (value: T) = Promise ) = Promise ; export async function withIsolatedContext (resource: T, runTest: (ctx: T) = Promise ): Promise { console.log(\"Setting up typed test fixture\"); try { await runTest(resource); } finally { console.log(\"Tearing down typed test fixture\"); } } Why it exists In JavaScript test frameworks, passing mock objects or test state through this context or global hooks lacks type safety. Developers frequently misspell fixture property names or access uninitialized fixtures without the compiler catching the defect. Typed fixture extension realizes CONCEPT-prog-v1-006-01-scoped-test-fixture: 1. Compile-time auto-completion: Tests declare fixtures as destructured arguments, and TypeScript validates that every requested fixture exists and matches its expected type. 2. Deterministic execution graph: The runner resolves dependencies between fixtures automatically, setting up and tearing down resources in the correct dependency order. 3. Strict test isolation: Eliminates mutable test suite instances by injecting pristine, typed fixture instances into each test."},{"t":"Typed Promise Concurrency","i":"CONCEPT-ts-v1-007-01-typed-promise-concurrency","h":"doc/catalogs/ts/concepts/CONCEPT-ts-v1-007-01-typed-promise-concurrency.html","s":"accepted","x":"Typed Promise Concurrency Definition In TypeScript, concurrent fan-out via Promise.all and Promise.allSettled leverages variadic tuple types (introduced in TypeScript 4.0) to preserve exact heterogeneous types across concurrent operations. Passing a tuple of promises [Promise , Promise ] into Promise.all returns a strongly typed tuple [User, Order[]], maintaining positional type safety across all parallel branches. ts export interface User { id: string; name: string; } export interface Order { id: string; total: number; } export async function loadDashboard(userId: string): Promise { const userPromise: Promise = fetchUser(userId); const ordersPromise: Promise = fetchOrders(userId); // TypeScript statically infers the exact tuple type [User, Order[]] const [user, orders] = await Promise.all([userPromise, ordersPromise]); return [user, orders]; } declare function fetchUser(id: string): Promise ; declare function fetchOrders(id: string): Promise ; Why it exists In JavaScript or untyped promise utilities, resolving heterogeneous operations in parallel degrades to returning an array of generic or any values. Developers are forced to insert manual type casts (const user = res[0] as User), which hide breaking changes if the promise ordering changes or an operation is modified. Typed promise concurrency realizes CONCEPT-prog-v1-007-01-parallel-fan-out: 1. Positional type integrity: Swapping or adding an asynchronous call immediately updates the inferred tuple types, alerting downstream consumers of any mismatch. 2. Exhaustive result checking: With Promise.allSettled, TypeScript provides discriminated unions for each element (PromiseFulfilledResult PromiseRejectedResult), forcing callers to handle rejected states properly. 3. High parallel performance: Maximizes I/O concurrency without sacrificing static type guarantees."},{"t":"Readonly Collection Types and Const Tuples","i":"CONCEPT-ts-v1-008-01-readonly-array-and-const-tuple","h":"doc/catalogs/ts/concepts/CONCEPT-ts-v1-008-01-readonly-array-and-const-tuple.html","s":"accepted","x":"Readonly Collection Types and Const Tuples Definition In TypeScript 3.4+, immutable collections are declared using readonly T[], ReadonlyArray , ReadonlyMap , ReadonlySet , and as const assertions on array literals. Marked collections disallow mutating methods (push, pop, splice, set, delete) at compile time. Applying as const to an array literal creates a fixed-length readonly tuple with exact literal member types. ts export const ROLES = ['admin', 'operator', 'viewer'] as const; // Type: readonly ['admin', 'operator', 'viewer'] export type Role = (typeof ROLES)[number]; // 'admin' 'operator' 'viewer' export function sanitizeTags(tags: readonly string[]): readonly string[] { // tags.push('new'); // Error: Property 'push' does not exist on type 'readonly string[]'. return tags.filter((t) = t.trim().length 0); } Why it exists JavaScript arrays are inherently mutable. Standard library routines such as .sort() mutate arrays in place, causing bugs when cached arrays or function parameters are sorted by one component while another component expects their original order. Readonly collections and const tuples realize CONCEPT-prog-v1-008-01-immutable-collection-literal: 1. Single source of truth: A const tuple defines both the runtime array of values and derives the union type of valid strings ((typeof ROLES)[number]), eliminating duplicate declarations. 2. Defensive copying elimination: Functions accepting readonly T[] guarantee callers that their input arrays will not be modified, removing the need to copy arrays defensively. 3. Zero runtime overhead: Pure compile-time enforcement that emits standard, lightweight JavaScript arrays without wrapper objects."},{"t":"Generic Function Signatures and Type Predicates","i":"CONCEPT-ts-v1-009-01-generic-function-signature","h":"doc/catalogs/ts/concepts/CONCEPT-ts-v1-009-01-generic-function-signature.html","s":"accepted","x":"Generic Function Signatures and Type Predicates Definition In TypeScript, first-class callables and higher-order functions are typed using generic function signatures ( (item: T) = R) and user-defined type predicates (x is SubType). Generic signatures preserve input and output type relationships across functional transformations, while type predicate signatures allow higher-order filtering functions to narrow collection element types statically. ts export function isNonNullable (value: T): value is NonNullable { return value !== null && value !== undefined; } export function compactList (items: readonly (T null undefined)[]): T[] { // Array.filter with type predicate narrows result type from (T null undefined)[] to T[] return items.filter(isNonNullable); } export function memoize ( fn: (...args: Args) = Result ): (...args: Args) = Result { const cache = new Map (); return (...args: Args): Result = { const key = JSON.stringify(args); if (!cache.has(key)) { cache.set(key, fn(...args)); } return cache.get(key)!; }; } Why it exists Without generic signatures, higher-order functions must resort to any or loose base types, erasing specific argument and return types. Furthermore, standard filtering operations like removing null values from a list (list.filter(x = x !== null)) previously retained the nullable type, requiring unsafe casts (as string[]). Generic signatures and type predicates realize CONCEPT-prog-v1-009-01-higher-order-function: 1. Type propagation: The precise return type of a passed function flows seamlessly into the return type of the enclosing higher-order wrapper. 2. Static filtering narrowing: Type predicates (x is T) instruct the compiler that returning true guarantees the refined type for subsequent operations. 3. Flexible composition: Enables utility decorators, retry handlers, and memoization helpers without losing original parameter typings."},{"t":"Validated Environment Configuration Binding","i":"CONCEPT-ts-v1-010-01-typed-env-config","h":"doc/catalogs/ts/concepts/CONCEPT-ts-v1-010-01-typed-env-config.html","s":"accepted","x":"Validated Environment Configuration Binding Definition In TypeScript, external environment binding maps string-based environment variables from process.env into a strictly validated, immutable configuration interface. Helper functions validate the presence of required keys, coerce strings to numbers or booleans, check domain constraints, and return a readonly typed object, failing immediately at application startup if validation fails. ts export interface AppEnvironment { readonly port: number; readonly databaseUrl: string; readonly isProduction: boolean; } export function loadAppEnvironment(env: Record = process.env): AppEnvironment { const rawPort = env.PORT ?? '8080'; const port = parseInt(rawPort, 10); if (Number.isNaN(port) port 65535) { throw new Error(Invalid PORT specified: \"${rawPort}\"); } const databaseUrl = env.DATABASEURL; if (!databaseUrl) { throw new Error(\"Missing required environment variable: DATABASEURL\"); } return Object.freeze({ port, databaseUrl, isProduction: env.NODEENV === 'production', }); } Why it exists In Node.js, process.env typed as NodeJS.ProcessEnv treats all properties as string undefined. Directly querying process.env across application components creates risks of undefined reference bugs, unvalidated string formats, and scattered fallback defaults. Validated configuration binding realizes CONCEPT-prog-v1-010-01-external-environment-binding: 1. Startup fail-fast: Missing credentials or invalid ports cause an immediate startup crash rather than failing halfway through request servicing. 2. Strict type contracts: Downstream application services depend on a clean, typed AppEnvironment interface without worrying about string parsing or null checks. 3. Environment testability: Accepting the environment dictionary as an optional parameter makes unit testing configuration parsing trivial without mutating global process.env."},{"t":"The SUT model","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-001-01-the-sut-model","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-001-01-the-sut-model.html","s":"draft","x":"The SUT model Definition One JSON document that declares a system under test: its states (places a journey can be, each with a marker that proves arrival), its elements (named locators), its actions (steps the SUT offers — the edges of the graph), its journeys (named paths through the actions) and its endpoints (the API surface). The contract is SPEC-taf-v1-001-04. Why it exists A test suite is usually a pile of files that encode knowledge of the SUT invisibly. Here the knowledge is written down once, as data: the same model generates the Python suite and the Java suite, refuses broken input with named reasons, and later is the graph that model-based walks run on. Change the model, regenerate, and the suite's diff is the change — visible in a pull request. In this repo sut.model.json per SUT (none yet — lane A-1 builds the loader). Read SPEC-taf-v1-001-04 for the clauses and the refusals."},{"t":"The seeded walk","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-002-01-the-seeded-walk","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-002-01-the-seeded-walk.html","s":"draft","x":"The seeded walk Definition A random test is only useful if it can fail twice. A seeded walk drives a pseudo-random number generator with a recorded seed, so the \"random\" journey it derived is exactly reproducible: same model, same start, same seed — the same steps. The walk's output is a journey declaration in the model's own JSON shape, so the replay is data you can commit, review, and emit — not a log file only a runner understands. python -m taf walk model.json --from board --seed 42 --steps 6 walk.json python -m taf emit --lang python model.json -o suite --journeys walk.json Why it exists MBT without replay is a bug report that says \"it failed once.\" Seed replay turns exploration into a regression asset: commit the seed (or the derived journey), and the failure is a named test forever. --cover prefers untaken edges — the first step toward coverage-directed generation. In this repo taf/graph.py — edges derives adjacency from declared from/to; walk traverses it under random.Random(seed); withjourneys merges derived journeys into a copy of the model. T-taf-006 proves determinism, coverage, and the merge path."},{"t":"The dwell","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-003-01-the-dwell","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-003-01-the-dwell.html","s":"draft","x":"The dwell Definition A {\"dwell\": } step — a declared pause, emitted as a literal page.waitfortimeout(800) / page.waitForTimeout(800) at exactly the step where a watching human needs the screen to hold still. Why it exists Automation that nobody can watch cannot teach. The old framework's visibilitySleep answered two needs with one sleep: letting the page settle and letting the eye follow. tafbase separates them — correctness belongs to markers and CONCEPT-playwright-v1-003-01-auto-waiting-and-actionability; observation belongs to dwell. Keeping them apart is the whole point: a sleep that papers over a timing bug is a lie the suite tells later. In this project dwell is a validated step form (taf/model.py, bad-dwell refusal); both emitters render it; the tracker model pauses 800 ms inside open-tot so a watcher sees the tot island land. Suite-wide pacing is the environment's job — TAFSLOWMO in CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-environment. Touch it bash .venv/bin/python -m pytest generated/python/ikc3-tracker -q watch the pause Neighbors wait-for in CONCEPT-dsl-v1-004-01-the-wait; narration in CONCEPT-dsl-v1-006-01-the-label."},{"t":"The wait","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-004-01-the-wait","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-004-01-the-wait.html","s":"draft","x":"The wait Definition Step forms that say how long to look. Implemented today: {\"wait-for\": {\"element\": x}} or {\"state\": y} — an auto-waiting assertion that must hold. Planned (lane H-8): {\"check\": ...}, the soft twin that collects a failure and continues, and {\"wait-any\": [...]} for UIs with alternating states. Why it exists Most flakiness is timing that was never declared. A junior reaches for sleep; a named wait says the intent — hold hard until the marker appears versus log and continue. The old framework kept the whole vocabulary: waitUntilVisibilityOfElement, waitInvisibilityOfElement, waitUntilVisibilityOfEitherElements, with soft variants that logged, saved DOM and screenshot, and went on. Hard fails the step; soft records it. A suite needs both — \"the SUT must be here\" and \"the SUT might offer this\". In this project wait-for is a step form in taf/model.py and both emitters (state → expectarrived, element → tobevisible). wait-any is implemented. expect … \"soft\": true collects into the SoftChecks stack flushed once at test end (CONCEPT-testing-v1-002-01-the-failure-funnel); a free-standing check form is still planned (lane H-8). Touch it Add {\"wait-for\": {\"state\": \"tot\"}} to a do list and run python -m taf check — the loader validates it like any step. Neighbors CONCEPT-playwright-v1-003-01-auto-waiting-and-actionability (the mechanism), CONCEPT-dsl-v1-003-01-the-dwell (observation, not correctness)."},{"t":"The versioned locator","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-005-01-the-versioned-locator","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-005-01-the-versioned-locator.html","s":"draft","x":"The versioned locator Definition One element, several declarations, keyed by the SUT's version (and, where needed, language): the locator that fits is chosen by nearest declared version at or below the target, with a plain fallback. Why it exists Suites rot fastest at locators, and an app's UI differs between releases. The old framework's VersionedLocator was a version x language matrix — addLocator(515, SPANISH, \"xpath\", ...) — resolved by getBestGuessLocator: largest version <= requested, English fallback. It decoupled the suite from app-version churn; a suite could run QA build 480 and UAT build 515 from one declaration. In this project Planned — lane I-9. Today's elements map one name to one rung of CONCEPT-qa-v1-002-01-the-locator-ladder (realized in Playwright by CONCEPT-playwright-v1-002-01-semantic-locators); the versioned form adds variants (v515+, es) that the emitter resolves at codegen — the lookup table becomes generated data, or per-version generated suites, not a runtime map. Touch it Not yet runnable — see lane I-9 in PLAN-taf-v1-001-01. Neighbors CONCEPT-dsl-v1-011-01-the-locale (the language dimension); CONCEPT-dsl-v1-012-01-the-target (which version a run aims at)."},{"t":"The label","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-006-01-the-label","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-006-01-the-label.html","s":"draft","x":"The label Definition A human name on an element, action, or step that emitted code narrates as it runs — the old framework's canonicalName + parentName that made every action announce itself: Clicking 'Sign In button' on 'Login Pom'. Why it exists A run's output should read as a story, not as selectors. The label is what a watcher hears; it is what a fault report can quote; and with a timestamp it is the raw material of CONCEPT-taf-v1-006-01-the-subtitle-track. The old framework pushed the same narration into document.title — progress visible even inside remote-session videos, a trick worth keeping because it is free. In this project Planned — lane H-8. Today an action narrates only by its model name (open-tot); label makes it prose. Emitted code prints the label before each step — cheap in generated code, invisible when absent. Touch it Not yet runnable — see lane H-8 in PLAN-taf-v1-001-01. Neighbors CONCEPT-taf-v1-006-01-the-subtitle-track, CONCEPT-testing-v1-002-01-the-failure-funnel."},{"t":"The step language","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-007-01-the-step-language","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-007-01-the-step-language.html","s":"draft","x":"The step language Definition The closed verb vocabulary inside do: and steps: — act (click, fill, check, select), move (navigate), observe (wait-for, expect), call the API (api, save), pace (dwell). A step is one key with its argument; anything else is refused, named. Why it exists A small closed vocabulary is a grammar a person learns in one sitting — and it is not new. The old framework's TSV interpreter ran the same shape as columns: step, action, field name, label, value, screenshot control, locale overrides, ticket. It was field-proven on juniors; generating code from the same grammar is its compile-time heir — what was a runtime interpreter becomes emitted statements a reader can step through. Planned siblings from the same harvest: maybe (the ifPresent idiom — click only if present, never fail), check/wait-any (see CONCEPT-dsl-v1-004-01-the-wait), click-image (see CONCEPT-dsl-v1-008-01-the-image-step). In this project STEPFORMS in taf/model.py is the whole grammar; SPEC-taf-v1-001-04 TAF-3 is the contract; each form renders in both emitters — parity is asserted by tests/testparity.py. Touch it bash .venv/bin/python -m taf check suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json Neighbors CONCEPT-dsl-v1-001-01-the-sut-model, CONCEPT-dsl-v1-003-01-the-dwell."},{"t":"The image step","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-008-01-the-image-step","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-008-01-the-image-step.html","s":"draft","x":"The image step Definition A step that acts on pixels, not the DOM: find a template image on the screen and click it — the old framework's OpenCV matchTemplate used to clear CloudFlare's \"verify you are human\" checkbox, and its Sikuli layer did wait/highlight/click-by-image and OCR. Why it exists Some surfaces have no accessible DOM — anti-bot gates, canvas apps, native dialogs. The image step is the last rung past the last rung: fragile by nature, and honest only when it says so. The better fix is always cooperation — the old framework also sent a tradu-test header so the backend whitelisted automation. Keep both: negotiate when you can, match pixels when you cannot. In this project Deferred — niche. The locator ladder's css/xpath rung is fragile the same way; an image step would carry fragile in the manifest like they do. See RESEARCH-taf-v1-002-01, harvest item 19. Touch it Not implemented. The honest placeholder: treat it as a fragile locator when it arrives. Neighbors CONCEPT-qa-v1-002-01-the-locator-ladder, CONCEPT-testing-v1-003-01-the-golden-file (image comparison done right)."},{"t":"The test id","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-009-01-the-test-id","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-009-01-the-test-id.html","s":"draft","x":"The test id Definition A short, stable identifier a test carries — the old framework's ASM011, extracted from the scenario name to drive golden filenames, result folders, and report groups. Why it exists Names are prose; ids are handles. Every artifact a run leaves — ASM011run42desktopstep3Expected.PNG, a rerun manifest entry, a fault report's subject — joins on the id. Parsing it out of a name was the workaround; declaring it (test ASM011 \"...\") makes it a first-class token the model owns and the manifest exports. In this project Planned — today the journey name is the de facto id (taf.manifest.json keys on it). Declared test ids arrive with the artifact grammar of lane J-10 and the rerun manifest of lane L-12. Touch it Read generated/python/ikc3-tracker/taf.manifest.json — the test fields are the ids a declared token will replace. Neighbors CONCEPT-testing-v1-003-01-the-golden-file, CONCEPT-taf-v1-004-01-the-artifacts, CONCEPT-testing-v1-004-01-the-rerun."},{"t":"The fixture block","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-010-01-the-fixture","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-010-01-the-fixture.html","s":"draft","x":"The fixture block Definition Arrange through the API, act through the UI: a fixture: block of backend calls a journey runs before its browser steps — the old framework's SOAP/XML command templates that prepared a trader account before the UI test ever opened a page. Why it exists Driving setup through the UI is slow and brittle — the journey then tests the login screen when it meant to test the ticket board. An API fixture is fast, precise, and honest about what is under test. It is also where test data comes from: create the account, seed the ticket, then open the browser. In this project The mechanism half exists — api steps already call endpoints and save response values. The declared fixture: arrangement (runs once before the act, clearly separated from steps under test) is planned; see RESEARCH-taf-v1-002-01, item 24. Touch it json {\"api\": \"list-tickets\"} // already runs mid-journey; a fixture block is the planned home Neighbors CONCEPT-testing-v1-001-01-arrange-act-assert — the fixture is the arrange; CONCEPT-dsl-v1-007-01-the-step-language."},{"t":"The locale","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-011-01-the-locale","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-011-01-the-locale.html","s":"draft","x":"The locale Definition A language dimension on the model's data and locators — one data table per language (table[lang][key]), locator variants per locale, and the occasional behavioral flip (the old framework's Arabic runs reversed slider direction). Why it exists A localized SUT is not one SUT — \"Log in\" and \"Ingresar\" are different locators for the same element. The old framework ran 18 languages off xlsx columns and transLabel/transValue row overrides; without the dimension declared, localization testing means a second, drifting copy of the model. In this project Deferred — pairs naturally with CONCEPT-dsl-v1-005-01-the-versioned-locator, whose matrix is (version x language). It lands when a second-language SUT model needs it; until then the model's elements/data are single-locale. Touch it Not implemented — see RESEARCH-taf-v1-002-01, items 5 and 25. Neighbors CONCEPT-dsl-v1-005-01-the-versioned-locator, CONCEPT-dsl-v1-012-01-the-target."},{"t":"The target","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-012-01-the-target","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-012-01-the-target.html","s":"draft","x":"The target Definition device: and env: header clauses — Pixel9ProXLchrome, qa, uat — resolved at emit time into the capabilities block and base URL the suite launches with. One token, everything derived. Why it exists The old framework parsed a resolution string at runtime — w10chrome, GalaxyS25Ultrasamsung, iPhone16Prosafari — through 300 lines of factory dispatching on OS, browser, device, local CDP emulation or BrowserStack. It was already a mini-DSL with a runtime interpreter. Generated code does better: the vocabulary is validated at emit time, the emitted suite carries the exact capabilities object, and the tag-to-environment mapping (@qaenv - suite + URL + locator version) bakes in the same way. The layered property files it replaced — root defaults, module config, user overrides — become inputs to codegen, resolved once and printed into the suite header. In this project Planned — lane K-11. Today the only retarget is TAFBASEURL (CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-environment). Touch it Not yet runnable — see lane K-11 in PLAN-taf-v1-001-01. Neighbors CONCEPT-dsl-v1-005-01-the-versioned-locator — target version picks locator variants; CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-environment."},{"t":"The absorption loop","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-013-01-the-absorption-loop","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-013-01-the-absorption-loop.html","s":"draft","x":"The absorption loop Definition The loop that grows the DSL: the language cannot say it → a test is authored by hand in custom/ → the manual pattern recurs or stabilizes → a model clause learns to say it (loader + both emitters + spec + a named refusal) → regenerate, and generated code replaces the manual code. custom/ trends back toward empty. Why it exists A fixed vocabulary eventually fails — the SUT does something the step language never imagined. Two bad answers exist: freeze (the DSL rots) or grow custom/ forever (the DSL becomes a suggestion and the manifest stops describing the suite). The loop is the third: hand-code is scaffolding, the model absorbs it, and the language is the residue of everything that ever needed saying twice. The old framework ran the same metabolism informally — waitUntilVisibilityOfEitherElements and the ifPresent idioms were born as hand-rolled helpers that recurred until they earned a name. Here it is deliberate: wait-any, maybe, soft expect, dwell are the loop already running, fed by the harvest rather than by custom code. In this project custom/ is the seam (planned — the emitter never writes there, the manifest can mark source: hand). A growing custom/ is the DSL reporting which clause is missing — a diagnostic, not a failure. Touch it The newest clauses are the proof: bash grep -E 'wait-any maybe soft dwell' suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json Neighbors CONCEPT-dsl-v1-007-01-the-step-language — the vocabulary the loop grows; CONCEPT-taf-v1-007-01-the-three-levels — custom/ is where Use reaches back toward Model."},{"t":"The two inputs — declared and derived","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-014-01-the-two-inputs","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-014-01-the-two-inputs.html","s":"draft","x":"The two inputs — declared and derived Definition A model has two authors: a black-box declaration — a person writes suts/.sut.model.json from what they know of the SUT — and a white-box derivation — a tool computes the draft model from SUT artifacts (source declarations, route tables, API specs, a crawl). taf derive produces a model; the loader cannot tell which author wrote it. Why it exists \"Describe your app in JSON\" is a tax. The honest promise is: say what you know, derive what the SUT already declares. IKC3's workflow.edn and support.edn already enumerate command surfaces; an OpenAPI spec already lists endpoints; a running app's routes already list states. Deriving the draft and letting the author refine it removes the blank-page problem while keeping the model the single hub — derivation is another way to write JSON, not a second pipeline. And a derived model feeds coverage: taf walk explores; systematic traversal generates a journey for every edge — the FSM's transitions become the test set. In this project Today: black-box only — suts/.sut.model.json is hand-authored; taf walk is the seeded start of traversal. Planned: taf derive and edge-coverage journey generation. Lane N-13. The input sources, by what feeds them: source mode example --- --- --- the author black-box the JSON model today; BDD/Gherkin notation later documents grey taf derive --from-spec prd.md — assisted extraction, never silent guessing SUT artifacts white-box IKC3's workflow.edn/support.edn, route tables, OpenAPI → endpoints the live SUT white-box a crawl: links/forms → states + edges, confidence-marked existing tests white-box mine an old suite's locators and actions A derived draft carries its ignorance honestly — underived: for what the source couldn't classify, openQuestions: for what needs a human — never an empty field pretending confidence (the honesty schema from RESEARCH-taf-v1-003-01's qaiq findings). Provenance and deviation. The model is one; the sources are many — so each fact's origin lives in a sidecar (.sut.provenance.json), not in the model itself: {path: {source, confidence, last-seen}}. Re-derivation then reports deviation, never overwrites and never hides: a deriver-owned fact that changed upstream updates the model with a changed: note; a human-authored fact that now disagrees with the source is surfaced (deviates: human says X, source says Y), not suppressed; a human fact absent from the source is not-in-source. The person reconciles; the framework only names what each side claims — the same honesty as the refusals, applied to drift. Touch it bash .venv/bin/python -m taf walk suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json --from board --seed 7 --steps 4 Neighbors CONCEPT-dsl-v1-001-01-the-sut-model, CONCEPT-dsl-v1-002-01-the-seeded-walk, CONCEPT-dsl-v1-013-01-the-absorption-loop — derivation is how the model absorbs the SUT's own declarations."},{"t":"The env: value","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-015-01-the-env-value","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-015-01-the-env-value.html","s":"draft","x":"The env: value Definition A fill value of the form env:NAME is a need, not a literal: the emitted code reads the variable NAME at run time. The model carries the name; the environment carries the value. Python spells it os.environ[\"NAME\"], Java Objects.requireNonNull(System.getenv(\"NAME\"), …) — same clause, each language's own reading. json {\"fill\": {\"element\": \"secret\", \"value\": \"env:TAFSUTSECRET\"}} Why it exists A credential in a model is a credential in the repository — the model is committed, diffed, and emitted twice. env: is how the model declares that a run needs a value without holding what it is. taf model.envneeds collects every need, so taf dry-run reports an unset one as missing, taf run refuses on it before a browser opens, and taf env prints it as a fill-in line — the need surfaces everywhere a run is prepared, while the secret stays in the shell. In this project model.envneeds(m) in taf/model.py is the single scan the emitters, the dry run, and the gate all use — one definition of \"needed\", many readers. The tracker's sign-in uses it: suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json fills handle and secret from env:TAFSUTHANDLE / env:TAFSUTSECRET. Touch it bash export TAFSUTHANDLE=ada TAFSUTSECRET=… taf suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json env needs → ok taf env suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json needs → fill-in lines Neighbors CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-environment — the contract the value travels in. CONCEPT-dsl-v1-014-01-the-two-inputs — env: is a third voice in the same choir: neither declared data nor derived, but needed. CONCEPT-taf-v1-005-01-the-preflight — a need unset is a gate's first finding."},{"t":"The auth clause","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-016-01-the-auth-clause","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-016-01-the-auth-clause.html","s":"draft","x":"The auth clause Definition sut.auth names the action that signs in — {\"auth\": {\"action\": \"log-in-with-code\"}}. The emitted suite runs it once per run in a bootstrap context at the action's from state, saves the session as storage state, and every test's fresh context inherits it. Python: context.storagestate(path=…) into results/ /auth-state.json, then browser.newcontext(storagestate=…). Java: signInOnce() and setStorageStatePath(…) in BaseTest. Why it exists Each test gets a fresh context — isolation by construction. But a credential that spends once per sign-in (a one-time code) cannot afford per-test logins: two tests would need two codes. Storage state splits the difference — the session is shared, the context still isn't. One code spent per run; no test's state bleeds into another's. In this project The clause is sut.auth in SPEC-taf-v1-001-04; the loader requires the named action to exist and to have a from state (the page the run navigates to so it can sign in) — refusals :unknown-action and :bad-auth. The action's env:/env-pool: needs join envneeds, so an unset TAFSUTCODES gates the run before a browser opens. Touch it json \"sut\": {\"auth\": {\"action\": \"log-in-with-code\"}} bash export TAFSUTCODES=\"code-1\" taf run suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json --slowmo 500 --video results/ /auth-state.json — the session every test reused Neighbors CONCEPT-playwright-v1-008-01-storage-state-reuse — the mechanism the clause rides on. CONCEPT-dsl-v1-015-01-the-env-value — the credential the action reads."},{"t":"The page object","i":"CONCEPT-pom-v1-001-01-the-page-object","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-pom-v1-001-01-the-page-object.html","s":"draft","x":"The page object Definition A class that is a page or state of the SUT: it holds the page's locators and its meaningful operations, so tests say board.expectarrived() instead of repeating selectors. python class BoardPage: def init(self, page): self.marker = page.locator(\".ikc-dock-theme\") def expectarrived(self): expect(self.marker).tobevisible() Why it exists Locators are the most-fragile line in a suite. When they live in one class per state, a UI change is a one-place fix — and tests read as intent (\"arrive at the board\"), not mechanics. In tafbase the pattern is stricter: a page object's marker is the proof of arrival the model declares, so the class also knows when its page is real. In this repo generated//pages.py — one class per declared state, generated from states in the model (SPEC-taf-v1-001-04)."},{"t":"The learning path","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-001-01-the-learning-path","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-001-01-the-learning-path.html","s":"draft","x":"The learning path Definition The ordered index of this corpus's concept documents, junior-first. The corpus is flat and wiki-linked; this page is the sequence a reader new to test automation can follow, each entry naming its prerequisites. Why it exists ADR-taf-v1-002-01 makes the delivered project a learning surface: every concept the generated code embodies is a page here, and generated files cite their concepts with @concept lines the checker enforces. This page answers \"where do I start?\" The path 1. Foundations of testing (ISTQB CTFL v4.0) → CONCEPT-istqb-v1-001-01-error-defect-failure, CONCEPT-istqb-v1-010-01-state-transition-testing, CONCEPT-istqb-v1-012-01-test-oracle 2. What a test is → CONCEPT-qa-v1-001-01-arrange-act-assert & CONCEPT-testing-v1-001-01-arrange-act-assert 3. Finding things on a page → CONCEPT-qa-v1-002-01-the-locator-ladder & CONCEPT-playwright-v1-002-01-semantic-locators 4. Knowing where you are → the state marker (SPEC-taf-v1-001-04, states) 5. The model → CONCEPT-dsl-v1-001-01-the-sut-model 6. From model to suite → the emitter and the manifest (SPEC-taf-v1-001-04) 7. Language archetypes → CONCEPT-prog-v1-001-01-immutable-data-carrier, CONCEPT-prog-v1-006-01-scoped-test-fixture, CONCEPT-prog-v1-010-01-external-environment-binding 8. Python & pytest lane → CONCEPT-python-v1-001-01-frozen-dataclass, CONCEPT-pytest-v1-001-01-dependency-injected-fixtures 9. Playwright mechanics → CONCEPT-playwright-v1-001-01-browser-context-page-hierarchy, CONCEPT-playwright-v1-003-01-auto-waiting-and-actionability 10. Organizing a suite → CONCEPT-qa-v1-005-01-page-object-model & CONCEPT-pom-v1-001-01-the-page-object 11. Configuring a run → CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-environment 12. Closing the loop → CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink 13. Java 21++ & JUnit 5 lane → CONCEPT-java-v1-001-01-junit-lifecycle-annotations, CONCEPT-java-v1-002-01-records, CONCEPT-java-v1-019-01-jspecify-and-nullaway 14. TypeScript & JavaScript @playwright/test lanes → CONCEPT-ts-v1-006-01-typed-test-fixture-extension, CONCEPT-js-v1-006-01-async-fixture-callback, CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-001-01-test-extend-fixtures 15. The graph — walks, seeds, coverage → CONCEPT-dsl-v1-002-01-the-seeded-walk & CONCEPT-qa-v1-010-01-model-based-seeded-walk Every entry is a checked citation — the corpus checker refuses a link to a page that does not exist. The domains The path is the on-ramp; the corpus is grouped by what a page teaches: domain teaches --- --- python the language surface — venv, env-adjacent mechanics pytest the test runner — fixtures, hooks, scope playwright the browser library — locators, contexts, waiting java the second emitter's world — JUnit lifecycle, Maven pom the page-object model — organizing a suite testing the discipline — AAA, failure handling, goldens, flakes dsl the model language — steps, locators, targets, walks taf the framework itself — its contract, sinks, artifacts A concept files under the domain it teaches; language-neutral ideas — the TAF contract"},{"t":"The result sink","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink.html","s":"draft","x":"The result sink Definition Where a finished test run sends what it found. A sink is a side channel: the suite never waits on it, and a sink that cannot file warns instead of failing the suite a second time. The first sink is the IKC3 tracker — a red test dispatches :support/file-report over the bus's WebSocket, and the tracker writes a :fault-report frame with its own page. Because the manifest maps each test to its journey, the report names the model element that failed, not only the test. python rid = ikc3sink.filereport( \"ws://127.0.0.1:4856/bus-ws\", \"taf journey failed: boardtotot — TimeoutError …\", url=page.url, dom=page.content()[:50000]) Why it exists A suite that only turns red ends the loop in a log file. The sink closes it: fail → report → ticket → fix → repaired is the quality loop the whole platform demonstrates, and the tracker is both the SUT and the tracker. In this repo taf/sinkikc3.py — the wire protocol and filereport. Emitted verbatim into every Python suite (ikc3sink.py) and driven by the pytestruntestmakereport hook in conftest.py when TAFIKC3WS is set. Verified live in tests/testlive.py (opt-in TAFLIVE=1)."},{"t":"The environment contract","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-environment","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-environment.html","s":"draft","x":"The environment contract Definition Every process inherits a set of text key=value pairs from the program that started it — its environment. The shell exports them; the program reads them. This page is the framework's contract over that OS mechanism: the TAF variables every generated suite honors, in every language. Python reads them through os.environ: python os.environ.get(\"TAFBASEURL\", \"http://127.0.0.1:4856\") value, or a default os.environ.get(\"TAFIKC3WS\") value, or None Java reads the same table through System.getenv(). One mechanism, two spellings — one contract, which is why a java page on environment variables does not exist: the variable means the same thing whoever reads it. A variable set for one command applies to that run only: bash TAFSLOWMO=800 .venv/bin/python -m pytest generated/python/ikc3-tracker -q (Each target language binds this contract through its own environment idiom — CONCEPT-python-v1-010-01-os-environ-get, CONCEPT-java-v1-031-01-system-getenv-binding, CONCEPT-ts-v1-010-01-typed-env-config, and CONCEPT-js-v1-010-01-process-env-binding.) Why it exists Generated code is immutable — you edit the model and regenerate, never the output. But where the SUT runs changes run to run. The environment is the seam where run-to-run variation belongs: the same committed suite tests a probe bus, a staging box, or production by setting a variable, not by editing code. The previous-generation framework externalized the same seam into config.properties files; environment variables are the twelve-factor form of the same idea. In this project variable read by meaning ---------------- ----------------------------- --------------------------------------------- TAFBASEURL conftest.py, BaseTest where the SUT lives — probe, staging, prod TAFIKC3WS pytestruntestmakereport when set, a red test files a fault report TAFHEADLESS conftest.py, BaseTest 1 for CI; headed is the default TAFSLOWMO conftest.py, BaseTest ms between Playwright actions TAFVIDEO conftest.py, BaseTest when set, the run records .webm TAFDEBUG conftest.py, BaseTest when set, a failure pauses the browser TAFRERUN conftest.py replays only the last failed.txt TAFRESULTS conftest.py, BaseTest the artifacts root; default results/ TAFLIVE tests/testlive.py opts the live acceptance tests in taf dry-run prints this table as the current shell sets it (CONCEPT-taf-v1-008-01-the-dry-run); taf env prints the same contract as export lines a console evals — base variables always export, opt-ins export on their flag, the rest print as comments so the generated script documents what it did not turn on. TAFBASEURL appears in taf/emitpython.py, taf/emitjava.py, and every emitted conftest.py/BaseTest.java. TAFIKC3WS drives CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink. Touch it bash env grep TAF what is set right now — usually nothing taf env suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json --video --sink prints the contract as export lines; a console adopts it by eval: eval \"$(taf env suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json --v"},{"t":"The artifacts","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-004-01-the-artifacts","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-004-01-the-artifacts.html","s":"draft","x":"The artifacts Definition Everything a run leaves behind, under a stable layout — results/ / / / carrying the video, the .srt subtitle sidecar, the DOM and screenshot on failure, the log slice. The old framework's CiTestsRunner established the contract: dated folders, suiteName.txt/publicURL.txt sidecars the report generator consumed. Why it exists Evidence outlives the run. A suite without artifacts answers only \"did it pass\"; with them it answers \"what happened\" — the difference between a test framework and a test observatory. The layout being declared and stable is what lets separate tooling (the HTML matrix report, a fault report, a human browsing) read it without asking the runner anything. In this project Today: the IKC3 sink ships DOM+URL into a fault report (CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink); pytest's own .pytestcache holds the rerun ledger. The results/ / layout, video, and the golden-file naming grammar are lanes J-10 and L-12. Touch it bash ls generated/python/ikc3-tracker/.pytestcache/v/cache/lastfailed 2 /dev/null Neighbors CONCEPT-testing-v1-003-01-the-golden-file, CONCEPT-taf-v1-006-01-the-subtitle-track, CONCEPT-testing-v1-004-01-the-rerun."},{"t":"The preflight","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-005-01-the-preflight","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-005-01-the-preflight.html","s":"draft","x":"The preflight Definition A declared gate before a run — requires: in the model (mail-server up, appium listening) — checked once, failing the whole suite fast and saying why. Its runtime form is the circuit breaker: the old framework's suiteStopReason flag that, once set (the mail server died), failed every subsequent test immediately rather than logging 40 identical red herrings. Why it exists Forty tests all failing \"connection refused\" is noise that costs an hour of triage before anyone asks whether the server was even up. Refusing early — with a named reason — is the same honesty the model loader applies statically, applied to a run's prerequisites. A suite that knows its requirements stops spending test failures on infrastructure facts. In this project Partly landed — lane L-12. The static analogue exists: taf check's refusals list every reason instead of dying on the first. The report form exists: taf dry-run names what a run lacks (CONCEPT-taf-v1-008-01-the-dry-run). The gate form begins: taf run probes by default and refuses to start while a probe finding is missing — before a browser opens. --no-probe skips the gate entirely: probes never run, env needs warn instead of refusing. Still planned: requires: — the declared gate, the run-time version of the same contract. Touch it bash .venv/bin/python -m taf check suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json refusals — the static form of the same idea .venv/bin/python -m taf dry-run suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json findings — the report form Neighbors CONCEPT-taf-v1-008-01-the-dry-run, CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink, CONCEPT-testing-v1-004-01-the-rerun — a preflight failure shouldn't file 40 reports, it should file one."},{"t":"The subtitle track","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-006-01-the-subtitle-track","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-006-01-the-subtitle-track.html","s":"draft","x":"The subtitle track Definition An .srt sidecar beside the run video: each narrated step (CONCEPT-dsl-v1-006-01-the-label) mapped onto video timecodes — 1 / 00:00:04,200 -- 00:00:05,000 / Clicking 'Sign In' on 'Login'. The run replays readable — a watcher sees what the suite was doing, not just where the mouse was. Why it exists The old framework wired all five teardown paths to capture a video- stop timestamp and call createSubTitlesFile — a writer that was only ever committed commented out. The intent was fully built except for the last function: narration existed (canonicalName), timestamps existed (setStartVideo), the video existed — only the SRT serializer was missing. In generated code it's nearly free: emitted steps know their text and their clock, so a teardown writes the file. In this project Planned — the narration half is lane H-8 (label), the video half is lane J-10's artifact layout (CONCEPT-taf-v1-004-01-the-artifacts); the .srt writer is the cheap tail of both. Touch it Not yet — the prerequisites it needs are the labels and the video. Neighbors CONCEPT-dsl-v1-006-01-the-label, CONCEPT-dsl-v1-003-01-the-dwell (a dwell is where a caption breathes), CONCEPT-taf-v1-004-01-the-artifacts."},{"t":"The three levels","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-007-01-the-three-levels","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-007-01-the-three-levels.html","s":"draft","x":"The three levels Definition tafbase is three activities, not one — and the reader is always only one of them at a time: Build — the framework itself: the loader, the emitters, the parity contract. Artifact: taf/, tests/. Model — applying the DSL to one SUT: declaring its states, elements, actions, journeys. Artifact: suts/.sut.model.json. Use — the generated suite running under the framework. Artifact: generated/. Why it exists The DSL First promise is a level boundary: the model author benefits without knowing the machinery — no metaprogramming, no emitters, no compiler mechanics. The promise is kept by keeping the levels apart: suts/ is data to level 1, authored input to level 2, and invisible to level 3 (who only ever sees generated code). In this project The repo layout is the separation: taf/ is Build, suts/ is Model, generated/ is Use. SPEC-taf-v1-001-04 mirrors it — Parts 1–2 are the model author's surface, Part 3 the framework's contract. CONTRIBUTING.md is the Build level's handbook; suts/ and generated/ each carry their own doc. Touch it bash python -m taf check suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json Model level .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests -q Build level .venv/bin/python -m pytest generated/python/ikc3-tracker -q Use level Neighbors CONCEPT-dsl-v1-001-01-the-sut-model (level 2's subject), CONCEPT-taf-v1-001-01-the-learning-path (which personas take which path)."},{"t":"The dry run","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-008-01-the-dry-run","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-008-01-the-dry-run.html","s":"draft","x":"The dry run Definition taf dry-run reports a run without executing one. It prints three things: the tests a run would execute (the manifest's truth), the TAF contract as this shell sets it, and findings — missing, off, note, ok. Video unset is off: the run leaves no recording. A compare-screen step with no golden file is missing: the run fails on it. --suite adds the emitted suite to the check — a stale manifest names itself. --probe touches the network: the SUT answers or it does not. Why it exists A run that fails for a reason a person could have read beforehand spends a test cycle on a fact. The model loader's rule — report every reason, never die on the first — applies to a run's context too. The dry run is the report form of that rule; requires: (the preflight, CONCEPT-taf-v1-005-01-the-preflight) is the gate form. A report says \"video is off\"; a gate refuses to start. In this project taf/dryrun.py builds the report; taf/main.py wires the subcommand. Exit code is 0 always — the person decides, not the tool. Opt-in variables (TAFVIDEO, TAFIKC3WS, TAFDEBUG) report off when unset, not missing — an opt-in feature is not a defect. What the model needs reports missing: goldens, a stale suite, an unreachable SUT under --probe. Touch it bash taf suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json dry-run is the default command taf dry-run suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json --suite generated/python/ikc3-tracker --probe taf dry-run suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json --json Neighbors CONCEPT-taf-v1-005-01-the-preflight, CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-environment, CONCEPT-taf-v1-004-01-the-artifacts"},{"t":"Arrange–Act–Assert","i":"CONCEPT-testing-v1-001-01-arrange-act-assert","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-testing-v1-001-01-arrange-act-assert.html","s":"draft","x":"Arrange–Act–Assert Definition The shape of a readable test: arrange the preconditions, act once, assert the outcome. Three phases, in that order, visible in the code. python def testticketmovestotriage(boardpage): ticket = boardpage.newticket(\"the report\") arrange ticket.moveto(\"triage\") act expect(boardpage.ticket(\"the report\")).tobein(\"triage\") assert Why it exists A test is documentation that runs. When the three phases are separated, a reader sees what is tested before how; when they interleave, the intent drowns in mechanics. In tafbase the phases come from the model: a journey's setup is its start state, the act is its steps, and the assertions are the state markers and expect-final the emitter writes for every arrival — so generated tests are AAA by construction. In this repo Generated suites under tools/ (lane B-2). Emitted tests carry @concept CONCEPT-testing-v1-001-01-arrange-act-assert."},{"t":"The failure funnel","i":"CONCEPT-testing-v1-002-01-the-failure-funnel","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-testing-v1-002-01-the-failure-funnel.html","s":"draft","x":"The failure funnel Definition One place every failure passes through — capture what the screen was (DOM, screenshot, URL, which step), then fail. The old framework called it teststepFail: every step def, every wait, funneled into the same routine that grabbed the DOM, took the driver-appropriate screenshot, and failed once. Why it exists Scattered failure handling loses evidence — each try invents its own half-broken capture. One funnel means one place to attach capture, reporting, and debugging; a red test leaves evidence, not just a stack. The companion idea — dead-session recovery — came from the same scar: a driver that died mid-wait was re-acquired rather than destroying the whole run. In this project pytestruntestmakereport in the generated conftest.py is the funnel: a red call → URL + DOM captured (guarded against mid-navigation), → ikc3sink.filereport → a tracker fault report. The capture half (screenshot, console slice) is lane J-10/L-12; the recover half is deferred (short tests rarely need it). The collect-and-flush stack (harvest item 28) also lives here: a SoftChecks collector per test — soft expects append {label, error} instead of failing on the spot — and the fixture (or Java @AfterEach) flushes once at test end, failing with all of them. The old framework's verificationErrors + final fail() is the ancestor; the [M x / N y] tally survives as the flush's count. Touch it bash TAFIKC3WS=ws://127.0.0.1:4856/bus-ws TAFBASEURL=http://127.0.0.1:9 .venv/bin/python -m pytest generated/python/ikc3-tracker -q a red test files a report — printed as 'fault report filed: ' Neighbors CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink, CONCEPT-taf-v1-004-01-the-artifacts, CONCEPT-testing-v1-005-01-the-debug-run."},{"t":"The golden file","i":"CONCEPT-testing-v1-003-01-the-golden-file","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-testing-v1-003-01-the-golden-file.html","s":"draft","x":"The golden file Definition An expected artifact — a baseline screenshot — that a compare screen step diffs the actual against, plus the discipline around it: a filename grammar (TCIDrunresstep{Expected,Actual,Diff}.PNG), tolerance and excluded regions per device, and human approval: a person promotes Actual to Expected via a selection file, never by auto-rebaselining. Why it exists A picture asserts what fifty locators cannot. The old framework made screenshots the golden standard, with ALT baselines for legitimate variants, exclusion masks for the iPhone notch, and a scanner that rendered a report grouping diffs. The approval loop is the non-obvious gem: goldens go stale because the SUT legitimately changed — a person must bless the new truth, or the suite silently re-baselines its own drift. In this project The {\"compare-screen\": \" \"} step emits Playwright's native golden compare — expect(page).tohavescreenshot / assertThat(page).hasScreenshot() — decided in ADR-taf-v1-003-01 (use the library's diff engine, keep our artifact layout). Promote with pytest --update-snapshots (Python) / mvn test -DupdateSnapshots (Java) — the approval loop's first form; the selection-file promote of the old grammar is the richer later shape. Touch it bash .venv/bin/python -m pytest generated/python/ikc3-tracker --update-snapshots Neighbors CONCEPT-dsl-v1-008-01-the-image-step, CONCEPT-taf-v1-006-01-the-subtitle-track (the step's caption is the same step's name)."},{"t":"The rerun","i":"CONCEPT-testing-v1-004-01-the-rerun","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-testing-v1-004-01-the-rerun.html","s":"draft","x":"The rerun Definition A manifest of what failed, written at run end; the next run replays only those. Cucumber's rerun plugin wrote target/rerun.txt and a dedicated runner consumed it — red suite, fix, rerun the reds only. Why it exists A suite of any size makes \"run everything again\" the expensive answer to \"did the fix work\". The rerun manifest closes the fix→verify loop in minutes instead of re-running the world. It also records what did fail — the ledger a repair proves against (the full E-5 criterion: fail → report → fix → repaired). In this project Python already has it for free — pytest's --lf/--last-failed reads its own cache. Lane L-12 makes it a framework feature: a generated rerun manifest keyed on CONCEPT-dsl-v1-009-01-the-test-id so Java gets the same replay and a repair walk can rerun exactly the journey that failed. Touch it bash .venv/bin/python -m pytest generated/python/ikc3-tracker --lf -q Neighbors CONCEPT-testing-v1-002-01-the-failure-funnel, CONCEPT-taf-v1-005-01-the-preflight."},{"t":"The debug run","i":"CONCEPT-testing-v1-005-01-the-debug-run","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-testing-v1-005-01-the-debug-run.html","s":"draft","x":"The debug run Definition A mode where a failing test stays: breakpoint() / PWDEBUG lands at the failing step, the browser is not torn down, and the runner lets you poke the live page. The old framework had breakPoint() (an empty method to hang an IDE breakpoint on), testIsUnderTest and keepBrowserOpen — all the affordances for interrogating a failure instead of reading it. Why it exists A failure you can inspect beats a failure you read. Most flaky-bug hunting is not \"does it fail\" but \"what did it look like the moment it did\" — and that question is only answerable if the run doesn't close the window on the way out. In this project Lane L-12 — --debug keeps the browser open and breaks on failure. Today: PWDEBUG=1 opens Playwright's inspector, TAFHEADLESS unset + TAFSLOWMO makes the run watchable, and CONCEPT-dsl-v1-003-01-the-dwell holds a step long enough to see. Touch it bash PWDEBUG=1 .venv/bin/python -m pytest generated/python/ikc3-tracker -q Neighbors CONCEPT-testing-v1-002-01-the-failure-funnel, CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-environment."},{"t":"The living spec","i":"CONCEPT-testing-v1-006-01-the-living-spec","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-testing-v1-006-01-the-living-spec.html","s":"draft","x":"The living spec Definition A specification whose examples execute — the old framework's Concordion fixtures returned display strings from action methods so the rendered spec embedded the actual outcome beside the expected one; its StoryboardExtension drew a per-step screenshot strip inside the spec document. Why it exists A spec that runs cannot drift silently — the document is the test, so an outdated claim is a red test. It's the same instinct as our release-note-first discipline (write the note before the work) taken one step further: the document doesn't just precede the work, it proves it on every run. In this project Deferred — the nearest live practice is the release-note-as-acceptance in PLAN-taf-v1-001-01 and taf.manifest.json as traceability. A living-spec emitter would render a document whose expected/actual cells fill at run time; it belongs with J-10's report work. Touch it Read the manifest: generated/python/ikc3-tracker/taf.manifest.json is the machine-readable ancestor of the idea — declared vs run. Neighbors CONCEPT-testing-v1-001-01-arrange-act-assert, CONCEPT-testing-v1-003-01-the-golden-file."},{"t":"The mail oracle","i":"CONCEPT-testing-v1-007-01-the-mail-oracle","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-testing-v1-007-01-the-mail-oracle.html","s":"draft","x":"The mail oracle Definition Verifying email: snapshot the inbox before the action, then poll only messages that arrived after a marked timestamp, comparing the body against a template whose volatile fields are masked (XPLACEHOLDERX, regex-masked Date:/Deposit ID:). Why it exists Mail is the canonical asynchronous oracle — you can't wait for it, you can only look for what wasn't there before and verify it loosely enough that volatile fields don't flake it. The old framework's tricks were the reusable pearls: the baseline snapshot (only new messages count), the sparse poll schedule ({2,15,25,43,...,179}s over a 182s budget — early checks are cheap, patience grows), and the masked template compare. It even scraped an MFA code back out of the mail HTML and typed it into the page — closing a full verify-by-mail loop. In this project Deferred — needs a mail-capable SUT. The pattern generalizes: any async oracle (a webhook, a queued job) wants snapshot-then-poll-new. Touch it Not implemented — RESEARCH-taf-v1-002-01, item 18. Neighbors CONCEPT-dsl-v1-010-01-the-fixture (arrange via API), CONCEPT-testing-v1-002-01-the-failure-funnel."},{"t":"ADR-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase stands alone","i":"ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone","h":"doc/decisions/ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone.html","s":"accepted","x":"ADR-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase stands alone Decided: 2026-10-14 · George Petrov + Devin Decisions 1. tafbase is its own repository and its own corpus. It does not live inside IKC3. gaks.project.json declares it; the static GAKS bundle of PLAN-ikc-v3-079-01 delivers the corpus tools. This is the first real consumer of the foreign-project path. 2. The DSL is JSON, not EDN. The SUT model must be readable and writable from Java and Python with no Clojure dependency — the same reason gaks.project.json replaced the EDN declaration in IKC3. 3. Generate, don't interpret. Emitters write committed, reviewable test files (testlogin.py, LoginJourneyTest.java). A generic runtime interpreter was considered and refused: generated code diffs in a PR are legible; hidden magic is not. 4. Tracker integrations are declared adapters, not a hard dependency. tafbase core runs with no IKC3. The IKC3 adapter (fault reports, repaired-on-merge) is the first sink and the demo loop; JIRA and GitHub Issues adapters are additions of the same kind. 5. The IKC3 tracker is the first SUT, not a component of tafbase. Dogfooding means tests exercise the real platform and file reports into it — not that tafbase embeds it. 6. MIT license. The goals are exposure and adoption; copyleft and dual-licensing are refused. Consequences Doc ids take the taf subproject prefix (PLAN-taf-v1-NNN-NN); numbering is this repository's own. The IKC3 deployment tracker can file tickets for tafbase work only if this corpus is registered as a vault of the deployment — an open question, deferred to the first lane."},{"t":"ADR-taf-v1-002-01 — the delivered project is a learning surface","i":"ADR-taf-v1-002-01-the-learning-surface","h":"doc/decisions/ADR-taf-v1-002-01-the-learning-surface.html","s":"accepted","x":"ADR-taf-v1-002-01 — the delivered project is a learning surface Decided: 2026-10-14 · George Petrov + Devin Context The precedent is Python-Selenium-Chrome-Demo/v.0.1/docs/concepts.md: a hand-written glossary of 60 concepts — Python, Selenium, pytest, design patterns, testing — each with a definition, an example, and a \"Used in\" pointer. It was good teaching material and it could rot: the pointers were prose, checked by nobody. Decisions 7. The corpus teaches. CONCEPT documents in arch-root/concepts/ cover the implicit and explicit concepts the project embodies, across four domains: the language and runner (Python: fixtures, yield, dataclasses; pytest/JUnit: discovery, markers, parametrize), the tool (Playwright/Selenium: locators, auto-waiting, browser contexts), test design (AAA, page object, isolation, flaky tests), and the DSL itself (model, emitter, manifest, parity, refusal). A concept is a page — a definition, a small example, where it lives in this repo — never a reference manual. 8. Citations are checked, not promised. Generated code carries @concept CONCEPT-taf-v1-NNN-NN- comment lines, the form the GAKS code.annotation-pattern already checks (code-link-unresolved fails when a cited concept has no document). The emitter decides which concepts each generated file demonstrates and writes the annotations; the manifest records the concept ids per test. An annotation with no document is a defect the checker refuses. A document nothing cites is not: a concept may be written before the code that embodies it — the learning path marks such pages as waiting on their lane, and a corpus report may list them, but the check does not fail on them. 9. A guided path orders the glossary. The corpus is flat and wiki-linked, but a learner needs a sequence. One concept document — CONCEPT-taf-v1-001-01-the-learning-path — is the ordered index: what a test is → AAA → locators → fixtures → the model → the emitters. Junior-first; each entry names its prerequisites. 10. PRINCIPLE documents join the corpus. tafbase's gaks.project.json declares the PRINCIPLE kind so the platform's own rules (DSL First, release-note-first, one-form-only) are corpus documents that code and plans can cite, not folklore in a README. Consequences Every emitter lane ships the concept documents its generated code cites; the corpus check is the gate — a lane that emits an uncited concept does not pass. The learning surface is a property of the delivered bundle, not of ikc3: gaks/site/ renders the concepts and their backlinks with no server, on tafbase.com or off a disk."},{"t":"ADR-taf-v1-003-01 — three audiences, and who decides what","i":"ADR-taf-v1-003-01-the-audiences","h":"doc/decisions/ADR-taf-v1-003-01-the-audiences.html","s":"accepted","x":"ADR-taf-v1-003-01 — three audiences, and who decides what Decided: 2026-10-16 · George Petrov + Devin Context The harvest debate surfaced a recurring confusion: a fork between Playwright's native screenshot compare and a hand-rolled filename grammar was put to a stakeholder whose interest is what a run produces, not how the diff engine works. Asking it conflated roles — the same mixing CONCEPT-taf-v1-007-01 names for artifacts must hold for decisions too. Decisions Three audiences, and a decision belongs to whoever's surface it changes: Framework creator — owns internals: how the generator works, which library diffs pixels, the journal's format, schema mechanics. Surface: taf/, tests/, this ADR log, CONTRIBUTING.md. Rulings are made here, by whoever writes the code, and recorded as ADRs — reviewable after the fact, not adjudicated before it. Test creator (model author) — owns the vocabulary: which clauses a model may declare. Surface: suts/, SPEC-taf-v1-001-04 Parts 1–2, the dsl concepts. A decision only reaches this audience when it adds, removes, or renames something the model can say. Results consumer — owns what a run shows: the report, the artifacts, the narration. Surface: generated/ output, manifests, video + .srt, fault reports. A decision reaches this audience when it changes what a run leaves behind. Capability vs selection. The artifact surface is two-layered: the framework creator makes an artifact possible (the diff engine, the recorder, the .srt writer — ADR territory), and the framework user selects and configures it — what is collected, where it lands, which sink reports go to. Mechanism vs policy: capability is built once and recorded in an ADR; selection is declared, per run via the TAF environment or per suite via the model — whichever owns the variation. The rule: put no internal fork to a creator or consumer. Put no vocabulary or visibility question to a creator without naming the surface it changes. An ADR is the record of a creator decision; a spec clause is the record of a vocabulary one; a release note is the record of a visible one. Consequences The evidence-stack fork is settled by its owner, not escalated: Playwright's native compare (tohavescreenshot/hasScreenshot) under tafbase's own artifact layout — we do not rebuild a diff engine to keep a filename. A per-test taf.journal.jsonl is the shared timestamped substrate — .srt, rerun manifests, and fault reports read it; it is designed once. The journey name is the test id; test-id is a naming rule, not a new field. targets: becomes a declared model section — a vocabulary change, so it is a spec clause, not an emitter detail."},{"t":"F-taf-v1-002-02 — the framework model: acceptance","i":"F-taf-v1-002-02-framework-model-acceptance","h":"doc/features/F-taf-v1-002-02-framework-model-acceptance.html","s":"draft","x":"F-taf-v1-002-02 — the framework model: acceptance Summary The framework model must be a real DSL artifact, consumed by every tool that needs its semantics — emitters, validator, dry-run — with conformance gates that make drift loud. Each criterion below is testable: a command, a test name, or a diff. Acceptance criteria Each item is testable: a command, a test name, or a diff. The model [ ] AC-1 taf/dsl/framework-spec.dsl parses via FrameworkSpec.g4 with zero syntax errors — proven by any consumer load [x] AC-2 Every form and rung carries a description — the model reads as documentation [x] AC-3 The grammar file is the artifact; the parser is generated, never hand-edited (Python3 target in taf/antlr/, Java target via antlr4-maven-plugin) The consumers [x] AC-4 codegen/java derives from the spec — regenerated suite is byte-identical to the checked-in generated/java/ikc3-tracker [x] AC-5 codegen/python derives the same way — byte-identical generated/python/ikc3-tracker [x] AC-6 model.py derives STEPFORMS/LOCATORRUNGS and ops.locatorrung derives preference order from the spec [ ] AC-7 dryrun.py derives its env table from contract.env — add a var in the spec, see it in taf env without code changes [ ] AC-8 model.py refusal names import from the contract — refusal sites take names from spec, not literals Governance (§3.5) [x] AC-9 testspeccoverage — every form variant and rung variant emits real output in both languages, or the build fails [x] AC-10 testspeccontract — every refusal name used in code is declared in the spec [ ] AC-11 Adding a form to the spec without implementations fails a test (coverage gate catches unspellable coordinates) The frameworks [x] AC-12 pytest tests/ — 91+ pass, 4 live skips [x] AC-13 mvn test-compile in a generated Java suite — BUILD SUCCESS [ ] AC-14 mvn test runs the Java suite live against the SUT (auth bootstrap + storage state, parity with the Python run) [ ] AC-15 codegen-java emits at least Elements.java from the spec — the Java-target generator produces a real artifact [ ] AC-16 A generated suite's pom regenerates itself when the model or spec changes (generate-sources wired) The discipline [x] AC-17 Feature lanes live on feature/ branches, not master [ ] AC-18 env:/env-pool: — no secret value appears in any generated file, draft, caption, or journal entry (grep-able) [ ] AC-19 .interp/.tokens ANTLR byproducts gitignored or removed [ ] AC-20 SPEC-002-02 updated: contract section documented in the metamodel table"},{"t":"PLAN-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase: a DSL First test automation framework","i":"PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase","h":"doc/plans/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase.html","s":"draft","x":"PLAN-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase: a DSL First test automation framework Status: in progress tafbase declares a system under test (SUT) once, as data, and generates real test suites from the declaration. Python+Playwright is the first emitter; Java is the second, and the second exists to make the parity claim true. Generated suites report into pluggable tracker adapters; the first adapter is IKC3's fault reports, so a red test files a bug into the very platform the tests exercise. Release note (draft) New: a SUT declared once in JSON runs as a Python+Playwright suite; the same declaration refuses the same broken model the same way. Accepted by: T-taf-001 (parity of refusal, env -i, python3 only) New: a failed generated test files a fault report into a configured tracker through a declared adapter; the IKC3 adapter is the first. Accepted by: T-taf-002 Internal: the Java+Playwright emitter proves the same declaration generates equivalent suites in two languages. Accepted by: T-taf-003 (Python and Java emitters agree on the suite) What this does not do v1 generates static journeys only. Model-based generation — walks of the SUT graph, coverage-directed paths, journal replay — is declared in ADR-taf-v1-001-01 and arrives by lanes, not all at once. Lanes ▶ Lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/A-1 — the SUT model is JSON The contract: SPEC-taf-v1-001-04-tafbase. A SUT declares states, transitions and journeys in one JSON document. The loader validates shape, refusals name the broken clause, and no ambiguous form is guessed — one form only. Release note (draft) New: sut.model.json declares pages, actions and journeys; a broken model names its fault. Accepted by: T-taf-001 ▶ Lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/B-2 — the Python+Playwright emitter From a valid model, taf emit --lang python writes a runnable Playwright+pytest suite: page objects, journey tests, and a manifest mapping each test back to the model element that produced it. Release note (draft) New: one declaration produces a committed, reviewable pytest suite. Accepted by: T-taf-004 ▶ Lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/C-3 — tracker adapters and fault reports Progress (2026-10-15). The IKC3 sink exists and works live: taf/sinkikc3.py dispatches :support/file-report over /bus-ws (EDN envelope, ikc3-bus subprotocol, no token needed where authz is off). Emitted verbatim into each Python suite as ikc3sink.py; the generated pytestruntestmakereport hook files on any red test when TAFIKC3WS is set, carrying page URL + DOM. Proven against a probe bus: a suite pointed at a dead SUT filed one report per red test (tests/testlive.py, opt-in). Still open: the Java-side hook, screenshot/attachment upload, auth-token support, a second (JIRA-shaped) adapter. A test outcome flows to a declared sink. The IKC3 adapter files a fault report and links it to the test's model element; adapters are a declared kind so JIRA or GitHub Issues are additions, not rewrites. Release note (draft) New: a red test files a fault report into IKC3, named for i"},{"t":"The interview surface","i":"RESEARCH-taf-v1-001-01-the-interview-surface","h":"doc/research/RESEARCH-taf-v1-001-01-the-interview-surface.html","s":"draft","x":"The interview surface What this is A coverage map: the corpus's concepts against the questions the market actually asks. Source: a widely-shared \"Top 25 Playwright interview questions\" list (LinkedIn, 2026 SDET interview prep). The corpus's stated job is to serve as a learning surface and interview prep — this document measures that promise against an external yardstick and names the gaps as work. The map interview asks covered by state --- --- --- --- 1 Playwright vs Selenium CONCEPT-playwright-v1-001-01-browser-context-page-hierarchy, CONCEPT-selenium-v1-001-01-webdriver-session-lifecycle covered 2 Project setup (TypeScript assumed) CONCEPT-ts-v1-006-01-typed-test-fixture-extension, CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-001-01-test-extend-fixtures covered 3 Browser / BrowserContext / Page CONCEPT-playwright-v1-001-01-browser-context-page-hierarchy covered 4 Auto-waiting, actionability CONCEPT-playwright-v1-003-01-auto-waiting-and-actionability covered 5 locator() vs page.$() CONCEPT-playwright-v1-002-01-semantic-locators covered 6 Dynamic elements, flaky tests CONCEPT-qa-v1-009-01-deterministic-replay-and-flakiness covered 7 getByRole vs getByText vs css/xpath CONCEPT-qa-v1-002-01-the-locator-ladder, CONCEPT-playwright-v1-002-01-semantic-locators covered 8 Dropdowns, checkboxes, alerts, iframes CONCEPT-playwright-v1-009-01-frame-and-shadow-dom-piercing covered 9 File upload/download — gap 10 Screenshots/videos on failure CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink, CONCEPT-playwright-v1-006-01-trace-viewer-and-artifacts covered 11 API testing via request context CONCEPT-playwright-v1-005-01-api-request-context + the model's api/save/expect steps covered 12 Authentication reuse CONCEPT-dsl-v1-016-01-the-auth-clause covered 13 storageState CONCEPT-playwright-v1-008-01-storage-state-reuse covered 14 Parallel execution, workers CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-005-01-projects-matrix-and-sharding, CONCEPT-pytest-v1-005-01-markers-and-xdist-parallelism covered 15 Sharding CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-005-01-projects-matrix-and-sharding covered 16 Retries, flaky-test investigation CONCEPT-qa-v1-009-01-deterministic-replay-and-flakiness covered 17 beforeEach/afterEach CONCEPT-java-v1-001-01-junit-lifecycle-annotations, CONCEPT-pytest-v1-001-01-dependency-injected-fixtures, CONCEPT-pwtest-v1-001-01-test-extend-fixtures covered (in four idioms) 18 Framework architecture: POM + fixtures CONCEPT-qa-v1-005-01-page-object-model, CONCEPT-pom-v1-001-01-the-page-object + the whole generated layout covered — our strongest answer 19 HTML reports the manifest + sink part — ours is better (traceable), say so 20 CI/CD integration — gap 21 Cross-browser (chromium/firefox/webkit) — gap — model has no browser clause yet 22 API mocking, network interception — gap 23 Debugging: trace viewer, headed, inspector — gap — not asked but we answer it seeded walks, parity contracts, checked citations, refusals our differentiators What the map says Seven covered outright, 4 partial, 14 gaps. The gaps cluster into themes, each of w"},{"t":"The harvest from qa-tafbase","i":"RESEARCH-taf-v1-002-01-the-harvest-from-qa-tafbase","h":"doc/research/RESEARCH-taf-v1-002-01-the-harvest-from-qa-tafbase.html","s":"draft","x":"The harvest from qa-tafbase What this is A previous-generation framework — a Java/Selenium/Cucumber suite (testsbase engine + per-product modules, JUnit4, Appium) — was mapped end-to-end for reusable design. This document keeps the result and the decisions it produced. The raw harvest — 28 ideas with evidence paths, translations and costs — is RESEARCH-taf-v1-003-01-the-harvest-in-detail. The rules the harvest confirmed Declared beats interpreted. What the old runtime computed — device capability strings, tag→env mapping, layered properties — tafbase emits at codegen. The generator absorbs what a factory once computed. Dwell is for observation, not synchronization. visibilitySleep (a global + per-call-site Thread.sleep around every action) answered two needs at once: letting JS settle and letting a human watch. Auto-waiting markers own the first; a declared dwell owns the second. They must not fuse — sleeps-for-correctness is the disease this framework is built to avoid. Adopted (lanes in PLAN-taf-v1-001-01) idea becomes lane --- --- --- visibilitySleep pacing dwell — per-step/ms or a suite profile, emitted as a literal H-8 named wait vocabulary + soft/hard wait/check step forms, wait any (X Y) H-8 version × language locator matrix versioned elements, generated lookup tables I-9 screenshot grammar + approval loop compare screen step, TCIDrunresstep{E,A,D}, taf promote J-10 capability token (Pixel9ProXLchrome) device:/env: model header emitting the driver block K-11 column-model schema (its TSV interpreter) stays as proof the grammar works — the tafbase DSL is its compile-time heir (settled) rerun.txt failed-only replay taf run --last-failed manifest L-12 debug affordances --debug: keep browser open, break on failure L-12 circuit breaker / preflight requires: model clause → emitted preflight L-12 self-narrating actions required human label on elements/steps → emitted logs H-8 the never-built .srt subtitles narrated steps + timestamps → sidecar .srt beside run video H-8 (with J-10 artifacts) The .srt item deserves a note: all five teardown paths of the old framework captured a video-stop timestamp and called a createSubTitlesFile that was only ever committed commented out — the intent was a subtitle track mapping each narrated action line (Clicking 'X' on 'Y') onto video timecodes. The hard half — timestamped narration — already existed. In generated code both halves are cheap: emitted steps know their text and their clock, so a teardown writer emits results/ / .srt beside the video. A run becomes replayable and readable. Deliberately left behind PPTX reports, Robot/AWT screen capture, hotkey-driven video, Swing overlay strips, VM-awake mouse jiggles, X:-drive archiving, hardcoded notification tokens. Playwright viewport capture + HTML reports + committed artifacts replace the whole cluster."},{"t":"The harvest in detail — evidence appendix","i":"RESEARCH-taf-v1-003-01-the-harvest-in-detail","h":"doc/research/RESEARCH-taf-v1-003-01-the-harvest-in-detail.html","s":"draft","x":"The harvest in detail — evidence appendix The full evidence behind RESEARCH-taf-v1-002-01: every idea recovered from the previous-generation framework (a Maven multi-module Java/Selenium/Cucumber/Appium suite — testsbase engine plus per-product modules), with the file that proves it, the translation to generated code, and where it landed in this corpus. Architecture context: JUnit4 + Cucumber 7.13 + Selenium 4.35 + Appium. A prior generation exists underneath (Concordion + a TSV interpreter — item 14). → marks the tafbase landing: a CONCEPT page, a lane of PLAN-taf-v1-001-01, or done. Per-step pacing / dwell 1. visibilitySleep / visiSleep — the global dwell Evidence: testsbase/.../utils/TimeUtils.java:478-498; config visibility.sleep.time=222 (config.properties:32); mutable static Configuration.VISIBILITYSLEEP (utils/Configuration.java:60,192). Called before and after nearly every action (com/fxcm/qa/TestBase.java:809-826). What it is: a Thread.sleep wrapper with two units and a zero-arg overload using the global. Two purposes: let JS/animations settle, and make live-watching pleasant (paired with the Overlay). Overridden per call-site (visiSleep(345) at TestBase.java:1082), per run (config), at runtime (non-final static; even inside JS — setTimeout(()= arguments[0].click(), VISIBILITYSLEEP) at TestBase.java:756,789). Translation: a DSL-level annotation, not a sleep — resolved at codegen into per-step literals. Cost: small. → done — {\"dwell\": }; CONCEPT-dsl-v1-003-01-the-dwell. Browser/driver factory 2. \"Resolution\" — one token drives local emulation AND BrowserStack Evidence: CucumberTestBase.initTest dispatches on \"desktop, mobile, tablet\".contains(resolution) (:139-143); local mobile/tablet via CDP Emulation.setDeviceMetricsOverride (:550-572); remote via token parsing — w10chrome, iPhone16Prosafari, GalaxyS25Ultrasamsung → os/osVersion/ deviceName/browserName switch (:319-430). Env creds override browserstack.yml (:289-290); buildIdentifier=TESTRUNID, sessionName=testNameexample (:306-307). Translation: already a mini-DSL — device: Pixel9ProXLchrome emits the exact capabilities object; zero runtime parsing, compile-time vocabulary. Cost: medium. → lane K-11; CONCEPT-dsl-v1-012-01-the-target. 3. Tag → environment → URL AND locator-version selection Evidence: isUatTest maps @qaenv/@uatenv/@browserstack to suiteName + testUrl + DEFAULTVERSION (CucumberTestBase.java:128-134,218-239; DEFAULTVERSIONQA/UAT in Configuration.java:28-29,139-148). Translation: env: bakes baseUrl + locator version into the emitted suite. Cost: small. → lane K-11; same concept as item 2. Waits 4. Named wait vocabulary, soft and hard Evidence: waitUntilVisibilityOfElement, waitLongVisibilityOfElement, waitInvisibilityOfElement, waitUntilClickable, waitUntilVisibilityOfEitherElements, waitUntilVisibilityOfText (TestBase.java:962-1146). Soft variant logs + saves DOM + screenshot, continues (waitUntilVisibilityOfElementParametrized, :985-1011); hard variant fails through test"},{"t":"Evaluation of project clarity, missing information, and implementation deficiencies","i":"RESEARCH-taf-v1-004-01-evaluation-and-implementation-deficiencies","h":"doc/research/RESEARCH-taf-v1-004-01-evaluation-and-implementation-deficiencies.html","s":"draft","x":"Evaluation of project clarity, missing information, and implementation deficiencies Why this exists A comprehensive architectural and technical evaluation of tafbase as of November 2026 (branch fix/pr1-review). The project's contract demands DSL-First test generation, byte-for-byte manifest parity between Python and Java suites, honest refusals, and deep learning surfaces. This document records an audit of whether the repository is self-explanatory to newcomers, what information is missing, and what defects exist in the current implementation phase. Related: PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase SPEC-taf-v1-001-04-tafbase SPEC-taf-v1-002-02-the-framework-spec-dsl F-taf-v1-002-02-framework-model-acceptance How it was measured 1. Corpus & layout inspection: Checked arch-root/ documents against GAKS rules (gaks/tools/py/gakscheck.py), reviewed READMEs, and mapped the directory structure. 2. Execution gates: Executed unit tests (pytest), Java suite compilation (mvn test-compile), manifest diff checks, and CLI commands (check, dry-run, env, walk). 3. Bytecode and API contract verification: Inspected Microsoft Playwright Java 1.49.0 bytecode via javap to verify interface methods referenced by the Java code emitter. 4. Boundary and negative fuzzing: Evaluated taf.model.validate with edge-case model definitions (inline steps, unchained final states, reserved keyword identifiers). The summary Self-explanatoriness: High architectural rigor in arch-root/, but obscured by \"Dual DSL\" ambiguity (SUT JSON model vs. Framework Spec ANTLR DSL), layout discrepancies, and an undocumented Java execution story. Missing information: Lack of a formal JSON Schema for IDE validation, sparse documentation on complex step arguments (api, save, expect), missing workflow guidance for taf record, and no generic result-sink SPI. Problems & deficiencies: 1. A broken import in tests/testlive.py (from taf import emitjava). 2. A flawed contract test in tests/testspeccontract.py that concealed three missing refusals in framework-spec.dsl. 3. Uncompilable Java code generated for compare-screen (no hasScreenshot() in Playwright Java) and api.save (no resp.json() in Playwright Java and no JSON parser dependency). 4. Parity gaps in Java: TAFRERUN is printed but never implemented; fault reporting via ikc3sink is absent in Java. 5. Unfulfilled acceptance criteria (AC-7, AC-8) from F-taf-v1-002-02, leaving dryrun.py hardcoded and omitting TAFARRIVEMS. 6. Model validation gaps in taf.model.validate: unvalidated final states, false-positive reachability rejections for inline navigation, and unvalidated identifier names producing syntax errors in emitted code. In detail: self-explanatory evaluation Strengths Exemplary Corpus Rigor: The documentation in arch-root/ sets a high bar. Plans define draft release notes as acceptance criteria, ADRs provide clear context for architectural boundaries, and over 35 concept documents explain every primitive following a strict 5-question depth bar. Cohesive CLI Lifec"},{"t":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-01 — the SUT model","i":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-01-the-sut-model","h":"doc/specs/SPEC-taf-v1-001-01-the-sut-model.html","s":"superseded","x":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-01 — the SUT model Superseded by SPEC-taf-v1-001-02-the-sut-model — same contract, rewritten normative with numbered clauses; kept as history. Related: PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone The design input of lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/A-1. This document is the contract: a loader and an emitter are correct together when each refusal and each generated behavior below holds. Purpose A system under test (SUT) is declared once, as one JSON document. The declaration is the model. From it, emitters write runnable suites (Python+Playwright, later Java+Playwright) that follow Playwright's own rules: semantic locators, auto-waiting assertions, one journey per test. The same document is the graph the MBT lanes walk later — states and transitions are first-class from v1, so no model is written twice. The document One file per SUT, sut.model.json by convention. Top level: json { \"sut\": {\"name\": \"ikc3-tracker\", \"base-url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:4855\"}, \"states\": {}, \"elements\": {}, \"actions\": {}, \"journeys\": {}, \"endpoints\": {} } sut name (string, required) names the SUT in refusals and the manifest. base-url (string, required) is where journeys begin. No environment indirection in v1: a different deployment is a different file, or a later lane adds env. states A state is a place a journey can be — a page, a dialog, a verified condition. A state is recognized, not assumed: json \"states\": { \"board\": { \"route\": \"/index.html?island=board\", \"marker\": {\"role\": \"heading\", \"name\": \"Board\"} } } route (optional) is the path under base-url that reaches the state directly. marker (required) is a locator that a generated test asserts visible after every arrival — a state is entered only when the SUT proves it. That one rule is what makes generated tests self-verifying instead of hopeful. elements Named locators, declared once, reused everywhere: json \"elements\": { \"new-ticket-button\": {\"role\": \"button\", \"name\": \"New ticket\"}, \"title-field\": {\"label\": \"Title\"}, \"report-card\": {\"testid\": \"report-card\"}, \"legacy-panel\": {\"css\": \".panel--legacy\"} } THE LOCATOR LADDER is the \"proper use\" clause, encoded, not documented: 1. role + name — the accessibility query, preferred 2. label / text / placeholder — user-visible queries 3. testid — the deliberate escape (data-testid) 4. css / xpath — the last resort, allowed and flagged fragile in the manifest A locator with no usable form is refused :bad-locator. The ladder is the emitter's only ordering; a model does not say which rung a locator sits on, the shape does. actions An action is a step the SUT offers — the transitions of the graph: json \"actions\": { \"move-to-triage\": { \"from\": \"backlog\", \"to\": \"triage\", \"do\": [ {\"click\": \"new-ticket-button\"}, {\"fill\": {\"element\": \"title-field\", \"value\": \"the report\"}}, {\"click\": \"save-button\"}, {\"api\": \"move-ticket\", \"args\": {\"category\": \"triage\"}} ] } } from and to name states and are optional per action, required for the graph to be a graph: an acti"},{"t":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-02 — the SUT model","i":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-02-the-sut-model","h":"doc/specs/SPEC-taf-v1-001-02-the-sut-model.html","s":"superseded","x":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-02 — the SUT model Superseded by SPEC-taf-v1-001-03-the-sut-language — the same contract reframed as a language specification: the JSON is the concrete syntax of the DSL, not the subject. Kept as history. Purpose tafbase is a test automation framework: a web application is described once, as one JSON document, and that document is compiled into runnable browser and API test suites — Playwright for Python and Java today. This document specifies that JSON document. It is the contract between a model author and the emitters: the implementation is correct when all clauses in this document hold. Read this document on its own — it uses no other document of this project. Keywords must, must not, should, and may are normative, in the RFC 2119 sense. Clauses carry identifiers (SM-n) so a test, a review, or a refusal can name what it verifies. Related: PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone ADR-taf-v1-002-01-the-learning-surface Terms term meaning --- --- SUT the system under test — the application the generated suite exercises model one JSON document declaring a SUT's states, elements, actions, journeys, endpoints state a recognizable place in the SUT — a page, a dialog — proven by its marker marker a locator a generated test asserts visible on arrival at a state locator one form of finding an element — role+name, label, text, placeholder, testid, css, xpath action a named step the SUT offers; with from/to it is an edge of the graph journey a named path through the graph; emitted as one test refusal a named rejection {clause, reason, message}; a broken model emits nothing emitter a code generator: a model in, a runnable suite out manifest taf.manifest.json — emitted beside the tests, mapping each test to its model elements SM-1 · One file, JSON A SUT is declared in one file, .sut.model.json by convention. It must be a JSON object; a document that is not is refused :not-json. A name declared twice in one clause is refused :duplicate-name — JSON's silent last-write-wins is not accepted. Top level: json { \"sut\": {\"name\": \"ikc3-tracker\", \"base-url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:4856\"}, \"states\": {}, \"elements\": {}, \"actions\": {}, \"journeys\": {}, \"endpoints\": {} } sut.name and sut.base-url are required (:missing-field). A model with no states and no journeys is refused :empty-model. elements, actions, and endpoints may be absent or empty. SM-2 · States are recognized, not assumed json \"states\": { \"board\": {\"route\": \"/index.html?island=board\", \"marker\": {\"role\": \"heading\", \"name\": \"Board\"}} } A state must declare marker, a locator (SM-3); a generated test must assert it visible on every arrival — the SUT proves the state, the test does not assume it (:missing-field without one). route is optional; when present it is the path under base-url that reaches the state directly. A state no journey can reach is refused :unreachable — a declared state with no path to it is a dead declaration. SM-3 · The locator ladder A locator must take "},{"t":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-03 — the SUT language","i":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-03-the-sut-language","h":"doc/specs/SPEC-taf-v1-001-03-the-sut-language.html","s":"superseded","x":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-03 — the SUT language Superseded by SPEC-taf-v1-001-04-tafbase — the subject is the framework, not the language; the DSL is its interface, usable without knowing it is one. Kept as history. Purpose tafbase is a DSL First test automation framework. Its input is a small language for declaring a system under test (SUT): the states a user can reach, the actions that move between them, and the journeys a suite must prove. From one declaration, emitters compile runnable browser and API test suites — Playwright for Python and Java today. This document specifies that language. Part 1 gives the language's constructs and their meaning — the abstract syntax. Part 2 gives the JSON form a model author writes — the concrete syntax. Part 3 gives the contract every implementation must honor: loading, refusals, emission, parity. This document stands alone. Keywords must, must not, should, and may are normative in the RFC 2119 sense. Clauses carry identifiers (SL-n) so a test, a review, or a refusal names what it verifies. Related: PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone ADR-taf-v1-002-01-the-learning-surface Terms term meaning --- --- DSL domain-specific language — here, the language this document defines SUT the system under test — the application a generated suite exercises model a program in the SUT language: one declaration of a SUT state a recognizable place in the SUT, proven by its marker marker a locator a generated test asserts visible on arrival locator one way to find an element — role+name, label, text, placeholder, testid, css, xpath action a named step the SUT offers; with from/to it is an edge journey a named path through the graph; compiled to one test refusal a named rejection {clause, reason, message}; a rejected model emits nothing emitter a compiler backend: a model in, a runnable suite out manifest taf.manifest.json — maps each emitted test to its model elements Part 1 — The constructs SL-1 · States are recognized, not assumed A state is where a journey can be. It declares a marker — the proof — and may declare a route, the address that reaches it directly. A test arrives at a state only when the SUT shows the marker. This is the language's core semantics: a state is proven, never presumed. SL-2 · Locators form a ladder A locator takes exactly one form, and the forms are ordered by what a user perceives: rung form standing --- --- --- 1 role + name how a user names a control — preferred 2 label / text / placeholder what a user reads 3 testid a deliberate testing hook 4 css / xpath structure, fragile — flagged in the manifest The ladder is a language rule, not a style guide: the shape of a locator chooses its rung, and rung 4 carries a fragile mark into the manifest. SL-3 · Actions are edges An action is a step the SUT offers. An action that declares from and to is an edge of the SUT's graph — the same graph the MBT lanes walk (taf walk), so a model is written once and serves journeys, walks, and cover"},{"t":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-04 — tafbase","i":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-04-tafbase","h":"doc/specs/SPEC-taf-v1-001-04-tafbase.html","s":"draft","x":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-04 — tafbase Purpose tafbase is a test automation framework. You describe a system under test (SUT) once — its screens, its controls, the journeys that matter — and tafbase generates runnable browser and API test suites: pytest + Playwright for Python, JUnit 5 + Playwright for Java. The description is a small DSL — but using tafbase needs no knowledge of languages or code generation. You fill in a JSON form; the framework turns it into tests you can read, run, and review. Everything in this document is about what you declare and what you get. This document stands alone. Keywords must, must not, should, and may are normative in the RFC 2119 sense. Clauses carry identifiers (TAF-n) so a test, a review, or a refusal names what it verifies. Related: PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone ADR-taf-v1-002-01-the-learning-surface Terms term meaning --- --- SUT the system under test — the application a generated suite exercises model your declaration of a SUT, written in tafbase's DSL state a recognizable place in the SUT, proven by its marker marker a locator a generated test asserts visible on arrival locator one way to find an element — role+name, label, text, placeholder, testid, css, xpath action a named step the SUT offers; with from/to it is an edge journey a named path through the SUT; compiled to one test refusal a named rejection {clause, reason, message}; a rejected model emits nothing emitter a generator: a model in, a runnable suite out manifest taf.manifest.json — maps each generated test to your model's elements walk a journey the tool derives by traversing the graph, replayable by seed Part 1 — What you declare TAF-1 · States are recognized, not assumed A state is where a journey can be — a page, a dialog, a verified condition. It declares a marker: the visible proof. A test arrives at a state only when the SUT shows that proof. A state may also declare a route, the address that reaches it directly. TAF-2 · Locators form a ladder A locator takes exactly one form. The forms are ordered by what a user perceives, and the order is a rule of the framework, not a style guide: rung form standing --- --- --- 1 role + name how a user names a control — preferred 2 label / text / placeholder what a user reads 3 testid a deliberate testing hook 4 css / xpath structure, fragile — flagged in the manifest The shape of a locator chooses its rung; you never declare one. Named locators are declared once in elements and used by name everywhere. TAF-3 · Actions are edges An action is a step the SUT offers. An action with from and to is an edge of the SUT's graph — which is also what taf walk traverses, so a model serves journeys, walks, and coverage without being written twice. An action's do list composes steps: act (click, fill, check, select), move (navigate), observe (wait-for, wait-any, expect — soft: true collects, flushed once at test end), call the API (api, save), pace (dwell — a pause for a watching human, "},{"t":"The framework model (PRD)","i":"SPEC-taf-v1-002-01-the-framework-model","h":"doc/specs/SPEC-taf-v1-002-01-the-framework-model.html","s":"draft","x":"The framework model (PRD) One line A test automation framework's semantics are declared once — as a model — and working framework implementations are generated from it in each target language. Python+Playwright and Java+Playwright are the first two outputs, not the design center. The object The product is the model, not a framework: a language-neutral declaration of what a test automation framework is — its step vocabulary, locator ladder, journey semantics, auth model, environment contract, evidence format, reporting protocol — plus a generator that spells those declarations as compilable, reviewable framework code per target language. Anything a practitioner would otherwise hand-code three times is a model clause; anything that differs per language is only a spelling. The generator is handwritten and stays that way — it is not the product; the model is. Layers dsl/ the framework model (the artifact) forms.dsl.json step forms: arg shapes, captions, per-language spellings locators.dsl.json ladder rungs + per-language spellings contract.dsl.json env contract, refusal table, evidence/rerun/report formats taf/ ops.py canonical IR — what a journey IS, language-free emitpython.py model → Python+Playwright/pytest framework emitjava.py model → Java+Playwright/JUnit5 framework A generated framework then does what it was modeled to do: load SUT models (its own input DSL), refuse ambiguity, emit or run journeys, produce evidence. The SUT model and the framework model are different objects — the second generates the programs that read the first. Requirements 1. Grammar as data. Step forms, locator rungs, their arg shapes and their per-language spellings live in declared tables. Adding a step form is one row, not three code changes. Validation and all emitters read the same table — divergence is impossible by construction, not just tested for. 2. Named refusals. Every invalid clause refuses with a named reason the spec lists. Never guess, never die on the first. 3. Language parity. Generated frameworks carry a canonical manifest; targets must produce identical semantics. Parity is proven, not promised. 4. Environment contract. Effective values resolve flag env model; needs (env:, env-pool:) derive from declared use and gate before a browser opens. Secrets are named, never stored. 5. Session semantics. auth declares sign-in-once: run it in a bootstrap context, keep the session as storage state, every test's fresh context inherits it. Isolation kept, one credential per run. 6. Evidence. Every test leaves a journal, timed .srt narration paired with .webm video, failure screenshot + DOM, a rerun manifest, and a printed artifact dir. Captions name steps, never values. 7. Reporting. Fault reports file on failure — through the SUT's own surface or an adapter — and never twice: a fingerprint ledger dedups an unchanged error across runs. 8. Authoring aids. record drafts model clauses from live driving (secrets redacted by locator); walk derives journeys by seeded gra"},{"t":"The framework-spec DSL","i":"SPEC-taf-v1-002-02-the-framework-spec-dsl","h":"doc/specs/SPEC-taf-v1-002-02-the-framework-spec-dsl.html","s":"draft","x":"The framework-spec DSL A project-specific, grammar-hosted DSL (DSL-First §2.4 — ANTLR — the family member Kernel/Behavior/Verification/Catalog cannot express: spellings as data). The model taf/dsl/framework-spec.dsl declares what the framework is; emitters derive framework code from it. Metamodel Clause Shape Meaning --- --- --- dsl {name, version, binding} language identity + version The grammar is dsl/FrameworkSpec.g4; the parser is ANTLR-generated into taf/antlr/ (regenerate on grammar change — see taf/dslparse.py docstring); dslparse walks the tree to the model dict. vocabulary {form: entry} step forms of the SUT language locators {rung: entry} locator-ladder rungs Form entry: arg (shape doc), caption (.srt template), spell ({python, java} templates or a line list), optional variants (keyed by when = arg keys present) or wrap (pre/post lines for maybe). Rung entry: key (locator arg), spell or variants. Spelling templates: ${key} placeholders; emitters substitute escaped values and computed fragments (${stateclass}, ${valueexpr}, ${locchain}, ${savekey}…). Values pass through py/j name transforms and per-language escaping. Well-formedness Every form/rung spells both python and java (parity is the point) Every when list matches arg keys exactly; variants are exhaustive ${value} never appears in a caption — captions name, never leak Consumers emit byte-identical output to the last handwritten emitter (the conformance test until all consumers derive) Semantics emitjava → Elements/assertThat/page. Java+Playwright lines emitpython → elements./expect/page. Python+Playwright lines model.py → checkstep arg-shape validation (pending M1) Adding a form or rung = one model row, all consumers derive Versioning dsl.version bumps on clause shape changes; additive forms/rungs are backward-compatible by construction."}];
